      <data key="d7">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="United States" target="National Comprehensive Cancer Network">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network is an alliance of top cancer centers located across the United States.</data>
      <data key="d6">institutional base, geographical location</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="United States" target="Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d4">15.0</data>
      <data key="d5">In the United States, significant racial disparities exist in prostate cancer incidence, age of onset, aggressiveness, and mortality rates.&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate cancer is documented as the second most common cancer diagnosed in males within the United States.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer prevalence, demographic health statistics&lt;SEP&gt;epidemiology, racial disparities</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="United States" target="Black Males">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">In the United States, Black males experience higher incidence and mortality rates from prostate cancer compared to other groups.</data>
      <data key="d6">demographic disparity, geographic health statistics</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="United States" target="Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The use of Pap smear screening programs in the United States has led to significantly reduced rates of cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease incidence, preventative screening</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="United States" target="Pap Smear">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The implementation and utilization of Pap smears have resulted in significantly lower rates of cervical cancer in the United States.</data>
      <data key="d6">public health impact, geographic health outcome</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="United States" target="HPV Vaccine">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A nine-strain HPV vaccine is authorized and accessible in the United States for people up to age 45.</data>
      <data key="d6">healthcare availability, vaccination access</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="United States" target="BCG Shortage">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The shortage of BCG has persisted for several years across the United States and globally.</data>
      <data key="d6">geographic impact, resource deficit</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="United States" target="U.S. FDA">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The FDA is a federal regulatory administration in the United States overseeing drug approval.</data>
      <data key="d6">regulatory authority, federal agency</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Basal Cell Skin Cancer" target="Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Basal cell skin cancer is the most common type of skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, cancer type</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Basal Cell Skin Cancer" target="NCCN">
      <data key="d4">15.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN provides educational materials and guidelines for patients diagnosed with basal cell skin cancer and other cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN provides patient guidelines to assist individuals diagnosed with basal cell skin cancer in understanding testing and treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical guidance, patient education&lt;SEP&gt;medical guidelines, cancer education</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Basal Cell Skin Cancer" target="Basal Cells">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Basal cell skin cancer originates when basal cells become abnormal and proliferate uncontrollably.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease origin, oncogenesis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Basal Cell Skin Cancer" target="UV Radiation">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Exposure to UV radiation damages the DNA of basal cells, serving as the main cause and risk factor for basal cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">carcinogenesis, etiology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Basal Cell Skin Cancer" target="Surgery">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Surgery is the primary and most common therapeutic intervention used to cure basal cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">oncological treatment, surgical intervention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Basal Cell Skin Cancer" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy can serve as a treatment modality for basal cell carcinoma or act as a predisposing risk factor when administered previously.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical therapy, risk factor</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Basal Cell Skin Cancer" target="Systemic Therapy">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Systemic therapy is an available treatment option utilized in specific cases of basal cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">pharmacotherapy, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Basal Cell Skin Cancer" target="Full Skin Exam">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Full skin exams are scheduled follow-up surveillance events conducted to check for cancer recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical monitoring, follow-up care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Basal Cell Skin Cancer" target="Immune Suppression">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Immune suppression weakens the body's defenses, significantly increasing an individual's risk for developing basal cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">risk factor, immunosuppression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Basal Cell Skin Cancer" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A biopsy is an essential diagnostic test used to detect and confirm basal cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer diagnosis, pathology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Basal Cell Skin Cancer" target="Imaging Tests">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Imaging studies are used in specific cases to assess and plan treatment for basal cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical imaging, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Skin Cancer" target="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Squamous cell skin cancer is a distinct classification of cancer originating in the epidermis alongside basal cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, cancer type</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Skin Cancer" target="Melanoma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Melanoma is a specific type of skin cancer referenced alongside basal cell and squamous cell cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, cancer type</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Skin Cancer" target="Health Care Provider">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Health care providers conduct regular screenings that may assess for skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">routine screening, preventive care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Skin Cancer" target="Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Skin cancer is the only cancer that surpasses prostate cancer in frequency among American men.</data>
      <data key="d6">epidemiological comparison, cancer incidence</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Skin Cancer" target="Primary Cutaneous Lymphomas">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Primary cutaneous lymphomas are explicitly differentiated from skin cancer, as PCL arises from lymphocytes rather than skin cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">differential diagnosis, disease classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer" target="NCCN">
      <data key="d4">15.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN produces guidelines specifically covering the management of squamous cell skin cancer, including cutaneous cancer of the lip.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN publishes patient guidelines specifically covering squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical guidance, medical guidelines&lt;SEP&gt;clinical guidance, patient care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer" target="Squamous Cells">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Squamous cell skin cancer originates directly from squamous cells located in the top layer of the skin.</data>
      <data key="d6">origin, cellular pathology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer" target="Nonmelanoma Skin Cancers">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Squamous cell carcinoma is classified under the nonmelanoma skin cancer category alongside basal cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical classification, categorization</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer" target="Basal Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Squamous cell carcinoma is the second most common skin cancer, following basal cell carcinoma in prevalence.</data>
      <data key="d6">comparative incidence, related conditions</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer" target="Melanoma">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Squamous cell carcinoma is differentiated from melanoma by its cellular origin, behavior, and classification.</data>
      <data key="d6">differential diagnosis, disease comparison</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer" target="Epidermis">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Squamous cell skin cancer forms in the epidermis, the outer layer of the skin.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical localization, pathology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer" target="Sun Exposure">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Excessive long-term sun exposure is identified as the primary risk factor for developing squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">causality, risk factor</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer" target="Indoor Tanning">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Indoor tanning significantly elevates the risk of developing squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">risk elevation, artificial radiation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer" target="Environmental Toxins">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Exposure to environmental pollutants such as tobacco smoke and arsenic contributes to the development of squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">environmental risk, carcinogenesis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer" target="Ultraviolet Rays">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Exposure to ultraviolet radiation from the sun or tanning equipment is the primary driver of squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">etiology, radiation risk</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer" target="Scars and Chronic Wounds">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma can arise within preexisting scars, burns, and chronic nonhealing wounds.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathogenesis, secondary malignancy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer" target="Marjolin Ulcer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Marjolin ulcer is a clinical presentation of cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma developing in scarred tissue.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathological variant, skin cancer</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer" target="Marjolin’s Ulcer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Marjolin’s ulcer is a specific, harder-to-treat variant of squamous cell skin cancer forming in scars, burns, or old wounds.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease variant, pathogenesis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer" target="Bowen’s Disease">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Bowen’s disease represents the earliest superficial stage of squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease stage, early form</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer" target="Cancer Care Team">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Patients diagnosed with squamous cell skin cancer are managed and treated by a specialized cancer care team.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient management, clinical care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer" target="Skin Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Skin biopsy is a core diagnostic method used to test lesions and confirm squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnosis, medical evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer" target="Lymph Node Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A lymph node biopsy is indicated when squamous cell skin cancer is suspected to have metastasized through the lymphatic system.</data>
      <data key="d6">staging, metastatic evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer" target="Surgery">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Surgery is a principal treatment category utilized to remove squamous cell skin cancer tumors.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment options, surgical therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy is a therapeutic treatment category used to treat squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment options, radiation oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer" target="Systemic Therapy">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Systemic therapy delivers cancer drugs throughout the body to treat advanced or widespread squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic therapy is used alone or alongside radiation to manage squamous cell skin cancer when surgery is not feasible.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer treatment, therapeutic application&lt;SEP&gt;treatment options, systemic treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer" target="Clinical Trials">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clinical trials offer treatment alternatives and investigational therapies for squamous cell skin cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment options, experimental research</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer" target="Topical Therapies">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Topical therapies can be utilized for treating certain squamous cell skin cancers and sun-damaged skin areas.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer treatment, dermatologic care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer" target="Mohs Surgery">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mohs surgery is a preferred, highly recommended treatment option for high-risk cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma that has not spread to lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical oncology, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer" target="Curettage and Electrodesiccation">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Curettage and electrodesiccation is a recommended treatment option for early, superficial cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedural treatment, cancer intervention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer" target="Standard Surgical Excision">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Standard surgical excision is a conventional operative method for removing squamous cell skin cancer tumors with clear margins.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical resection, tumor removal</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer" target="Lymph Node Dissection">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A lymph node dissection is performed if squamous cell skin cancer has metastasized to regional lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">metastasis management, surgical oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer" target="Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Sentinel lymph node biopsy is used to assess whether squamous cell skin cancer has spread to primary lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic surgery, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer" target="Immunotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Immunotherapy utilizing PD-1 inhibitors is a preferred systemic treatment for advanced squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">targeted treatment, disease management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer" target="Regional CSCC">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Regional CSCC is a progression or classification of squamous cell skin cancer that has spread to adjacent lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, disease progression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer" target="Metastatic Squamous Cell Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Metastatic squamous cell skin cancer is an advanced, less common form of CSCC where cancer spreads to distant organs.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease progression, staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer" target="Local CSCC">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Local CSCC represents a localized stage of squamous cell skin cancer that has not spread to nearby lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Melanoma" target="NCCN">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN provides patient guidelines and information specifically for melanoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical guidance, medical guidelines</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Melanoma" target="Germline Mutations">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Germline mutations in BRCA1 and BRCA2 are linked to an increased risk of melanoma (skin cancer).</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic susceptibility, hereditary oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Melanoma" target="BRCA2">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Inherited alterations in BRCA2 are linked to elevated incidence of melanoma skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic predisposition, cancer syndrome</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Epidermis" target="Basal Cells">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Basal cells are located in the lower layer of the epidermis.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical structure, cellular component</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Epidermis" target="Squamous Cells">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Squamous cells are flat cells situated in the upper layer of the epidermis.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical structure, cellular component</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Epidermis" target="Melanocytes">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Melanocytes are situated within the lower part of the epidermis.&lt;SEP&gt;Melanocytes reside within the epidermal layer of the skin, producing protective melanin.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical structure, cellular component&lt;SEP&gt;anatomical structure, cellular distribution</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Epidermis" target="Dermis">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The epidermis and dermis are adjacent functional layers constituting human skin.</data>
      <data key="d6">skin anatomy, tissue layers</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Epidermis" target="Skin">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The epidermis constitutes the outermost protective layer of the skin.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical structure, bodily organ</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Dermis" target="Hypodermis">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The dermis lies directly above the hypodermis, together forming the deeper structural layers of the skin.</data>
      <data key="d6">skin anatomy, tissue layers</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Dermis" target="Skin">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The dermis forms the intermediate connective tissue layer of the skin beneath the epidermis.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical structure, bodily organ</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hypodermis" target="Skin">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The hypodermis is the deepest structural layer of the skin consisting of subcutaneous tissue and fat.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical structure, bodily organ</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Basal Cells" target="Squamous Cells">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Basal cells continually divide and migrate upward, flattening to become squamous cells on the skin surface.&lt;SEP&gt;Basal cells continuously divide and mature into cells that replace surface squamous cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">cellular differentiation, maturation&lt;SEP&gt;cellular renewal, physiological process</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Melanocytes" target="UV Radiation">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Melanocytes produce melanin, which protects skin cells against harmful ultraviolet radiation.</data>
      <data key="d6">photoprotection, biological defense</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Melanocytes" target="Melanin">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Melanin is synthesized within epidermal melanocytes to impart coloration and photoprotection.</data>
      <data key="d6">cellular synthesis, pigmentation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adrenal Tumors" target="Adrenocortical Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Adrenocortical carcinoma is the most common form of malignant adrenal cancer among adrenal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, malignancy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adrenal Tumors" target="Neuroblastoma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Neuroblastoma is a pediatric cancerous classification of adrenal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, pediatric oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adrenal Tumors" target="Aldosteronoma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Aldosteronoma is an aldosterone-producing adrenal adenoma classification within adrenal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d6">hormone hypersecretion, tumor subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adrenal Tumors" target="Cushing Syndrome">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cushing syndrome is a condition resulting from a cortisol-producing adrenal tumor.</data>
      <data key="d6">hormonal disorder, disease etiology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adrenal Tumors" target="Adrenal Glands">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Adrenal tumors develop within one or both of the adrenal glands.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease origin, organ pathology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adrenal Tumors" target="Benign Adrenal Tumors">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Benign tumors represent a primary category of adrenal tumors that do not spread.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathological classification, tumor behavior</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adrenal Tumors" target="Functional Adrenal Tumors">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Functional tumors are a category of adrenal tumors capable of actively secreting hormones.</data>
      <data key="d6">hormone hypersecretion, tumor subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adrenal Tumors" target="Malignant Adrenal Tumors">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Malignant tumors constitute the cancerous category of adrenal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, malignancy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adrenal Tumors" target="Imaging Tests">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Adrenal tumors are commonly detected and evaluated using imaging scans such as CT or MRI.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic imaging, tumor detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adrenal Tumors" target="NCCN">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN publishes patient guidelines specifically dedicated to the diagnosis and management of adrenal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical guidelines, disease education</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adrenal Tumors" target="FDG-PET/CT Scan">
      <data key="d4">15.0</data>
      <data key="d5">FDG-PET/CT scans are identified as diagnostic imaging tools specifically helpful for evaluating adrenal tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;FDG-PET/CT scans are utilized to determine the metabolic activity and extent of adrenal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic imaging, tumor localization&lt;SEP&gt;disease assessment, diagnostic application</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adrenal Tumors" target="SSTR-PET Scan">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">SSTR-PET scans are explicitly noted as useful imaging modalities for patients diagnosed with adrenal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic application, tumor evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adrenal Tumors" target="SSTR-PET/CT Scan">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">SSTR-PET scans target somatostatin receptors to aid in the diagnostic imaging of adrenal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d6">receptor targeting, diagnostic imaging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adrenal Tumors" target="Biochemical Tests">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biochemical testing measures hormone levels in bodily fluids to diagnose and monitor adrenal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d6">hormonal assessment, diagnostic testing</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adrenal Tumors" target="Blood Tests">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Blood tests are conducted to evaluate hormone levels secreted by adrenal tumors or response to tumor therapies.</data>
      <data key="d6">laboratory assessment, disease monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adrenocortical Carcinoma" target="Adrenal Cortex">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Adrenocortical carcinoma originates within the cells of the adrenal cortex.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer origin, anatomical site</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adrenocortical Carcinoma" target="Adrenal Gland Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Adrenocortical carcinoma is a specific form of adrenal gland cancer originating in the adrenal cortex.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, malignancy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adrenocortical Carcinoma" target="Adenoma">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Adenoma and adrenocortical carcinoma are distinct forms of adrenal tumors originating in the cortex, differing primarily in malignancy.</data>
      <data key="d6">tumor differentiation, disease classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adrenocortical Carcinoma" target="Beckwith-Wiedemann Syndrome">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome is a hereditary condition that elevates the risk of developing adrenal tumors and cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic predisposition, risk factor</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adrenocortical Carcinoma" target="Li-Fraumeni Syndrome">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Li-Fraumeni syndrome is a genetic hereditary condition recognized as a risk factor for adrenal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic predisposition, risk factor</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Neuroblastoma" target="Adrenal Gland Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Neuroblastoma is classified as a rare cancerous tumor originating in the adrenal glands.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, malignancy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cushing Syndrome" target="Adenoma">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">An adrenal adenoma that secretes excessive cortisol induces Cushing syndrome.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical etiology, hormonal excess</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Aldosteronoma" target="Adenoma">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">An aldosteronoma is a specific type of adrenal adenoma characterized by aldosterone overproduction.</data>
      <data key="d6">tumor classification, endocrine pathology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adrenal Glands" target="Endocrine System">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The adrenal glands are critical hormone-producing components of the body's endocrine system.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical classification, hormone production</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adrenal Glands" target="Adrenal Cortex">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The adrenal cortex constitutes the outer anatomical portion of the adrenal gland.</data>
      <data key="d6">organ anatomy, structural subdivision</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adrenal Glands" target="Adrenal Medulla">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The adrenal medulla constitutes the inner anatomical portion of the adrenal gland.</data>
      <data key="d6">organ anatomy, structural subdivision</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adenoma" target="Adenocarcinoma">
      <data key="d4">44.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Adenocarcinoma is the form of cancer that develops directly within an adenoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenomas are colorectal polyps that can progress over time to form adenocarcinomas, which are invasive cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenomatous polyps serve as precursor lesions that can transform into invasive adenocarcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer that develops within an adenoma polyp is identified as an adenocarcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer that starts within an adenoma polyp is classified as an adenocarcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease progression, cancer development&lt;SEP&gt;disease progression, malignancy transformation&lt;SEP&gt;pathogenesis, oncogenesis&lt;SEP&gt;pathology, cancer classification&lt;SEP&gt;pathology, progression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adenoma" target="Rectal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Adenomas are benign polyps that can evolve over time into invasive rectal adenocarcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenomas are common polyps that can become invasive rectal cancer over time.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer precursor, disease progression&lt;SEP&gt;disease progression, malignancy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adenoma" target="Colonoscopy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">During a colonoscopy, adenomas and other polyps can be found and removed through polypectomy to prevent cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">screening, polyp removal, prevention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adenoma" target="Polypectomy">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Adenomatous polyps are removed via polypectomy to prevent progression into adenocarcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypectomies are utilized to extract adenomas to prevent them from progressing into invasive cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer prevention, surgical intervention&lt;SEP&gt;treatment, polyp excision</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adenoma" target="Colon Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Colon cancer commonly originates from adenomas, which are non-cancerous polyps that can become malignant over time.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease etiology, carcinogenesis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adenoma" target="Pedunculated Polyps">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pedunculated polyps represent a morphological subtype of adenomas that can progress to invasive cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">polyp morphology, tumor development</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adenoma" target="Sessile Polyps">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Sessile polyps are flatter polyp formations that can develop as adenomas in the colon wall.</data>
      <data key="d6">polyp morphology, tumor development</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adenoma" target="Invasive Colon Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Adenomas can gradually progress over many years into invasive colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathology, malignancy transition</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adrenal Gland Cancer" target="Pheochromocytoma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pheochromocytoma is an adrenal tumor arising in the adrenal medulla that can sometimes be cancerous.</data>
      <data key="d6">tumor classification, endocrinology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pheochromocytoma" target="Hereditary Paraganglioma-Pheochromocytoma Syndrome">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hereditary paraganglioma-pheochromocytoma syndrome directly predisposes patients to pheochromocytomas.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic syndrome, tumor development</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Carney Complex" target="Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Carney complex is listed among the inherited syndromes that elevate the risk of developing thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">hereditary syndrome, disease risk</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Familial Adenomatous Polyposis" target="Rectal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Familial adenomatous polyposis causes extensive polyp formation in the rectum and colon, which can progress to invasive rectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">inherited condition, cancer risk</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Familial Adenomatous Polyposis" target="Genetic Counselor">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Genetic counselors discuss inherited risks and coordinate genetic tests for hereditary conditions such as Familial Adenomatous Polyposis.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic counseling, risk assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Familial Adenomatous Polyposis" target="Colon Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Familial adenomatous polyposis is an inherited syndrome characterized by numerous polyps that frequently progress to colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic predisposition, disease origin</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Familial Adenomatous Polyposis" target="Polyps">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Familial adenomatous polyposis causes hundreds to thousands of polyps to grow along the colon and rectum.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease manifestation, symptom profile</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Familial Adenomatous Polyposis" target="Inherited Cancer Syndromes">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Familial adenomatous polyposis is an inherited cancer syndrome leading to extensive polyp formation and cancer development.</data>
      <data key="d6">classification, hereditary condition</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Familial Adenomatous Polyposis" target="Colorectal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Familial adenomatous polyposis is an inherited disorder recognized as a major risk factor for colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">hereditary risk, genetic predisposition</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Familial Adenomatous Polyposis" target="Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Familial adenomatous polyposis is identified as an inherited syndrome linked to an increased risk of thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic risk, hereditary cancer</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Li-Fraumeni Syndrome" target="Pancreatic Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Li-Fraumeni syndrome is a hereditary syndrome that elevates the likelihood of developing pancreatic cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic predisposition, risk factors</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Li-Fraumeni Syndrome" target="Genetic Tests">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Genetic testing identifies hereditary alterations linked to Li-Fraumeni syndrome.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic screening, risk identification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lynch Syndrome" target="Hereditary Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lynch syndrome is an inherited condition that causes hereditary ovarian cancer as well as colon and uterine cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic syndrome, cancer predisposition</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lynch Syndrome" target="Genetic Testing">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Comprehensive genetic testing detects mutations associated with Lynch syndrome.</data>
      <data key="d6">syndrome identification, genetic screening</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lynch Syndrome" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">24.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Families with a history of Lynch syndrome have an elevated risk for developing ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Individuals diagnosed with Lynch syndrome have an increased vulnerability to developing ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch syndrome elevates the lifetime risk of developing ovarian cancer in affected individuals.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer predisposition, disease risk&lt;SEP&gt;hereditary risk, cancer predisposition&lt;SEP&gt;hereditary risk, genetic syndrome</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lynch Syndrome" target="Rectal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lynch syndrome is an inherited genetic condition that significantly elevates the risk of developing rectal and other colorectal cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Rectal tumors with mismatch repair deficiency or microsatellite instability prompt testing for Lynch syndrome.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic predisposition, cancer risk&lt;SEP&gt;genetic screening, hereditary cancer</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lynch Syndrome" target="Genetic Counselor">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Genetic counselors assess patients and order genetic testing to evaluate for inherited syndromes such as Lynch syndrome.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic counseling, testing, diagnosis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lynch Syndrome" target="Mismatch Repair Testing">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Abnormal mismatch repair findings (dMMR or MSI-H) prompt subsequent diagnostic evaluation for inherited Lynch syndrome.&lt;SEP&gt;Abnormal mismatch repair test results (dMMR/MSI-H) indicate the necessity of testing for Lynch syndrome.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic screening, diagnostic indicator&lt;SEP&gt;genetic screening, diagnostic pathway</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lynch Syndrome" target="Endometrial Cancer">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Individuals with Lynch syndrome have a significantly increased risk of developing endometrial cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Inherited mutations associated with Lynch syndrome predispose individuals to a higher risk of developing endometrial cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer predisposition, disease risk&lt;SEP&gt;hereditary risk, disease predisposition</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lynch Syndrome" target="Mismatch Repair Deficiency">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The presence of mismatch repair deficiency (dMMR/MSI-H) prompts testing for Lynch syndrome.</data>
      <data key="d6">biomarker association, hereditary screening</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lynch Syndrome" target="Pancreatic Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lynch syndrome is a hereditary genetic condition recognized as increasing the chance of pancreatic cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic predisposition, risk factors</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lynch Syndrome" target="Genetic Tests">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Genetic tests assess mutations responsible for Lynch syndrome.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic screening, risk identification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lynch Syndrome" target="Colon Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lynch syndrome is an inherited disorder that puts individuals at high risk of developing colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic predisposition, risk factor</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lynch Syndrome" target="Inherited Cancer Syndromes">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lynch syndrome is an inherited cancer syndrome caused by inherited mutations in mismatch repair genes.</data>
      <data key="d6">classification, hereditary predisposition</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lynch Syndrome" target="Colorectal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lynch syndrome is an inherited hereditary condition strongly linked to the risk of developing colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">hereditary risk, genetic predisposition</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lynch Syndrome" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lynch syndrome is an inherited genetic condition that increases susceptibility to urinary tract and bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic risk, hereditary condition</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lynch Syndrome" target="Germline Testing">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A personal or family history of Lynch syndrome is an indicator for pursuing germline genetic testing.</data>
      <data key="d6">hereditary risk, genetic screening</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Von Hippel-Lindau Syndrome" target="Kidney Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Von Hippel-Lindau syndrome increases the risk for developing kidney tumors as well as adrenal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic predisposition, multi-organ risk</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Kidney Cancer" target="NCCN">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
