    <edge source="PET Scan" target="FDG-PET/CT Scan">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">FDG-PET/CT is a specific type of PET scan combined with computed tomography used for cancer evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype, medical imaging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET Scan" target="SSTR-PET Scan">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">SSTR-PET is a specific type of PET scan categorized under positron emission tomography that is helpful for adrenal tumor evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic classification, nuclear medicine</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET Scan" target="CT Scan">
      <data key="d4">57.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CT scans are frequently combined with PET scans to produce integrated PET/CT diagnostic imaging.&lt;SEP&gt;PET and CT technologies are combined to form a combined PET-CT scan.&lt;SEP&gt;PET scans are frequently combined with CT scans to evaluate cancer staging and metabolic activity.&lt;SEP&gt;PET scans are frequently combined with CT scans to locate and evaluate cancer using radiotracers.&lt;SEP&gt;PET scans are frequently paired or combined with CT scans to form a PET/CT imaging modality.&lt;SEP&gt;PET scans are frequently paired with CT scans (PET/CT) to identify cancer spread and examine deep tissue abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;PET scans are frequently paired with CT scans (PET/CT) to offer combined metabolic and anatomical perspectives of prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic combination, multimodality imaging&lt;SEP&gt;diagnostic imaging, combined modalities&lt;SEP&gt;hybrid imaging, multi-modal diagnostic&lt;SEP&gt;imaging combination, diagnostic synergy&lt;SEP&gt;multimodal imaging, cancer detection&lt;SEP&gt;multimodal imaging, diagnostic combination&lt;SEP&gt;technology combination, multimodal imaging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET Scan" target="Radioactive Tracer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A radioactive tracer is injected into the bloodstream, circulating to target cancer cells that appear bright on PET scans.&lt;SEP&gt;A radioactive tracer is injected into the patient's bloodstream as an integral component of a PET scan.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiopharmaceutical, imaging mechanism&lt;SEP&gt;tracer injection, functional imaging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET Scan" target="FDG-PET/CT">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">FDG-PET/CT is a specific form of PET scan that employs fluorodeoxyglucose as a radiotracer.&lt;SEP&gt;FDG-PET/CT is a specific type of PET scan combined with CT that uses a glucose-based radiotracer.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiotracer imaging, metabolic diagnosis&lt;SEP&gt;sub-modality, imaging technology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET Scan" target="MRI">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MRI can be performed alongside PET as an alternative to CT scans for cancer staging.&lt;SEP&gt;PET scans can be combined with MRI imaging to provide functional and anatomical evaluation of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic imaging, multimodal scanning&lt;SEP&gt;multimodal imaging, diagnostic combination</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET Scan" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PET scans highlight active cancer cells to show small clusters of disease and check if ovarian cancer has spread.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease detection, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET Scan" target="PET/CT Scan">
      <data key="d4">36.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A PET scan is integrated with computed tomography to form the combined PET/CT diagnostic procedure.&lt;SEP&gt;PET imaging is combined with CT scanning to produce a hybrid PET/CT scan for enhanced image detail.&lt;SEP&gt;PET/CT is formed by combining standard PET scan imaging with computed tomography (CT).&lt;SEP&gt;PET/CT scan is a hybrid imaging technique combining a standard PET scan with CT.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic combination, medical imaging&lt;SEP&gt;imaging combination, hybrid modality&lt;SEP&gt;imaging modality, technical evolution&lt;SEP&gt;technology integration, multimodal imaging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET Scan" target="Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PET scans identify cancerous prostate cells throughout the body and monitor therapeutic response.</data>
      <data key="d6">metastasis evaluation, cancer monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET Scan" target="PSMA-PET Scan">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PSMA-PET is a targeted subtype of PET imaging that uses tracers specific to prostate cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">specialized diagnostic, imaging subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET Scan" target="PET/MRI Scan">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PET imaging is coupled with MRI technology to form a PET/MRI scan that provides detailed soft tissue and metabolic data.</data>
      <data key="d6">imaging combination, hybrid modality</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET Scan" target="Radiotracer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A PET scan injects a radioactive tracer to detect active cancer cells consuming glucose in the body.</data>
      <data key="d6">tracer application, oncologic imaging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET Scan" target="Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PET scans identify breast cancer cells by detecting their metabolic uptake of radioactive sugar tracers.</data>
      <data key="d6">cellular detection, oncology staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET Scan" target="Cancer Staging">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PET scans contribute essential metabolic and anatomical information to determine cancer staging.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical staging, imaging correlation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET Scan" target="Bone Cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PET scans are imaging procedures used to locate cancer cells and evaluate metabolic activity in tumors.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer detection, imaging evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FDG-PET/CT Scan" target="Neuroendocrine Tumors">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">FDG-PET scans help assess whether neuroendocrine tumors are becoming more aggressive.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease monitoring, malignancy assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FDG-PET/CT Scan" target="18-Fluorodeoxyglucose">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">FDG-PET scans employ 18-fluorodeoxyglucose as a radioactive sugar tracer to identify hypermetabolic cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiotracer, diagnostic imaging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FDG-PET/CT Scan" target="Stage 1 Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">FDG-PET/CT scans are indicated 3 to 6 months post-treatment for stage 1B3 or post-chemoradiation stage 1 cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease staging, surveillance imaging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FDG-PET/CT Scan" target="Stages 2, 3, And 4A Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">FDG-PET/CT is preferred 3 to 6 months after treatment for stages 2, 3, and 4A cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">imaging protocols, staging surveillance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Contrast Materials" target="CT Scan">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Contrast materials are frequently administered orally or intravenously during CT scans to enhance visualization of body structures.</data>
      <data key="d6">imaging enhancement, diagnostic procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Heart Disease" target="Family History">
      <data key="d4">15.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Family history assessments inquire about the presence and age of diagnosis of heart disease in blood relatives.&lt;SEP&gt;Heart disease in blood relatives is specifically recorded as part of the family history.</data>
      <data key="d6">hereditary disease, health record&lt;SEP&gt;hereditary risk, family background</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Heart Disease" target="Care Team">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The care team reviews family history regarding conditions such as heart disease to understand overall health context.</data>
      <data key="d6">health history, risk evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Heart Disease" target="Health Care Provider">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Healthcare providers assess family history for conditions including heart disease.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical inquiry, risk evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Diabetes" target="Family History">
      <data key="d4">15.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Diabetes in family members is documented during the family history intake.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history records document occurrences of diabetes among biological relatives.</data>
      <data key="d6">hereditary disease, health record&lt;SEP&gt;medical history, family screening</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Diabetes" target="Pancreatic Cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Diabetes is an underlying health condition that increases the risk of developing pancreatic cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">risk factors, metabolic comorbidities</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Diabetes" target="Care Team">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The care team documents family history of diabetes during the patient health history assessment.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical history, health evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Diabetes" target="Health Care Provider">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Healthcare providers review family incidence of diabetes to identify familial risk profiles.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical inquiry, risk evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer" target="Family History">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Family history documentation specifically details types and locations of cancer found in blood relatives.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history involves gathering information regarding instances and types of cancer among blood relatives.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic risk, hereditary factors&lt;SEP&gt;hereditary risk, oncological family tracking</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer" target="AML">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">AML is a specific form of cancer affecting blood and bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer" target="Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lymphoma is a specific type of cancer resulting from the abnormal proliferation of lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, malignancy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="High Blood Pressure" target="Physical Exam">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Blood pressure is measured during a physical exam to check for conditions such as high blood pressure.</data>
      <data key="d6">vital signs, clinical monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Environmental Toxins" target="Lung Cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Exposure to environmental carcinogens such as tobacco smoke is highlighted as a primary risk factor for developing lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">environmental risk, carcinogen</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Environmental Toxins" target="Squamous Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Exposure to environmental toxins causes tissue damage that can lead to cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">environmental risk, carcinogenesis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Environmental Toxins" target="Arsenic">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Arsenic is identified as a dangerous environmental pollutant found in soil, water, and air that damages skin.</data>
      <data key="d6">pollutant classification, toxic exposure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Neuroendocrine Tumors" target="Lung Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Neuroendocrine tumors represent another recognized form of lung carcinoma alongside non-small cell lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, tumor types</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SSTR-PET/CT Scan" target="Somatostatin Receptors">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">SSTR-PET scans use targeted tracers that selectively bind to somatostatin receptors on cell surfaces.</data>
      <data key="d6">receptor targeting, diagnostic imaging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Somatostatin" target="Somatostatin Receptors">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Somatostatin receptors are cell-surface binding sites for the regulatory hormone somatostatin.</data>
      <data key="d6">receptor-ligand interaction, hormonal regulation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Melanin" target="Ultraviolet Rays">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Melanin functions biologically to shield skin tissue against injury caused by harmful ultraviolet rays.</data>
      <data key="d6">photoprotection, radiation defense</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Scars and Chronic Wounds" target="Marjolin Ulcer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A Marjolin ulcer is specifically characterized as malignant transformation arising in chronic scars and nonhealing wounds.</data>
      <data key="d6">malignant transformation, chronic wound</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lung Cancer" target="Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Non-small cell lung cancer is the most prevalent general class of lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, cancer subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lung Cancer" target="Small Cell Lung Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Small cell lung cancer represents a distinct, fast-growing major type of lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, cancer subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lung Cancer" target="Tobacco Smoke">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Smoking and exposure to tobacco smoke represent the largest risk factor for the development of lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">causality, risk factor</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lung Cancer" target="Lung Nodule">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lung nodules are evaluated through imaging and biopsies to determine whether they are cancerous.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical diagnosis, risk assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lung Cancer" target="Pulmonologist">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pulmonologists evaluate lung conditions and collaborate on the multidisciplinary care team diagnosing lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical care, diagnosis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lung Cancer" target="Thoracic Surgeon">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Thoracic surgeons perform operations within the chest as part of lung cancer assessment and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical treatment, clinical care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lung Cancer" target="NCCN">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN produces clinical screening guidelines and patient resources for lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient resources, medical guidelines</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lung Cancer" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A biopsy serves as the definitive procedural method for diagnosing whether a patient has lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical diagnosis, tissue sampling</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lung Cancer" target="Lung Cancer Screening">
      <data key="d4">19.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Regular lung cancer screening allows detection of tumors at their earliest and most curable stages.&lt;SEP&gt;Screening tests seek to identify lung cancer at an early, asymptomatic stage when therapeutic success is highest.</data>
      <data key="d6">detection, preventative medicine&lt;SEP&gt;early detection, preventive care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lung Cancer" target="Clinical Trial">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clinical trials recruit patients to study treatments and preventive strategies against lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical research, cancer prevention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lung Cancer" target="Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Most lung cancers belong to the subtype known as carcinomas, which begin in the airway-lining cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Nearly all primary lung cancers belong to the broad pathological category of carcinomas.</data>
      <data key="d6">classification, histology&lt;SEP&gt;pathology, cancer classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lung Cancer" target="Second-Hand Smoke">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Inhaling second-hand smoke significantly elevates an individual's probability of developing lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">carcinogenic exposure, environmental risk</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lung Cancer" target="Radon">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radon exposure is a primary non-tobacco environmental factor causing lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">environmental hazard, cancer etiology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lung Cancer" target="Asbestos">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Asbestos inhalation damages lung tissue and substantially increases lung cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d6">occupational hazard, cancer risk</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lung Cancer" target="Air Pollution">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Airborne pollutants and particulate matter are environmental contributors to lung cancer incidence.</data>
      <data key="d6">environmental risk, respiratory health</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lung Cancer" target="Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Breast cancer can metastasize to the lungs, presenting secondary tumors that must be clinically differentiated from primary lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">metastasis, differential diagnosis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lung Cancer" target="Risk Assessment">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Risk assessment calculates an individual's personal likelihood of developing lung cancer based on activities, genetics, and environment.</data>
      <data key="d6">risk stratification, disease probability</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lung Cancer" target="Genetic Risk Factors">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Genetic traits passed down from biological parents are known to influence a person's risk for lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">heredity, disease etiology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </edge>
