    </edge>
    <edge source="Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia" target="B Cells">
      <data key="d4">19.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CLL develops from abnormal B cells within the immune and lymphatic systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Chronic lymphocytic leukemia originates in B lymphocytes, where abnormal B cells multiply uncontrollably and suppress healthy cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">cellular origin, hematology&lt;SEP&gt;pathophysiology, cellular origin</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia" target="Small Lymphocytic Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CLL and SLL represent the same underlying cancer cells, differing only by cell location and count in the blood versus lymph nodes and spleen.&lt;SEP&gt;Chronic lymphocytic leukemia and small lymphocytic lymphoma are closely related blood disorders addressed concurrently in cancer education guides.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, pathological equivalence&lt;SEP&gt;disease relationship, clinical correlation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia" target="NCCN">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN provides patient guidelines designed to help individuals diagnosed with CLL understand their care and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN publishes patient guidelines and care recommendations to guide patients and providers in making decisions about CLL treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical guidance, patient care&lt;SEP&gt;patient education, clinical guidelines</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia" target="DNA Mutation">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Acquired cellular mutations in bone marrow over time are associated with the development of chronic lymphocytic leukemia in older adults.</data>
      <data key="d6">etiology, oncogenesis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia" target="Bone Marrow">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CLL originates from changes within the bone marrow, where cancerous cells are predominantly found.&lt;SEP&gt;Changes and uncontrolled cell proliferation within bone marrow give rise to abnormal B cells characteristic of CLL.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease origin, pathology&lt;SEP&gt;pathophysiology, disease site</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia" target="Richter Transformation">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CLL patients may develop Richter transformation, a rare and aggressive complication requiring intensive treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Richter transformation is a severe complication or progression that can occur in patients with CLL.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease progression, complication&lt;SEP&gt;disease progression, medical complication</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia" target="Mantle Cell Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CLL is differentiated from mantle cell lymphoma by the presence of specific surface proteins like CD200 and LEF1.</data>
      <data key="d6">differential diagnosis, cellular immunophenotype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia" target="Hematopathologist">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A hematopathologist is responsible for diagnosing CLL by analyzing blood, bone marrow, and lymph node specimens.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer diagnosis, pathology expertise</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia" target="Flow Cytometry">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Flow cytometry is utilized as a confirmatory blood testing method to diagnose CLL and examine cell markers.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic testing, cell analysis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia" target="Watch And Wait">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Watch and wait is a monitoring strategy used for CLL patients who do not require immediate treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Watch and wait is an observational management stage commonly applied to CLL patients when immediate treatment is not required.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease management, clinical surveillance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia" target="Minimal Residual Disease">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Minimal residual disease represents a successful treatment state in CLL where blood cells remain largely healthy.</data>
      <data key="d6">therapeutic outcome, disease status</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia" target="Anemia">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The onset of anemia is a symptom and clinical indicator that treatment for CLL may be needed.</data>
      <data key="d6">symptom, clinical indicator</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia" target="CLL Specialist">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A CLL specialist provides specialized medical guidance, consultations, and personalized treatment advice for patients with chronic lymphocytic leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical consultation, disease management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia" target="Biomarker Tests">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biomarker tests analyze genetic mutations and chromosomal defects in CLL cells to inform prognosis and select treatments.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic testing, treatment planning</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia" target="Bone Marrow Tests">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Bone marrow tests are utilized to evaluate causes of low blood cell counts in patients diagnosed with CLL.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical evaluation, disease diagnosis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia" target="Diagnostic CT">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Diagnostic CT scans assess the anatomical spread of disease and check for enlarged lymph nodes before starting CLL treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease staging, cancer imaging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia" target="Oncologist">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The oncologist directs cancer care and treatment decisions for patients with CLL.</data>
      <data key="d6">oncological care, treatment direction</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia" target="Differential">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A differential measures white blood cell counts, which are typically elevated at the time of CLL diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic testing, hematologic analysis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia" target="Comprehensive Metabolic Panel">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A comprehensive metabolic panel evaluates organ health, specifically kidney and liver function, in patients with CLL.</data>
      <data key="d6">screening evaluation, physiological assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia" target="17p Deletion">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The 17p deletion is a critical biomarker identified in CLL cells to inform prognosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic alteration, prognostic indicator</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia" target="Chemoimmunotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemoimmunotherapy is a targeted systemic treatment option used for patients with CLL.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment modality, cancer therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia" target="Targeted Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Targeted therapy is being evaluated and used to treat specific molecular subtypes of CLL.</data>
      <data key="d6">targeted treatment, clinical oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia" target="Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chronic lymphocytic leukemia increases the risk of anal cancer due to immune system impairment.</data>
      <data key="d6">hematologic risk, immune impairment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Immunosuppressants" target="Human Papillomavirus">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Immunosuppressants weaken the immune system, hindering the body's capacity to clear HPV infections.</data>
      <data key="d6">immunosuppression, infection persistence</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Immunosuppressants" target="Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Use of anti-rejection immunosuppressant drugs post-transplant increases the risk of anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">medication risk, immunosuppression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Skin Punch Biopsy" target="Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Skin punch biopsy is utilized to sample suspicious skin tissue for the pathological diagnosis of cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical testing, diagnostic procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Skin Punch Biopsy" target="Leukemia Predisposition Syndromes">
      <data key="d4">26.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A skin punch biopsy is performed to isolate germline DNA to determine if a patient has an inherited leukemia predisposition syndrome.&lt;SEP&gt;A skin punch biopsy is used to assess inherited germline genetics for leukemia predisposition syndromes, distinguishing them from somatic leukemia mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;A skin punch biopsy provides non-leukemic DNA necessary to evaluate whether a patient has inherited a leukemia predisposition syndrome.")</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic diagnosis, hereditary predisposition&lt;SEP&gt;genetic verification, diagnostic procedure&lt;SEP&gt;hereditary testing, sample procurement</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Skin Punch Biopsy" target="Acute Myeloid Leukemia">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Skin punch biopsy provides DNA that has not been altered by AML to distinguish germline genetics from leukemia-specific mutations.</data>
      <data key="d6">differential diagnosis, disease testing</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Excision Biopsy" target="Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Excision biopsy is used to remove and evaluate suspected cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma lesions, often providing definitive treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnosis, surgical treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Excision Biopsy" target="Hospital">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">An excision biopsy for skin lesions can be performed in a hospital setting.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedure location, clinical setting</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma" target="Actinic Keratosis">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Actinic keratosis represents a precancerous condition with an elevated risk of developing into cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease progression, risk factor</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma" target="Marjolin's Ulcer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Marjolin's ulcer is a specific form of squamous cell skin cancer that arises within chronic wounds, scars, or burn sites.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease subtype, cancer manifestation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma" target="Local Squamous Cell Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Local squamous cell skin cancer is an early or localized stage of cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma confined to the initial area.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, disease staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma" target="Regional Squamous Cell Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Regional squamous cell skin cancer is a stage of cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma indicating metastasis into lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, metastasis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma" target="Doctor">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A doctor diagnoses, stages, and directs treatment plans for cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical diagnosis, patient care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma" target="PET/CT Scan">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PET/CT scans help identify deep tissue invasion or advanced progression of cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease staging, multimodality imaging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma" target="Care Team">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The patient's care team utilizes scans and biopsies to track cancer cell activity and formulate therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">oncological care, treatment management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma" target="Low-Risk CSCC">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Low-Risk CSCC is a subtype category of Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Low-Risk CSCC is one of the clinical risk stratifications used to evaluate and treat cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, risk assessment&lt;SEP&gt;disease classification, risk categorization</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma" target="High-Risk CSCC">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">High-Risk CSCC is a risk category of cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma that indicates an increased risk of local recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, risk assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma" target="Very High-Risk CSCC">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Very High-Risk CSCC is an advanced risk category of cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma with higher risk of recurrence and metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, risk assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma" target="Clinical Trial">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clinical trial participation is presented as an alternative or additional treatment option for patients with CSCC.</data>
      <data key="d6">experimental therapy, clinical care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma" target="Mohs Surgery">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mohs surgery is an indicated surgical treatment method for local cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical oncology, skin cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma" target="Curettage And Electrodesiccation">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Curettage and electrodesiccation is a specific surgical approach used to treat squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical oncology, tissue destruction</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma" target="Standard Surgical Excision">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Standard surgical excision is used to remove localized cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical oncology, tumor resection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy serves as a primary alternative for nonsurgical patients or an adjuvant therapy to kill remaining CSCC cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation oncology, adjuvant treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma" target="Systemic Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Systemic therapy is utilized when cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma spreads beyond local margins or fails surgery and radiation.</data>
      <data key="d6">advanced cancer care, systemic treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma" target="High-Risk or Very High-Risk CSCC">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">High-Risk or Very High-Risk CSCC is an advanced classification category of Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, risk categorization</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma" target="Tumor Grade">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Tumor grade determines how aggressive and rapidly Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma is expected to grow and spread.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathology assessment, tumor grading</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma" target="Local CSCC">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Local CSCC is a classification of cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma where the tumor has not spread to lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma" target="Regional CSCC">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Regional CSCC represents a stage of cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma characterized by spread to nearby lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, metastasis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma" target="Risk Assessment">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Risk assessment evaluates prognostic features of CSCC to assign tumors into low-, high-, or very high-risk categories to guide treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">prognostic staging, risk stratification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma" target="NCCN">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN provides patient guidelines covering the treatment of cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma of the vermilion lip.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical guidance, medical resources</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Actinic Keratosis" target="Topical Therapies">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Actinic keratosis is frequently treated using topical therapies such as 5-fluorouracil, imiquimod, calcipotriene, and tirbanibulin.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical treatment, topical therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Actinic Keratosis" target="5-Fluorouracil">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">5-fluorouracil is a topical chemotherapy applied specifically to treat actinic keratosis lesions.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical therapy, precancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Actinic Keratosis" target="Imiquimod">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Imiquimod cream stimulates an immune response to damage and eliminate abnormal actinic keratosis cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">immunotherapy, lesion management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Actinic Keratosis" target="Tirbanibulin">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Tirbanibulin is applied once daily for 5 days as a microtubule inhibitor to eradicate actinic keratoses on the face or scalp.</data>
      <data key="d6">targeted treatment, topical oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Actinic Keratosis" target="Hyperkeratosis">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hyperkeratotic actinic keratoses present as thick, rough lesions requiring softening or pre-treatment before topical drug application.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathological variation, clinical manifestation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Actinic Keratosis" target="Chemical Peels">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemical peels such as trichloroacetic acid are used as treatment options for actinic keratoses.</data>
      <data key="d6">chemical resurfacing, lesion removal</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Actinic Keratosis" target="Resurfacing Techniques">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Resurfacing techniques including laser and dermabrasion are used to treat sun-damaged skin affected by actinic keratoses.</data>
      <data key="d6">physical resurfacing, skin therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Actinic Keratosis" target="Topical Diclofenac">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Topical diclofenac is applied as a therapeutic option for actinic keratoses.</data>
      <data key="d6">pharmacotherapy, anti-inflammatory treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Regional Squamous Cell Skin Cancer" target="Needle Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Needle biopsy is performed on enlarged lymph nodes to confirm whether cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma has progressed to regional nodal disease.</data>
      <data key="d6">staging confirmation, diagnostic testing</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Regional Squamous Cell Skin Cancer" target="Nodal Disease">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Nodal disease is the clinical manifestation of regional squamous cell skin cancer when cancer reaches lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, metastasis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="Contrast Dye">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Contrast dye is administered during an MRI to make soft tissues and organs appear more distinctly.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic enhancement, imaging protocol</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MRI is utilized to clarify uncertain ultrasound findings and identify distant spread of ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">secondary imaging, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="Care Team">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The care team can provide sedatives if patients experience anxiety or claustrophobia inside the MRI machine.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient care, procedural support</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="Oropharyngeal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MRI is an imaging test used to evaluate the main tumor and neck structures to guide treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic imaging, clinical assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="Contrast Material">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Specific contrast materials are utilized during MRI examinations to improve the visibility of organs and structures.</data>
      <data key="d6">image enhancement, diagnostic contrast</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="Open MRI">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Open MRI is a specific structural variant of magnetic resonance imaging designed with open sides.&lt;SEP&gt;Open MRI is an alternative design of MRI that leaves openings on the ends to alleviate claustrophobia.</data>
      <data key="d6">classification, imaging variant&lt;SEP&gt;imaging classification, equipment design</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="Closed MRI">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Closed MRI is a specific form of MRI characterized by an enclosing capsule-like magnet.&lt;SEP&gt;Closed MRI is the standard configuration of magnetic resonance imaging where magnets enclose the patient.</data>
      <data key="d6">classification, imaging variant&lt;SEP&gt;imaging classification, equipment design</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="Imaging Tests">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MRI is an imaging method employed to evaluate the prostate, determine tumor presence, and assist in biopsy decisions.</data>
      <data key="d6">imaging modality, diagnostic imaging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="Breast Diagnostic Radiologist">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A breast diagnostic radiologist interprets MRI scans and other diagnostic imaging.</data>
      <data key="d6">imaging analysis, clinical diagnostics</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MRI scans are utilized in certain situations to guide biopsy procedures in the breast.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedural guidance, diagnostic imaging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="Primary CNS Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MRI scans are utilized as a primary imaging tool to visualize tumors and evaluate the extent of primary CNS lymphoma in the brain.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic imaging, cancer detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="Intrathecal Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MRI findings showing cancer in the spine help indicate the necessity of administering intrathecal chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic indication, treatment planning</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="Whole-Brain Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Whole-brain radiation therapy is capable of treating microscopic CNS tumors that remain undetectable on an MRI scan.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic limitations, therapeutic coverage</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MRI is used to evaluate the primary tumor and check whether cervical cancer has spread to adjacent pelvic structures.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, soft tissue imaging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="Small Cell Neuroendocrine Carcinoma of the Cervix">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Patients diagnosed with NECC are specifically recommended to undergo an MRI of the brain during initial evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical protocol, neuroimaging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="Transvaginal Ultrasound">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Transvaginal ultrasound serves as an alternative diagnostic imaging technique if a pelvic MRI cannot be performed.</data>
      <data key="d6">imaging alternative, diagnostic evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="Cancer Staging">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Results from pelvic and brain MRIs are utilized to establish the clinical stage of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">staging evaluation, diagnostic scans</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="X-Ray">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MRI provides detailed soft-tissue imaging without the ionizing radiation associated with X-rays.</data>
      <data key="d6">comparative imaging, radiation safety</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="Ultrasound">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Detection of suspicious neck lymph nodes during an ultrasound warrants further evaluation using MRI imaging.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic escalation, imaging confirmation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="Glioma">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MRI is identified as the most useful imaging test for diagnosing and planning treatment for gliomas.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic imaging, tumor detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="CT Scan">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A CT scan serves as an alternative diagnostic imaging method when an MRI cannot be performed or is unavailable.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic alternatives, medical imaging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="Brain Tumor">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MRI scans are utilized to detect, locate, measure, and monitor brain tumors.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic imaging, disease detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="Diagnostic Imaging">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MRI is a key radiological modality included in diagnostic imaging.</data>
      <data key="d6">classification, diagnostic modality</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Needle Biopsy" target="CLL">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Needle biopsy provides lymph node tissue samples for immunophenotyping to help diagnose CLL safely.</data>
      <data key="d6">minimally invasive biopsy, diagnosis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hospital" target="Clinical Trial">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hospitals and associated facilities make clinical trials accessible to patients seeking primary CNS lymphoma therapies.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical research, healthcare delivery</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hospital" target="Supportive Care">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hospitals offer supportive care services, such as counseling, physical therapy, and palliative care, to support lymphoma patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient support, healthcare services</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hospital" target="Integrative Medicine">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hospitals and care facilities provide integrative medicine alongside cancer treatments to improve quality of life.</data>
      <data key="d6">complementary medicine, institutional care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Head-to-Toe Skin Exam" target="Doctor">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A doctor conducts a complete head-to-toe skin exam following a CSCC diagnosis to look for secondary lesions.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical examination, post-diagnosis care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lymph Node Exam" target="Doctor">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A doctor palpates lymph nodes to determine whether cancer has begun spreading beyond the original skin site.</data>
      <data key="d6">physical exam, lymphatic assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lymph Node Exam" target="Nodal Disease">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A lymph node examination allows the clinician to feel for swelling that indicates nodal disease.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease detection, physical evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET/CT Scan" target="Positron Emission Tomography Scan">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PET/CT is a hybrid imaging technique incorporating positron emission tomography.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic integration, multimodality imaging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET/CT Scan" target="Computed Tomography Scan">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PET/CT combines computed tomography with positron emission tomography for enhanced anatomical and functional localization.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic integration, hybrid imaging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET/CT Scan" target="FDG-PET/CT">
      <data key="d4">27.0</data>
      <data key="d5">FDG-PET/CT is a specialized PET/CT scan that utilizes the radiotracer fluorodeoxyglucose.&lt;SEP&gt;FDG-PET/CT is a specialized variation of PET/CT that employs fluorodeoxyglucose as a radioactive tracer.&lt;SEP&gt;FDG-PET/CT is a specific form of PET/CT scan that utilizes the F-18 fluorodeoxyglucose radiotracer.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedural subtype, diagnostic application&lt;SEP&gt;scan type, radiotracer imaging&lt;SEP&gt;scan type, radiotracer integration</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET/CT Scan" target="CT Scan">
      <data key="d4">26.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A PET/CT scan integrates CT cross-sectional imaging with PET data to identify cancerous pelvic lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;CT scanning provides the anatomical component in combined PET/CT diagnostic imaging.&lt;SEP&gt;PET/CT scans integrate computed tomography with PET scanning to provide combined anatomical and functional images.</data>
      <data key="d6">imaging combination, diagnostic technology&lt;SEP&gt;imaging combination, hybrid modality&lt;SEP&gt;multimodal imaging, diagnostic cross-reference</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET/CT Scan" target="Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PET/CT imaging provides combined metabolic and anatomical localization of prostate cancer and pelvic lymph node metastases.&lt;SEP&gt;PET/CT scans illustrate primary prostate cancer alongside metastatic spread to pelvic lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease visualization, staging&lt;SEP&gt;multimodal imaging, metastasis detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET/CT Scan" target="Primary CNS Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Whole-body PET/CT scans are recommended to determine whether primary CNS lymphoma has spread outside the central nervous system.&lt;SEP&gt;Whole-body PET/CT scans are used during the staging process of primary CNS lymphoma to detect systemic cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d6">imaging, cancer staging&lt;SEP&gt;systemic staging, whole-body imaging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET/CT Scan" target="Computed Tomography">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Computed tomography is combined with positron emission tomography to produce detailed whole-body PET/CT scans.</data>
      <data key="d6">imaging combination, diagnostic modality</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET/CT Scan" target="Positron Emission Tomography">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A PET/CT scan integrates PET imaging with CT technology for enhanced diagnostic accuracy.&lt;SEP&gt;Positron emission tomography is integrated with CT to detect malignant areas as bright spots across the body.</data>
      <data key="d6">imaging combination, metabolic scan&lt;SEP&gt;multimodal imaging, procedure integration</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET/CT Scan" target="Testicular Ultrasound">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A testicular ultrasound may not be necessary if a preceding whole-body PET/CT scan shows no evidence of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">subsequent testing, clinical decision</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET/CT Scan" target="Cancer Center">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PET/CT scans are conducted using specialized machinery housed within cancer centers.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical testing, facility equipment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET/CT Scan" target="Diagnostic Imaging">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PET/CT scanning is an advanced imaging procedure classified under diagnostic imaging.</data>
      <data key="d6">classification, diagnostic modality</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Nodal Disease" target="Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Nodal disease refers to malignant involvement within regional lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathology, lymphatic oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Contrast Dye" target="CT Scan">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Contrast dye is routinely injected during CT scans to enhance anatomical visualization unless contraindicated.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic enhancement, imaging protocol</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radioactive Tracer" target="Bone Scan">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Bone scans use a radioactive tracer that accumulates in damaged bone areas to highlight bone metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d6">skeletal imaging, radioactive labeling</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bowen’s Disease" target="Superficial Treatments">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Superficial treatments are utilized to treat Bowen’s disease because it is restricted to the top layer of skin.</data>
      <data key="d6">indication, therapeutic approach</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Superficial Treatments" target="Photodynamic Therapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Photodynamic therapy is a specific type of superficial treatment used for skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment classification, modality</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Superficial Treatments" target="Cryotherapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cryotherapy is a modality of superficial treatment that destroys cells through freezing.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment classification, modality</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Superficial Treatments" target="Topical Therapies">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Topical therapies are categorized as superficial treatments applied directly to the skin.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment classification, modality</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cryotherapy" target="Hyperkeratosis">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cryotherapy is an intervention used prior to or in treatment of hyperkeratotic actinic keratosis lesions.</data>
      <data key="d6">cryoablation, lesion reduction</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Topical Therapies" target="5-Fluorouracil">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">5-fluorouracil is a topical prescription cream used directly on the skin for early skin cancer and precancerous lesions.</data>
      <data key="d6">pharmacotherapy, topical agent</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Topical Therapies" target="Imiquimod">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Imiquimod is a topical medication classified under superficial treatments for early skin cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">pharmacotherapy, topical agent</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Topical Therapies" target="Calcipotriene">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Calcipotriene is a topical cream utilized in dermatologic cancer therapy to stimulate the immune system.</data>
      <data key="d6">pharmacotherapy, immune activation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Care Team" target="Dermatologists">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Dermatologists serve as essential members of the skin cancer care team responsible for exams and diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d6">team composition, clinical role</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Care Team" target="Mohs Surgeons">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mohs surgeons are specialist members of the care team performing precise tumor removal and reconstruction.</data>
      <data key="d6">team composition, surgical care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Care Team" target="Surgical Oncologists">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Surgical oncologists are team members who remove tumors that may extend beyond the skin.</data>
      <data key="d6">team composition, surgical intervention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Care Team" target="Medical Oncologists">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Medical oncologists are care team specialists responsible for administering chemotherapy and systemic treatments.</data>
      <data key="d6">team composition, medical oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Care Team" target="Radiation Oncologists">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation oncologists are members of the cancer care team who administer radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">team composition, radiation therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Care Team" target="Pathologists">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pathologists support the care team by examining biopsy specimens to stage and identify tumors.</data>
      <data key="d6">team composition, diagnostic support</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Care Team" target="Plastic Surgeons">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Plastic surgeons collaborate with the care team to close complex wounds and perform reconstruction.</data>
      <data key="d6">team composition, surgical reconstruction</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Care Team" target="Oncology Nurses">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Oncology nurses are integral care team members providing hands-on care, navigation, and support.</data>
      <data key="d6">team composition, patient support</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Care Team" target="Head And Neck Surgeons">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Head and neck surgeons participate in the care team for tumors affecting deeper areas of the head, throat, ears, or neck.</data>
      <data key="d6">team composition, surgical specialization</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Care Team" target="Pharmacists">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pharmacists are integrated into the care team to guide medication administration and manage drug side effects.</data>
      <data key="d6">team composition, medication management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Care Team" target="Geneticists">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Geneticists contribute to the cancer care team by assessing genetic syndrome risks and counseling families.</data>
      <data key="d6">team composition, genetic risk assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Care Team" target="Pulmonologist">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A pulmonologist is one of the core board-certified expert members composing the cancer care team.</data>
      <data key="d6">team composition, medical specialization</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Care Team" target="Thoracic Radiologist">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A thoracic radiologist serves on the multidisciplinary cancer care team to provide chest imaging expertise.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical imaging, multidisciplinary collaboration</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Care Team" target="Thoracic Surgeon">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A thoracic surgeon works within the cancer care team to determine and perform operative treatments.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical oncology, team collaboration</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Care Team" target="Supportive Care">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The cancer care team discusses symptoms and provides supportive care to patients throughout treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient support, symptom management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Care Team" target="Pregnancy Test">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The cancer care team administers a pregnancy test before initiating cancer therapy in eligible patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient safety, clinical screening</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Care Team" target="Fertility Specialist">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The cancer care team provides patient referrals to fertility specialists prior to cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient referral, multidisciplinary care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pathologists" target="Plastic Surgeons">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Plastic surgeons collaborate intraoperatively with pathologists to achieve immediate examination of margins and tissue samples.</data>
      <data key="d6">intraoperative collaboration, diagnostic coordination</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lymph Node Biopsy" target="Blastic Plasmacytoid Dendritic Cell Neoplasm">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A lymph node biopsy is performed when BPDCN is suspected in an AML workup.&lt;SEP&gt;A lymph node biopsy is performed when evaluating patients suspected of having the BPDCN subtype of AML.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic procedure, subtype identification&lt;SEP&gt;subtype evaluation, tissue sampling</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="5-Fluorouracil" target="Calcipotriene">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Calcipotriene is frequently co-administered with 5-fluorouracil to treat actinic keratoses by enhancing immune response.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcipotriene is frequently combined with 5-fluorouracil to treat actinic keratosis.</data>
      <data key="d6">combination therapy, dermatologic pharmacology&lt;SEP&gt;combination therapy, drug synergy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mohs Surgery" target="Peripheral and Deep En Face Margin Assessment">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mohs surgery is the most prevalent and recognized form of peripheral and deep en face margin assessment (PDEMA).</data>
      <data key="d6">procedural subtype, margin evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mohs Surgery" target="Mohs Surgeon">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mohs surgery must be conducted by a Mohs surgeon who both excises the tissue and examines it microscopically.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical specialty, surgical execution</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mohs Surgery" target="Low-Risk CSCC">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mohs surgery is an accepted surgical option for low-risk CSCC that has not spread to lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical treatment, surgical procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mohs Surgery" target="High-Risk or Very High-Risk CSCC">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mohs surgery is the preferred surgical treatment option for very high-risk CSCC.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical treatment, surgical preference</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mohs Surgery" target="Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Sentinel lymph node biopsy may be considered before or during Mohs surgery/PDEMA to assess cancer spread to lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic procedure, surgical staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mohs Surgery" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy is considered if Mohs surgery does not achieve complete removal or reveals concerning features.</data>
      <data key="d6">adjuvant therapy, multimodal treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mohs Surgery" target="Local CSCC">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mohs surgery is a recommended surgical procedure for local CSCC, especially in high-risk scenarios or when clear margins are needed.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical procedure, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mohs Surgery" target="PDEMA">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PDEMA and Mohs surgery are closely related margin-controlled surgical procedures used to treat high-risk or residual CSCC.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical margin assessment, specialized surgery</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Peripheral and Deep En Face Margin Assessment" target="Standard Surgical Excision">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Peripheral and deep en face margin assessment (PDEMA) techniques can also be utilized during standard surgical excision procedures.</data>
      <data key="d6">margin evaluation, surgical enhancement</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Curettage and Electrodesiccation" target="Low-Risk CSCC">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Curettage and electrodesiccation is a recommended surgical treatment for early, low-risk CSCC.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical treatment, surgical procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Standard Surgical Excision" target="Pathologist">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Tissue removed via standard surgical excision is examined by a pathologist to assess surgical margins for residual cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathology examination, margin assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Standard Surgical Excision" target="Low-Risk CSCC">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Standard surgical excision is a recommended surgical option for localized low-risk CSCC.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical treatment, surgical procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Standard Surgical Excision" target="High-Risk or Very High-Risk CSCC">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Standard surgical excision with a wider margin is an option for treating high-risk and very high-risk CSCC.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical treatment, surgical procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Standard Surgical Excision" target="Local CSCC">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Standard surgical excision is a primary treatment modality recommended for local CSCC.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment recommendation, oncology protocol</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lymph Node Dissection" target="Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A sentinel lymph node biopsy determines early lymphatic spread, which can precede a more extensive lymph node dissection if positive.&lt;SEP&gt;Sentinel lymph node biopsy is a targeted method used during lymph node dissection to evaluate the primary drainage nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic surgery, lymphatic mapping&lt;SEP&gt;lymphatic assessment, staging and treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lymph Node Dissection" target="Surgery">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lymph node dissection is a surgical component performed alongside cervical cancer resections to assess lymphatic spread.</data>
      <data key="d6">lymphadenectomy, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lymph Node Dissection" target="Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lymph nodes are removed and tested during surgery for cervical cancer to assess and limit cancer dissemination.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, surgical oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lymph Node Dissection" target="Pelvic Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pelvic lymph nodes are directly targeted and removed during lymph node dissection procedures for cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical staging, lymphadenectomy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lymph Node Dissection" target="Abdominoperineal Resection">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lymph node dissection can be performed concurrently with APR or as an independent surgical procedure.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical procedure, operative management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy" target="Surgeon">
      <data key="d4">26.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A surgeon performs the sentinel lymph node biopsy using dye and radioactive tracer to locate sentinel nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgeons perform sentinel lymph node biopsies by injecting dye and removing lymph nodes for analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;The surgeon uses injected radioactive dye to locate and surgically remove the sentinel lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical procedure, clinical role&lt;SEP&gt;surgical procedure, cancer staging&lt;SEP&gt;surgical procedure, lymph node staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy" target="Pathologist">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pathologists analyze the lymph node tissue samples removed during a sentinel lymph node biopsy to detect cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;The sentinel lymph nodes removed during surgery are sent to a pathologist for microscopic evaluation and cancer detection.</data>
      <data key="d6">laboratory pathology, cancer diagnosis&lt;SEP&gt;tissue evaluation, laboratory testing</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy" target="General Anesthesia">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Sentinel lymph node biopsy procedures are generally conducted under general anesthesia.</data>
      <data key="d6">anesthetic protocol, surgical procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy" target="Mastectomy">
      <data key="d4">24.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A sentinel lymph node biopsy is commonly performed concurrently with breast surgery such as a mastectomy.&lt;SEP&gt;A sentinel lymph node biopsy is often conducted prior to or in conjunction with breast removal during mastectomy surgery.&lt;SEP&gt;Sentinel lymph node biopsy is performed during mastectomy surgery to check if cancer has spread beyond the breast.</data>
      <data key="d6">concurrent surgical procedure, cancer staging&lt;SEP&gt;surgical procedure, cancer staging&lt;SEP&gt;surgical staging, operative procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy" target="Lumpectomy">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A sentinel lymph node biopsy is frequently carried out alongside a lumpectomy to check for regional spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Sentinel lymph node biopsy is conducted during a lumpectomy to evaluate whether cancer cells have reached the lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">concurrent surgical procedure, cancer staging&lt;SEP&gt;surgical procedure, diagnostic staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy" target="Breast Cancer Staging">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A sentinel lymph node biopsy determines lymph node involvement, which is a key component (N) of breast cancer staging.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic procedure, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hyperkeratosis" target="Topical Keratolytics">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Topical keratolytics such as urea, lactic acid, or salicylic acid are used to soften hyperkeratotic outer skin layers prior to primary therapies.</data>
      <data key="d6">pre-treatment, keratolytic action</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hyperkeratosis" target="Topical Tazarotene">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Topical tazarotene is employed prior to other interventions to help manage hyperkeratotic actinic keratoses.</data>
      <data key="d6">retinoid therapy, pre-treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surgeon" target="Mohs Procedure">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Surgeons perform Mohs procedures and subsequent wound repair methods such as skin grafts or linear closures.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical intervention, skin cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surgeon" target="Low Anterior Resection">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The surgeon performs low anterior resection and evaluates whether to reconnect the bowel or place a colostomy.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical procedure, operative care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surgeon" target="Colostomy Creation">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The surgeon creates a colostomy to allow the bowel site to heal or when reconnection is not possible.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical intervention, patient care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surgeon" target="Transrectal Ultrasound">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Surgeons rely on transrectal ultrasound imagery to remove prostate tissue samples without abdominal incisions.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical guidance, minimally invasive procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surgeon" target="Inflammatory Breast Cancer (IBC)">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A surgeon definitively diagnosed inflammatory breast cancer after multiple previous doctor visits failed to detect it.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical diagnosis, surgical consultation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surgeon" target="Modified Radical Mastectomy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A surgeon performs the modified radical mastectomy to excise the tumor, breast tissue, and lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical specialty, surgical execution</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surgeon" target="Colectomy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A surgeon performs the colectomy, assesses lymph nodes, and rejoins the healthy segments of the colon.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical execution, operative care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surgeon" target="Treatment Team">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The surgeon is a member of the expert care team who carries out surgical interventions for thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">team composition, surgical care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surgeon" target="Thyroidectomy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Surgeons perform thyroidectomies to remove cancerous thyroid tissue.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedure execution, surgical treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surgeon" target="Pathologist">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pathologists analyze tissues resected by surgeons to guide subsequent operative and clinical decisions.</data>
      <data key="d6">interdisciplinary care, diagnostic collaboration</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surgeon" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">An experienced surgeon is specifically recommended to carry out the biopsy procedure at the treating medical facility.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical procedure, clinical expertise</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radiation Oncologist" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">27.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation oncologists are the medical specialists responsible for assessing and delivering radiation therapy courses.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiation oncologists specialize in the planning and delivery of radiation therapy for cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The radiation oncologist determines the dosage and oversees the delivery of radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical specialty, treatment administration&lt;SEP&gt;oncology specialization, treatment administration&lt;SEP&gt;radiation oncology, therapy prescription</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radiation Oncologist" target="Assessment Team">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A radiation oncologist participates in the assessment team to plan radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation treatment, specialist care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radiation Oncologist" target="Whole-Brain Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The radiation oncologist oversees whole-brain radiation therapy and advises patients on its expected side effects and neurological risks.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical care, patient consultation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radiation Oncologist" target="Treatment Team">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The radiation oncologist is a physician member of the multidisciplinary thyroid care team.&lt;SEP&gt;The radiation oncologist is a specialized physician working within the broader treatment team managing cancer therapies.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical collaboration, multidisciplinary care&lt;SEP&gt;team composition, radiation therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radiation Oncologist" target="Simulation">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The radiation oncologist utilizes CT imaging and specialized software during and following simulation to design the treatment plan.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment planning, clinical workflow</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radiation Oncologist" target="External Beam Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A radiation oncologist plans and oversees external beam radiation therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;The radiation oncologist prescribes the dosage, fractions, and configuration for external beam radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical supervision, oncology practice&lt;SEP&gt;specialty role, clinical treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radiation Oncologist" target="Multidisciplinary Care Team">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A radiation oncologist is a member of the multidisciplinary care team focused on prescribing and planning radiation treatments.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer therapy, multidisciplinary team</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radiation Oncologist" target="Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The radiation oncologist devises the specific treatment plan and beam trajectory for IMRT.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical planning, medical oversight</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radiation Oncologist" target="Pelvis">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The radiation oncologist plans the radiation beam trajectories targeted at the tumor and lymph nodes in the pelvis.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical targeting, clinical planning</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radiation Oncologist" target="Groin">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The radiation oncologist devises treatment fields directed toward the lymph nodes in the groin.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical targeting, radiation field</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mohs Procedure" target="Skin Graft">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A skin graft may be applied immediately or days following a Mohs surgical excision to reconstruct the tissue.</data>
      <data key="d6">post-surgical reconstruction, wound repair</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CSCC" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy is utilized to treat CSCC, especially after surgery or for lesions in challenging anatomical areas like the head and neck.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer therapy, disease management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CSCC" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy, including platinum compounds like cisplatin and carboplatin, is utilized in the management of cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">systemic cancer therapy, pharmacotherapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CSCC" target="Scalp">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma frequently manifests on the scalp and can recur within previously radiated tissue.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease localization, recurrence risk</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CSCC" target="Cisplatin">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cisplatin is administered as a systemic therapeutic agent against squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">systemic therapy, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CSCC" target="Carboplatin">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Carboplatin is utilized in chemotherapy regimens to treat cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">systemic therapy, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="External Beam Radiation Therapy" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">28.0</data>
      <data key="d5">EBRT is a delivery method of radiation therapy using external x-ray machines.&lt;SEP&gt;External beam radiation therapy is one of the primary forms of radiation therapy used to treat CSCC.&lt;SEP&gt;External beam radiation therapy is the standard modality used when administering radiation therapy for bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation modality, cancer therapy&lt;SEP&gt;radiation technique, treatment modality&lt;SEP&gt;treatment subtype, oncology therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="External Beam Radiation Therapy" target="Electron Beam Radiation">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Electron beams represent a common modality of external beam radiation therapy targeted at superficial tumors.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation modality, external beam delivery</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="External Beam Radiation Therapy" target="Photon Beam Radiation">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Photon radiation is a form of external beam radiation therapy utilized for deeper, invasive lesions.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation modality, external beam delivery</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="External Beam Radiation Therapy" target="Regional Nodal Irradiation">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Regional nodal irradiation is delivered as part of external beam radiation therapy targeting specific lymph node basins.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation technique, treatment delivery</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="External Beam Radiation Therapy" target="Whole Breast Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Whole breast radiation therapy is administered via external beam radiation using a linear accelerator.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation modality, therapeutic delivery</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="External Beam Radiation Therapy" target="Ovarian Transposition">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Ovarian transposition is performed prior to EBRT to relocate the ovaries out of the radiation field and protect them from damage.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian transposition is performed to shift ovaries away from the radiation beam to avoid radiation-induced premature menopause.</data>
      <data key="d6">fertility preservation, side-effect prevention&lt;SEP&gt;fertility preservation, surgical intervention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="External Beam Radiation Therapy" target="Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">External beam radiation therapy is a core therapeutic modality employed for cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;External beam radiation therapy is used to kill cervical cancer cells and target nearby involved lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation oncology, cancer therapy&lt;SEP&gt;radiation oncology, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="External Beam Radiation Therapy" target="Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Intensity-modulated radiation therapy is an advanced variant of external beam radiation therapy used to reduce collateral damage to surrounding organs.&lt;SEP&gt;Intensity-modulated radiation therapy is an advanced, specialized subtype of external beam radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">technical subtype, radiation modality&lt;SEP&gt;technological advancement, treatment modality</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="External Beam Radiation Therapy" target="Stereotactic Body Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stereotactic body radiation therapy is a specialized form of external beam radiation therapy focused on focal metastatic sites.&lt;SEP&gt;Stereotactic body radiation therapy is a specialized, targeted type of external beam radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation modality, specialized treatment&lt;SEP&gt;treatment subtype, radiation therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="External Beam Radiation Therapy" target="Simulation">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A simulation session is required prior to radiation therapy to plan dosage and patient positioning.&lt;SEP&gt;Simulation is a required planning session utilizing CT imaging and positioning tools conducted before starting external beam radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment planning, procedural preparation&lt;SEP&gt;treatment planning, quality assurance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="External Beam Radiation Therapy" target="Menopausal Hormone Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Menopausal hormone therapy is used to treat menopause caused by radiation therapy when the ovaries are within the radiation field.</data>
      <data key="d6">hormone management, side-effect mitigation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="External Beam Radiation Therapy" target="Chemoradiation">
      <data key="d4">46.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemoradiation concurrently combines external beam radiation therapy with platinum-based chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation consists of external beam radiation therapy delivered simultaneously with chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation integrates external beam radiation therapy concurrently with chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation integrates external beam radiation therapy with platinum-based chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;External beam radiation therapy is a core component administered concurrently as part of chemoradiation.</data>
      <data key="d6">combination therapy, radiation treatment&lt;SEP&gt;multimodal protocol, concurrent treatment&lt;SEP&gt;multimodal therapy, concurrent treatment&lt;SEP&gt;treatment component, combined modality&lt;SEP&gt;treatment component, radiation modality</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="External Beam Radiation Therapy" target="Premature Menopause">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy fields that encompass the ovaries cause ovarian failure, leading directly to premature menopause.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation toxicity, ovarian dysfunction</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="External Beam Radiation Therapy" target="Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Brachytherapy is administered in combination with or following external beam radiation therapy for cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy is typically initiated in the final week of or immediately following EBRT to treat residual tumor tissue.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation oncology, sequential therapy&lt;SEP&gt;sequential therapy, multimodal radiation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="External Beam Radiation Therapy" target="Platinum Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Platinum chemotherapy is delivered concurrently with external beam radiation therapy to achieve chemoradiation.</data>
      <data key="d6">concurrent therapy, chemoradiation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="External Beam Radiation Therapy" target="Vaginal Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Vaginal brachytherapy may be administered supplementary to external beam radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">multimodal radiation, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="External Beam Radiation Therapy" target="Metastatic Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">External beam radiation therapy and its specialized forms are used to manage localized metastatic lesions in distant organs.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation oncology, palliative and local control</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="External Beam Radiation Therapy" target="Local Or Regional Recurrence">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">EBRT is recommended after surgery for local or regional recurrence if radiation has not been previously utilized.</data>
      <data key="d6">adjuvant treatment, recurrence</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="External Beam Radiation Therapy" target="Anaplastic Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">External beam radiation therapy is almost always used as the standard therapeutic intervention for anaplastic thyroid cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;External beam radiation therapy is specifically indicated for aggressive cancers such as anaplastic thyroid carcinoma that cannot be resected surgically.</data>
      <data key="d6">indicated treatment, disease management&lt;SEP&gt;therapeutic intervention, cancer management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="External Beam Radiation Therapy" target="Papillary Cancer">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">External beam radiation therapy is rarely used to manage papillary thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">contraindication, rare treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="External Beam Radiation Therapy" target="Follicular Cancer">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">External beam radiation therapy is rarely employed for follicular thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">contraindication, rare treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="External Beam Radiation Therapy" target="Linear Accelerator">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">External beam radiation therapy utilizes a linear accelerator to administer external radiation.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment equipment, radiation delivery</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Brachytherapy" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">19.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Brachytherapy is a specific form of radiation therapy applied directly to or near the tumor.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy is an internal delivery form of radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation technique, internal irradiation&lt;SEP&gt;treatment subtype, radiation delivery</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Brachytherapy" target="Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Brachytherapy is a key internal radiation therapy method used in the targeted management and treatment of cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy is a standard internal radiation treatment option for cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer therapy, targeted radiation&lt;SEP&gt;radiation therapy, oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Brachytherapy" target="Intracavitary Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Intracavitary brachytherapy is the most frequently utilized form of brachytherapy for treating cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedural subtype, internal radiation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Brachytherapy" target="Interstitial Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Interstitial brachytherapy is a specialized variant of brachytherapy used for advanced tumors extending into the parametrium.</data>
      <data key="d6">specialized technique, procedural subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Brachytherapy" target="High Dose-Rate Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">High dose-rate brachytherapy is the predominant dosing regimen utilized by most cancer centers for brachytherapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation delivery method, dosing technique</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Brachytherapy" target="Low Dose-Rate Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Low dose-rate brachytherapy is an alternative dosing schedule within brachytherapy treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">dosing modality, radiation therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Brachytherapy" target="Tandem And Ovoid Applicator">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The tandem and ovoid applicator is a specialized delivery device used in brachytherapy procedures.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical device, radiation delivery</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Brachytherapy" target="Pelvic Recurrence">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Brachytherapy may be given after surgery for pelvic recurrence in patients not previously irradiated.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation modality, recurrent cancer</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Brachytherapy" target="Local Or Regional Recurrence">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Brachytherapy may be given following surgery or considered in select patients with localized cancer recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d6">adjuvant therapy, local recurrence</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adjuvant Therapy" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy is commonly used as an adjuvant therapy following surgery to reduce cancer recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d6">post-operative therapy, recurrence prevention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adjuvant Therapy" target="Systemic Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Adjuvant therapy is postoperative systemic therapy administered to eliminate residual disease after surgical resection.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment staging, postoperative care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Basal Cell Nevus Syndrome" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy is contraindicated in patients with basal cell nevus syndrome due to the elevated risk of radiation-induced cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment contraindication, genetic vulnerability</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lupus" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy may not be appropriate for patients suffering from connective tissue disorders such as lupus.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical contraindication, connective tissue disease</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lupus" target="Autoimmune Disease">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lupus is specifically presented as an example of an autoimmune disease.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical classification, disease example</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Scleroderma" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Patients with scleroderma may be contraindicated for radiation therapy due to connective tissue complications.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical contraindication, connective tissue disease</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Head And Neck" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy serves as an effective treatment option for small tumors located in the head and neck region.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical targeting, clinical efficacy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Scalp" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Tissues of the scalp are weakened by radiation, which significantly impairs post-radiation wound healing if CSCC recurs.</data>
      <data key="d6">tissue vulnerability, adverse effects</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Secondary Intention Healing" target="Surgical Excision">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Following surgical excision, wounds may be managed by secondary intention healing rather than stitched shut.</data>
      <data key="d6">wound management, surgical outcome</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Linear Closure" target="Surgical Excision">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Linear closure is a primary wound repair technique utilized by surgeons following surgical excision.</data>
      <data key="d6">wound repair, surgical closure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Skin Graft" target="Surgical Excision">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Skin grafts are frequently transplanted to cover large or complex defects left after surgical excision.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical reconstruction, wound repair</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Skin Graft" target="PDEMA">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Skin graft repair can take place on the same day or shortly after a PDEMA procedure.</data>
      <data key="d6">post-surgical reconstruction, tissue repair</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
