    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Early-Stage Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Early-stage prostate cancer is a localized classification of prostate cancer where cells remain within the prostate.&lt;SEP&gt;Early-stage prostate cancer is a localized clinical stage of prostate cancer that has not spread outside the prostate.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, localized cancer&lt;SEP&gt;disease staging, cancer classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Advanced-Stage Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d4">36.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Advanced-stage prostate cancer is a severe progression of prostate cancer where cancer cells have spread outside the gland.&lt;SEP&gt;Advanced-stage prostate cancer is an advanced state of prostate cancer characterized by metastasis outside the prostate gland.&lt;SEP&gt;Advanced-stage prostate cancer represents a progression of prostate cancer where disease spreads beyond the prostate gland.&lt;SEP&gt;Advanced-stage prostate cancer represents cancer progression where cells have spread beyond the prostate gland.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease progression, metastasis&lt;SEP&gt;disease progression, staging&lt;SEP&gt;disease stage, progression&lt;SEP&gt;disease staging, cancer metastasis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Risk Factors For Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Risk factors such as advancing age, genetic mutations, and family history increase the likelihood of developing prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Risk factors such as age and family history correlate with an elevated probability of developing prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease risk, etiology&lt;SEP&gt;etiology, risk association</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="BRCA2 Gene">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Inherited mutations in the BRCA2 gene significantly increase an individual's risk of developing prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic predisposition, risk factor</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Black Males">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Black males face higher rates of prostate cancer diagnosis, more aggressive progression, and higher mortality rates than other demographics.</data>
      <data key="d6">health disparities, disease vulnerability</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Diet And Lifestyle">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Obesity, smoking, and high-fat diets increase prostate cancer risk and mortality, while exercise and plant-based foods may reduce risk.</data>
      <data key="d6">lifestyle risk, disease progression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Cancer Researchers">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cancer researchers study cellular mechanisms, risk factors, and treatments for prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer researchers study prostate cancer to discover what triggers prostate cells to grow out of control.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical research, oncology investigation&lt;SEP&gt;scientific inquiry, oncology research</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Prostate-Specific Antigen Test">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The PSA test is used to detect potential prostate cancer and monitor cancer recurrence or response to therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">biomarker screening, disease detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Digital Rectal Exam">
      <data key="d4">41.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A digital rectal exam is utilized to check for irregular or hardened areas on the prostate that may signify a tumor.&lt;SEP&gt;An abnormal digital rectal exam is a clinical finding that points to possible prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Digital rectal exams assess the prostate for abnormalities and are used alongside PSA and imaging tests to evaluate cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;The digital rectal exam serves as a common screening test to check for signs of prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The digital rectal exam serves as a direct screening tool to detect tumors, irregularities, or hardening in the prostate gland.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer screening, clinical indicator&lt;SEP&gt;diagnostic screening, clinical examination&lt;SEP&gt;physical screening, cancer detection&lt;SEP&gt;physical screening, preliminary diagnosis&lt;SEP&gt;screening procedure, cancer detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Scientists">
      <data key="d4">15.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Scientists study prostate cancer to develop better detection methods, therapies, and treatment choices.&lt;SEP&gt;Scientists study prostate cancer to develop more effective detection methods and treatment options for patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical research, disease understanding&lt;SEP&gt;medical research, treatment development</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Doctor">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The doctor assesses diagnostic results and clinical history to determine the presence of prostate cancer and plan treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;The doctor uses diagnostic test results to assess prostate cancer risk, plan care, and discuss treatment with the patient.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical care, cancer management&lt;SEP&gt;diagnosis, patient care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="PSA Testing">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Elevated results from PSA testing serve as a warning sign indicating the potential presence of prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer screening, diagnostic indicator</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">34.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A biopsy directly removes and examines prostate tissue to confirm the presence of prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A biopsy is conducted to identify and evaluate primary prostate cancer or metastatic spread in organs and bones.&lt;SEP&gt;Biopsies provide tissue evidence to determine the presence and severity of prostate cancer alongside imaging tests.&lt;SEP&gt;Biopsy is the clinical procedure used to confirm and diagnose the presence of prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer diagnosis, tissue confirmation&lt;SEP&gt;medical diagnosis, cancer detection&lt;SEP&gt;tissue confirmation, cancer diagnosis&lt;SEP&gt;tissue diagnosis, risk assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Imaging Tests">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Imaging modalities visualize the primary tumor, prostate size, and potential metastasis of prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging tests assess whether prostate cancer has spread beyond the prostate gland, though they may not be required in early stages.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer imaging, disease staging&lt;SEP&gt;disease staging, metastasis detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Prostate-Specific Antigen">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Abnormally high concentrations of prostate-specific antigen in the blood can indicate prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease indicator, diagnostic biomarker</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="CT Scan">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CT scans are employed to capture detailed internal images to evaluate cancer spread when an MRI scan is unavailable or not suitable.</data>
      <data key="d6">alternative diagnostic, cancer assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="PSMA/PET Scan">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PSMA/PET imaging targets specific antigens on prostate cancer cells to detect advanced metastasis and recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d6">targeted imaging, recurrence monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Bone Scan">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Bone scans are used in prostate cancer management to detect skeletal metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone scans specifically evaluate whether prostate cancer has spread to the bones.</data>
      <data key="d6">metastasis detection, oncological imaging&lt;SEP&gt;metastasis detection, skeletal imaging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Care Team">
      <data key="d4">34.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The care team assesses cancer risk, staging, and treatment strategies for prostate cancer management.&lt;SEP&gt;The care team evaluates, diagnoses, and monitors patients who have or are suspected of having prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The care team reviews diagnostic findings to determine the severity of prostate cancer and formulate a treatment plan.&lt;SEP&gt;The care team works directly with the patient to diagnose prostate cancer and implement personalized treatment regimens.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer management, clinical decision-making&lt;SEP&gt;oncological care, treatment planning&lt;SEP&gt;oncology management, treatment planning&lt;SEP&gt;patient management, clinical treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="PSA Test">
      <data key="d4">25.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Elevated PSA levels in the bloodstream serve as an indicator of potential prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;PSA test levels help determine whether prostate cancer risk is low or if further imaging and interventions are needed.&lt;SEP&gt;The PSA test is used to detect potential prostate cancer and monitor the effectiveness of ongoing cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d6">biomarker screening, cancer detection&lt;SEP&gt;biomarker screening, risk assessment&lt;SEP&gt;diagnostic testing, cancer monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="PET/MRI Scan">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PET/MRI scans combine metabolic detection and high-resolution soft-tissue imaging to evaluate prostate cancer extent.</data>
      <data key="d6">hybrid imaging, staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Prostate Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">20.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A prostate biopsy is the definitive invasive test required to confirm the presence and extent of prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A prostate biopsy is the definitive invasive test used to confirm the diagnosis and assess the aggressiveness of prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer diagnosis, pathological confirmation&lt;SEP&gt;diagnosis, cancer confirmation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Germline Testing">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Germline testing is used in prostate cancer care to discover whether an individual carries inherited genetic risks for the disease.</data>
      <data key="d6">risk assessment, oncology testing</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Bone">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Prostate cancer can cause bone damage, which shows as bright spots on imaging scans using radioactive tracers.</data>
      <data key="d6">bone metastasis, disease progression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="PSA Level">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">An elevated PSA level is a key clinical sign that indicates the potential presence of prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Elevated PSA levels in the bloodstream serve as an indicator of potential risk for prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">biomarker, screening indicator&lt;SEP&gt;disease biomarker, risk indicator</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Molecular Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biomarker testing helps establish whether prostate cancer is lower risk or higher risk to inform management and treatment choices.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular biomarker testing is one of the primary genetic testing modalities used in prostate cancer management.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic test, molecular oncology&lt;SEP&gt;prognostic evaluation, risk stratification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Genetic Germline Testing">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Germline testing is recommended for prostate cancer patients with specific risk levels or family histories.</data>
      <data key="d6">risk assessment, cancer screening</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Germline Mutations">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Inherited germline mutations increase the susceptibility and risk profile for prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic susceptibility, hereditary risk</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Risk Groups">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Risk groups provide the framework for predicting prostate cancer prognosis and tailoring aggressive or conservative treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d6">prognosis, treatment guidance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Prognosis">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Prognosis determines the expected course, outcome, and therapeutic strategy for an individual diagnosed with prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease trajectory, treatment planning</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Grade Group">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The Grade Group system determines how rapidly prostate cancer is likely to grow and spread.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease aggressiveness, pathological grading</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Gleason Score">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The Gleason score reflects how aggressive prostate cancer cells are based on their tissue architecture.</data>
      <data key="d6">histological scoring, malignancy estimation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Grade Group System">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Prostate cancer is categorized into Grade Groups to assess its severity, risk level, and potential aggressiveness.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, prognosis assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Male Reproductive System">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Prostate cancer originates within the prostate gland, which is an essential organ of the male reproductive system.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical origin, disease manifestation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Health Care Providers">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Health care providers diagnose prostate cancer and assess symptoms that may require special testing.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical care, diagnosis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Prostate Gland">
      <data key="d4">19.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Prostate cancer originates from uncontrolled cell mutations directly within the prostate gland.&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate cancer originates when cells within the prostate gland grow out of control.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical origin, pathogenesis&lt;SEP&gt;disease origin, pathology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Age">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Advancing age is the single greatest risk factor for prostate cancer, particularly for individuals aged 65 and above.</data>
      <data key="d6">risk factor, demographic correlation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Family History">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A family history of prostate cancer in close relatives substantially elevates an individual's personal risk of developing the disease.</data>
      <data key="d6">hereditary risk, genetic predisposition</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Black People">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Black individuals are categorized as having an increased risk for developing prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">demographic risk, health disparity</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Family Member">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Having a close family member with prostate cancer increases an individual's risk of developing the disease.</data>
      <data key="d6">hereditary risk, family history</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Diagnostic Tests">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Diagnostic tests are utilized to confirm the presence, extent, and potential spread of prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease detection, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="PSMA-PET Scan">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PSMA-PET scans are utilized to detect recurrence and evaluate metastatic prostate cancer expressing PSMA.&lt;SEP&gt;PSMA-PET scans specifically track prostate cancer cells by binding to prostate-specific membrane antigen.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer detection, disease monitoring&lt;SEP&gt;disease monitoring, targeted imaging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Metastatic Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Metastatic prostate cancer is an advanced progression of primary prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease progression, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Genetic Testing">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Genetic testing evaluates hereditary cancer risk and guides personalized clinical management for prostate cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing identifies inherited and tumor-specific mutations to evaluate cancer risk and guide management decisions.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic testing, genomic profiling&lt;SEP&gt;disease evaluation, risk assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="TNM Staging System">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The TNM staging system provides a standardized method to describe the extent, size, and spread of prostate cancer tumors.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, clinical evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Hereditary Gene Testing">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hereditary gene testing identifies inherited mutations in genes like BRCA1 and BRCA2 that elevate the risk of prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">hereditary risk, genetic screening</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Liquid Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Liquid biopsy collects blood samples to look for prostate cancer cells and tumor DNA circulating in the body.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic testing, cancer detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="BRCA1">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mutations in BRCA1 increase the risk of prostate cancer and guide therapeutic choices.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic risk, cancer predisposition</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="BRCA2">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mutations in BRCA2 are strongly associated with aggressive prostate cancer and sensitivity to targeted treatments.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic risk, biomarker</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="MSH6">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Alterations in MSH6 form part of the hereditary genetic testing panel for prostate cancer risk assessment.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic screening, risk assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="PMS2">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PMS2 gene mutations are screened in patients with prostate cancer and a history of related cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic testing, risk evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="TNM System">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The TNM system is used to evaluate tumor extent, lymph node spread, and metastasis in prostate cancer patients to guide treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease assessment, diagnostic criteria</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="NCCN">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN provides educational resources, guidelines, and apps detailing assessments and care pathways for prostate cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical guidance, patient education</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Hormone Therapy">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hormone therapy has served as the mainstay treatment for advanced prostate cancer for decades.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormone therapy is a primary treatment modality used to slow or shrink advanced prostate cancer by cutting off hormone fuels.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer treatment, therapeutic management&lt;SEP&gt;oncological therapy, disease management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Non-Hormone Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Non-hormone therapies such as radiation, chemotherapy, targeted therapy, and immunotherapy are key treatment options for advanced prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer treatment, multimodality therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Cancer Staging">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cancer staging is essential for predicting the prognosis of prostate cancer and developing an appropriate treatment plan.</data>
      <data key="d6">prognostic assessment, clinical planning</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="PSA">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">High levels of PSA in the bloodstream serve as an indicator and diagnostic marker for prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">biomarker, diagnostic indicator</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Clinical Trials">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clinical trials are listed among the management options available for patients navigating prostate cancer care.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical research, treatment modality</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Androgen Deprivation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Androgen deprivation therapy is used as a primary treatment to reduce testosterone levels in prostate cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer treatment, hormone regulation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Anti-Androgens">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Anti-androgens are established oral medications used in treating advanced prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical treatment, oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Corticosteroids">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Corticosteroids are prescribed as oral medications utilized in the management and treatment protocols of prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer therapy, pharmacological treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Androgen Synthesis Inhibitors">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Androgen synthesis inhibitors are oral therapeutics designed to arrest androgen production in prostate cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">oncology, targeted therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Surgical Castration">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Surgical castration is an effective hormonal intervention employed to treat advanced prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer treatment, hormone blocking</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Health Care Provider" target="Care Team">
      <data key="d4">24.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Health care providers form the core multidisciplinary members of a patient's oncology care team.&lt;SEP&gt;Individual healthcare providers comprise and collaborate within the multidisciplinary care team.&lt;SEP&gt;The health care provider works as part of the patient's care team to track risks and family health history.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical collaboration, patient care&lt;SEP&gt;team composition, professional role</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Health Care Provider" target="Physical Exam">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Health care providers administer the physical exam by checking vitals, palpating organs, and inspecting lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;The health care provider directly conducts the physical examination to inspect organs, vitals, and lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical administration, patient examination&lt;SEP&gt;clinical evaluation, patient examination</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Health Care Provider" target="Cancer Treatment">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Health care providers manage cancer treatment, advise on reproductive planning, and discuss imaging results with patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient care, clinical guidance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Health Care Provider" target="Fertility Preservation">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Patients are advised to discuss their fertility preservation options and pregnancy intentions directly with their health care provider.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical consultation, reproductive health</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Health Care Provider" target="Patient Portal">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Health care providers review diagnostic reports that are subsequently made accessible to patients via their patient portal.</data>
      <data key="d6">health communication, information access</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Health Care Provider" target="Genetic Counselor">
      <data key="d4">15.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Health care providers refer patients with significant family medical history to genetic counselors for risk evaluation.&lt;SEP&gt;Health care providers refer patients with suspected hereditary risks to genetic counselors for specialized care.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical referral, clinical collaboration&lt;SEP&gt;medical referral, collaborative care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Health Care Provider" target="Histology">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A health care provider interprets histology reports and discusses the findings with the patient to guide next steps in clinical management.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical consultation, diagnosis interpretation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Health Care Provider" target="Family Cancer Health History">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Patients share their family cancer health history with their health care provider to assess cancer risk and testing needs.</data>
      <data key="d6">risk assessment, clinical consultation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Health Care Provider" target="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The treatment team and health care providers recommend appropriate biomarker tests for patient care.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical recommendation, care planning</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Health Care Provider" target="Hereditary Genetic Testing">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A health care provider orders and coordinates hereditary genetic testing based on a patient's family history.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical referral, diagnostic ordering</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Health Care Provider" target="Physical Examination">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Health care providers conduct the physical exam, checking vitals, organs, and lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical evaluation, medical assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Health Care Provider" target="Imaging Tests">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Health care providers receive diagnostic reports from radiologists and discuss imaging test results with the patient.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient care, diagnostic communication</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Health Care Provider" target="Genetic Testing">
      <data key="d4">15.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Health care providers refer patients for genetic testing to learn more about the biology of their cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Healthcare providers evaluate whether family members have had genetic testing when gathering a family health history.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical referral, diagnostic testing&lt;SEP&gt;diagnostic review, hereditary assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Western Countries" target="Small Cell Lung Cancer (SCLC)">
      <data key="d4">5.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Diagnoses of SCLC in people without smoking history are documented as rare in Western countries.</data>
      <data key="d6">epidemiology, geographic occurrence</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Small Cell Lung Cancer (SCLC)" target="NCCN">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN offers patient guidelines and educational resources related to the diagnosis and management of SCLC.</data>
      <data key="d6">guidelines, medical information</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Small Cell Lung Cancer (SCLC)" target="Paraneoplastic Syndromes">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">SCLC can lead to paraneoplastic syndromes as an abnormal systemic bodily reaction to the cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">symptom manifestation, medical complication</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Small Cell Lung Cancer (SCLC)" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A biopsy procedure is used to obtain tissue or fluid to confirm the presence of lung cancer, including SCLC.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic procedure, medical testing</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Small Cell Lung Cancer (SCLC)" target="Cigarette Smoking">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cigarette smoking is the primary cause and strongest risk factor for developing Small Cell Lung Cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">etiology, cancer risk</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Small Cell Lung Cancer (SCLC)" target="Carcinogens">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Exposure to carcinogens increases the risk and likelihood of developing SCLC.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease risk, etiology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Small Cell Lung Cancer (SCLC)" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Prior radiation therapy to the chest is a clinical risk factor for the future development of SCLC.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment side effect, risk factor</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Small Cell Lung Cancer (SCLC)" target="Airways Of The Lungs">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">SCLC tumors originate in and spread through the airways and lung lobes, impairing respiratory function.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical impact, respiratory impairment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Small Cell Lung Cancer (SCLC)" target="CT Scan">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CT scans are imaging events used to detect lung tumors such as SCLC before or after symptoms appear.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic imaging, cancer detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer (NSCLC)" target="NCCN">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN provides patient guidelines and cancer resources addressing NSCLC.</data>
      <data key="d6">guidelines, medical resources</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Esophageal Cancer" target="Digestive System">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Esophageal cancer directly impacts the esophagus, which is a key component of the digestive system.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical localization, pathology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Esophageal Cancer" target="Esophageal Squamous Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Esophageal squamous cell carcinoma is one of the two primary histological types of esophageal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, oncology subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Esophageal Cancer" target="Esophageal Adenocarcinoma">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Esophageal adenocarcinoma is one of the two primary histological types of esophageal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, oncology subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Esophageal Cancer" target="Siewert Types">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Siewert types are utilized to stage tumors positioned between the esophagus and stomach as esophageal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, medical classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Esophageal Cancer" target="Early-Stage Esophageal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Early-stage esophageal cancer represents one of the major diagnostic progression categories of esophageal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease staging, clinical categorization</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Esophageal Cancer" target="Locoregional Esophageal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Locoregional cancer represents an intermediate stage of esophageal cancer where tumor cells invade adjacent walls and nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease staging, clinical categorization</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Esophageal Cancer" target="Metastatic Esophageal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Metastatic cancer is the advanced staging category of esophageal cancer where cells spread to distant organs.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease staging, metastasis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Esophageal Cancer" target="Care Team">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The care team designs and executes diagnostic and therapeutic plans for patients with esophageal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical treatment, patient care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Esophageal Cancer" target="Esophagogastric Junction">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Tumors initiating in the stomach that cross the esophagogastric junction are often categorized and treated as esophageal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical boundary, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Esophageal Cancer" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biopsies provide tissue samples required to diagnose and characterize esophageal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic confirmation, pathology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Esophageal Cancer" target="Imaging Tests">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Imaging tests detect tumor extent, invasion, and distant metastases in esophageal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">staging investigation, oncological evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Esophageal Cancer" target="Endoscopy Procedures">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Endoscopy procedures allow direct inspection and biopsy sampling of suspected esophageal neoplasms.</data>
      <data key="d6">investigative endoscopy, diagnostic procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Esophageal Cancer" target="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biomarker testing assesses molecular characteristics of esophageal tumors to assist treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d6">molecular oncology, targeted assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Esophageal Cancer" target="Genetic Risk Testing">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Genetic risk testing identifies underlying genetic profiles and risks associated with esophageal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetics, risk profiling</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Esophageal Cancer" target="Nutritional Assessment">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Nutritional assessment is critical in managing esophageal cancer due to swallowing impairment and metabolic impacts.</data>
      <data key="d6">nutritional support, patient care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Esophageal Cancer" target="Performance Status">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A patient's performance status directly influences tolerance and planning for esophageal cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">functional assessment, therapy suitability</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Esophageal Cancer" target="NCCN">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN publishes patient guidelines and resources dedicated to esophageal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN publishes specialized patient guidelines and resources concerning esophageal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">guideline publication, patient education&lt;SEP&gt;medical guidelines, patient resources</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Digestive System" target="Peristalsis">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Peristalsis is the physiological process that moves food along the digestive tract from the esophagus into the stomach.</data>
      <data key="d6">digestive motility, muscular function</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Digestive System" target="Oral Cavity">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The oral cavity forms the entry point and primary initial structure of the digestive system.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical structure, bodily systems</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Digestive System" target="Hepatobiliary System">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The hepatobiliary system produces and secretes bile and functions as a specialized part of the larger digestive system.</data>
      <data key="d6">biological hierarchy, anatomical system</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Digestive System" target="Gastrointestinal Tract">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The gastrointestinal tract represents the continuous pathway component of the digestive system.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical subdivision, digestive biology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Assessment Team" target="Primary Care Provider">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The primary care provider refers the patient to the multidisciplinary assessment team when cancer is suspected.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical referral, clinical care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Assessment Team" target="Medical Oncologist">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A medical oncologist serves on the team assessing cancer diagnosis and systemic treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">oncological care, treatment planning</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Assessment Team" target="Interventional Radiologist">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">An interventional radiologist obtains tissue samples for the assessment team via biopsy.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic procedure, team member</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Assessment Team" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The assessment team uses biopsy procedures to collect tissue and confirm cancer diagnoses.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic protocol, tumor confirmation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Primary Care Provider" target="Health Checkup">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Primary care providers administer routine regular health checkups and order preventative care.</data>
      <data key="d6">preventative care, clinical examination</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Medical Oncologist" target="Care Team">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
