    <edge source="Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor Therapy" target="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biomarker tests evaluating PD-L1 levels and tumor mutational burden guide the administration of immune checkpoint inhibitor therapies.</data>
      <data key="d6">targeted therapy, diagnostic guidance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor Therapy" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy is combined with immune checkpoint inhibitor therapy to treat cancers expressing PD-L1 with a CPS of 1 percent or higher.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemotherapy is frequently combined with immune checkpoint inhibitor therapy for patients displaying sufficient PD-L1 expression.</data>
      <data key="d6">combination therapy, cancer treatment&lt;SEP&gt;combination therapy, oncology treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor Therapy" target="Tumor Mutational Burden-High">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Immune checkpoint inhibitors are frequently used to treat metastatic or unresectable cancers identified as tumor mutational burden-high.&lt;SEP&gt;TMB-H status serves as a decisive biomarker indicating responsiveness to immune checkpoint inhibitor therapies targeting PD-1 and PD-L1.</data>
      <data key="d6">targeted treatment, oncology therapy&lt;SEP&gt;therapeutic indication, molecular targeting</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor Therapy" target="PD-L1 Testing">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PD-L1 expression results help oncologists determine eligibility for immune checkpoint inhibitor therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment selection, immunotherapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Nicotine" target="Surgery">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Nicotine use impairs wound healing and increases the risk of post-surgical complications.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical complication, tissue healing</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Fine-Needle Aspiration" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">27.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Fine-needle aspiration is a needle-based diagnostic technique used to perform biopsies of tumors and lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Fine-needle aspiration is a specific needle-based biopsy procedure used to evaluate tumors and lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Fine-needle aspiration is a specific technique used to perform a biopsy.</data>
      <data key="d6">biopsy method, tissue sampling&lt;SEP&gt;procedural subtype, diagnostic sampling&lt;SEP&gt;sub-type, diagnostic procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Fine-Needle Aspiration" target="Pathologist">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pathologists analyze the cell and tissue samples collected via fine-needle aspiration to diagnose cancer and determine its type.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic evaluation, pathology analysis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Fine-Needle Aspiration" target="Molecular Tests">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">When fine-needle aspiration biopsy cannot definitively differentiate between benign and malignant lesions, molecular tests are applied to aid the diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic refinement, pathology assistance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Fine-Needle Aspiration" target="Follicular Thyroid Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Fine-needle aspiration can only suggest follicular carcinoma, as definitive diagnosis requires pathological examination of vascular or capsular invasion.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic limitation, biopsy evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Fine-Needle Aspiration" target="Oncocytic Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Fine-needle aspiration cannot definitively confirm oncocytic carcinoma without evaluating vascular or capsular invasion on resected tissue.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic limitation, medical biopsy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Fine-Needle Aspiration" target="Inguinal Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Fine-needle aspiration is performed to sample suspicious or enlarged groin lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">biopsy procedure, nodal sampling</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MSI-H/dMMR Mutation" target="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biomarker testing assesses tumors for alterations such as microsatellite instability and deficient mismatch repair.&lt;SEP&gt;MSI-H and dMMR analyses are specific molecular biomarker tests used to detect DNA repair defects in tumor cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">genomic profiling, biomarker identification&lt;SEP&gt;molecular profiling, mutation detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MSI-H/dMMR Mutation" target="Mismatch Repair Proteins">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MSI-H/dMMR arises when mismatch repair proteins fail or are deficient in fixing errors in microsatellites.</data>
      <data key="d6">protein defect, DNA repair failure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PD-L1 Testing" target="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d4">25.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PD-L1 testing is a biomarker evaluation that quantifies immune protein expression to inform cancer treatment choices.&lt;SEP&gt;PD-L1 testing is a form of biomarker testing evaluating immune protein levels for checkpoint inhibitor therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;PD-L1 testing is a specific form of biomarker testing that checks for immune protein expression.</data>
      <data key="d6">biomarker assessment, therapy selection&lt;SEP&gt;medical testing, subtype&lt;SEP&gt;molecular diagnostics, immunotherapy assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PD-L1 Testing" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PD-L1 scoring guides whether patients should receive combined chemotherapy and immunotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">combination therapy, treatment planning</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PD-L1 Testing" target="Immunotherapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PD-L1 testing identifies whether patients are candidates for immune checkpoint inhibitor therapies and chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment selection, targeted therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Tumor Mutational Burden-High" target="Tumor Mutation Testing">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Tumor mutation testing assesses genomic aberrations and base-pair mutation rates to determine whether a cancer is classified as TMB-H.</data>
      <data key="d6">genomic testing, biomarker qualification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="American Joint Committee On Cancer" target="TNM Staging System">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The American Joint Committee on Cancer created the cancer staging system that incorporates TNM scores to determine cancer extent and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;The American Joint Committee on Cancer developed a staging framework that utilizes TNM scores to determine cancer progression.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, medical standards&lt;SEP&gt;staging standard, cancer classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="American Joint Committee On Cancer" target="Throat Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The AJCC staging system is used to evaluate, stage, and determine the prognosis of throat cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, clinical evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM Staging System" target="Clinical Stage">
      <data key="d4">24.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clinical stage is the pre-treatment categorization derived using the TNM staging framework.&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM staging system includes clinical staging as an initial pre-treatment assessment category.&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM staging system is used to formulate a clinical stage based on pretreatment diagnostic findings.</data>
      <data key="d6">classification, diagnostic assessment&lt;SEP&gt;medical classification, diagnostic staging&lt;SEP&gt;staging evaluation, cancer assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM Staging System" target="Pathologic Stage">
      <data key="d4">32.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pathologic stage is determined post-surgery using the TNM framework based on excised tissue examination.&lt;SEP&gt;Pathologic staging integrates TNM assessment criteria based on tissue removed during surgical procedures.&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM staging system includes pathologic staging as a post-surgical evaluation category.&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM staging system provides the criteria used to determine the pathologic stage following surgery.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, diagnostic framework&lt;SEP&gt;staging evaluation, cancer assessment&lt;SEP&gt;surgical pathology, staging classification&lt;SEP&gt;surgical staging, pathology assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM Staging System" target="Anatomic Staging">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The TNM system directly defines anatomic staging through measurements of tumor size, lymph node involvement, and distant spread.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, diagnostic metrics</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM Staging System" target="Prognostic Staging">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Prognostic staging incorporates TNM scores alongside expected standard-of-care treatment outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease prognosis, staging criteria</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM Staging System" target="Numbered Cancer Stages">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Numbered stages ranging from Stage 0 to Stage 4 are derived directly from TNM scores.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease severity, staging system</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM Staging System" target="Locoregional Disease">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">TNM criteria define locoregional progression based on tumor invasion into surrounding tissues and regional lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease progression, staging criteria</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM Staging System" target="Metastatic Disease">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Metastatic disease corresponds to the 'M' category within the TNM system indicating distant organ spread.</data>
      <data key="d6">metastasis, tumor progression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM Staging System" target="Squamous Cell Carcinoma Of The Mouth">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Squamous cell carcinoma of the mouth is classified and evaluated using TNM staging, assessing tumor size and depth of invasion.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, clinical evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM Staging System" target="Throat Cancer">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">TNM scores form the core anatomic basis for evaluating the stage and prognosis of throat cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM system is used to evaluate and assign stages to throat cancer based on tumor size, node spread, and metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, diagnostic scoring&lt;SEP&gt;clinical staging, diagnostic classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM Staging System" target="Surgery">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cancer staging is often conducted after surgery to obtain detailed and accurate TNM measurements regarding tumor size and lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathologic evaluation, post-surgical staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM Staging System" target="Imaging Tests">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Imaging tests provide internal visual data necessary to determine the tumor size (T), regional nodal spread (N), and distant metastasis (M).</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical staging, diagnostic imaging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM Staging System" target="Physical Exam">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Physical examination findings directly contribute to establishing clinical stage (c) prior to cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical assessment, staging input</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM Staging System" target="Tumor Size (T)">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Tumor size (T) is one of the three foundational anatomical components evaluated in the TNM Staging System.</data>
      <data key="d6">staging component, diagnostic criteria</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM Staging System" target="Lymph Node Status (N)">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lymph node status (N) serves as a primary metric within the TNM Staging System to assess regional cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d6">staging component, disease progression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM Staging System" target="Distant Metastasis (M)">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Distant metastasis (M) is the TNM parameter that identifies systemic spread beyond the primary site and regional lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">staging component, metastatic assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM Staging System" target="Prognostic Stage">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Prognostic staging incorporates anatomic TNM scores alongside tumor grade and biomarker results.</data>
      <data key="d6">classification integration, prognostic assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM Staging System" target="Doctors">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Doctors interpret diagnostic test results to assign specific scores and stages within the TNM system.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical evaluation, clinical decision-making</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM Staging System" target="Ductal Carcinoma In Situ">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The TNM staging system classifies ductal carcinoma in situ as stage 0 (Tis, N0, M0).</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, diagnostic criteria</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM Staging System" target="Invasive Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The TNM system is used to determine whether invasive breast cancer is stage 1, 2, or 3 based on tumor, node, and metastasis scores.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease staging, cancer assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM Staging System" target="Metastatic Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The TNM system identifies stage 4 metastatic breast cancer when cancer cells spread to distant anatomical sites.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease progression, staging framework</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM Staging System" target="American Joint Committee on Cancer">
      <data key="d4">27.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The American Joint Committee on Cancer developed cancer staging systems that define cancer extent using the TNM framework.&lt;SEP&gt;The American Joint Committee on Cancer developed the TNM system to classify and stage colon cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The American Joint Committee on Cancer developed the TNM system used to determine the stage of anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">development, cancer classification&lt;SEP&gt;medical guidelines, staging framework&lt;SEP&gt;staging framework, oncology standards</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM Staging System" target="Stage 0 Colon Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The TNM system classifies early abnormal colon wall cell growth as Stage 0.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, clinical staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM Staging System" target="Stage 1 Colon Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The TNM system defines Stage 1 based on depth of tumor invasion without lymph node spread.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, clinical staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM Staging System" target="Stage 2 Colon Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The TNM system defines Stage 2 by deeper invasion into or through the colon wall without lymph node spread.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, clinical staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM Staging System" target="Stage 3 Colon Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The TNM system defines Stage 3 by the presence of regional lymph node involvement or tumor deposits.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, clinical staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM Staging System" target="Stage 4 Colon Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The TNM system defines Stage 4 by the presence of distant metastases to organs such as the liver or lungs.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, clinical staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM Staging System" target="Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The TNM staging system provides specific criteria for categorizing the extent and spread of nasopharyngeal carcinoma tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM system is utilized to stage nasopharyngeal carcinoma and calculate overall prognostic stages.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, clinical classification&lt;SEP&gt;disease classification, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM Staging System" target="Squamous Cell Carcinoma Of The Nasopharynx">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The TNM staging system specifies classification criteria for squamous cell carcinoma of the nasopharynx.</data>
      <data key="d6">staging framework, tumor classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM Staging System" target="Squamous Cell Carcinoma of the Nasopharynx">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The TNM staging system provides structured diagnostic criteria specifically for squamous cell carcinoma of the nasopharynx.</data>
      <data key="d6">oncologic staging, clinical evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM Staging System" target="Bone Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The TNM system is used to evaluate and stage most bone cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, clinical evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM Staging System" target="Anal Squamous Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The TNM system is utilized to determine the stage, extent, and treatment plan for anal squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, clinical evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM Staging System" target="Inguinal Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The N component of the TNM staging system evaluates whether nearby lymph nodes, such as inguinal nodes, contain cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">staging criteria, nodal involvement</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Clinical Stage" target="Imaging Tests">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Imaging test results are critical components used prior to treatment to establish clinical staging.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging tests provide diagnostic visualization of tumor and lymph nodes to establish clinical stage.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic imaging, staging assessment&lt;SEP&gt;radiology, diagnostic staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Clinical Stage" target="Physical Exam">
      <data key="d4">24.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A physical exam is one of the primary diagnostic procedures used to determine clinical staging.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical examination provides initial clinical observation data required to formulate a patient's clinical stage.&lt;SEP&gt;Physical examinations are part of the pre-treatment evaluations used to determine clinical cancer stage.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical evaluation, diagnostic staging&lt;SEP&gt;diagnostic evaluation, staging assessment&lt;SEP&gt;pre-treatment assessment, clinical staging</data>
