    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Grade" target="Pathologist">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A pathologist evaluates cancer tissue under a microscope to assign the cancer grade.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist examines cancer cells to determine their grade by comparing them to normal cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathological evaluation, grading&lt;SEP&gt;pathology analysis, cancer grading</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Grade" target="TNM System">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cancer grade complements the TNM system to comprehensively describe cancer aggressiveness and determine stage.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, histology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Grade" target="Cancer Staging">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cancer grade (G) is combined with tumor (T), node (N), and metastasis (M) scores to calculate numbered cancer stages.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical evaluation, staging factors</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Grade" target="Histology">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cancer grade is defined through the histology of tumor cells viewed under a microscope.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic evaluation, cellular morphology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Grade" target="GX Grade">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">GX represents the specific grade category when tumor differentiation cannot be determined.</data>
      <data key="d6">classification subcategory, staging system</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Grade" target="G1 Grade">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">G1 is the well-differentiated, low-grade category in the histological grading system.</data>
      <data key="d6">classification subcategory, staging system</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Grade" target="G2 Grade">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">G2 is the moderately differentiated, high-grade category in cancer grading.</data>
      <data key="d6">classification subcategory, staging system</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Grade" target="G3 Grade">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">G3 is the poorly differentiated, high-grade category representing highly abnormal cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">classification subcategory, staging system</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Grade" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Bladder cancer aggressiveness is evaluated through cancer grade, distinguishing low-grade from high-grade tumors.</data>
      <data key="d6">prognosis, tumor classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Grade" target="Low Grade">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Low grade (LG) represents one of the primary cancer grading levels indicating slow cell growth.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathology grading, tumor classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Grade" target="High Grade">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">High grade (HG) is a grading level representing rapidly growing and aggressive cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathology grading, tumor classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Grade" target="Perianal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cancer grade is a determining factor for whether local excision is suitable for treating perianal cancer, being limited to low- and intermediate-grade tumors.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical assessment, staging and grading</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Stage" target="Surgery">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Surgery is necessary to determine the precise extent of cancer spread and establish the cancer stage.</data>
      <data key="d6">staging, diagnostic surgery</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Stage" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Bladder cancer treatment and prognosis depend heavily on cancer staging, especially whether it has invaded the muscle layer.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease staging, treatment planning</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Stage" target="Stage 0 Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 0 describes the earliest non-invasive cancer staging within the bladder lining.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, non-muscle-invasive</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Stage" target="Stage 1 Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 1 defines a stage of bladder cancer that has reached connective tissue but not muscle.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, tissue invasion</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Stage" target="Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cancer staging categorizes stages 2, 3, and 4 as muscle-invasive bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, muscle invasion</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Social Workers" target="Care Team">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Social workers function as members of the multidisciplinary care team to address patient anxiety and practical concerns.</data>
      <data key="d6">multidisciplinary care, patient support</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Pap Test">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The Pap test is specifically used to detect cervical cancer or pre-cancer rather than ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease detection, cancer screening</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Cervical Intraepithelial Neoplasia">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cervical intraepithelial neoplasia represents pre-invasive dysplastic changes that can progress into cervical cancer if left untreated.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease progression, premalignant lesion</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="High-Grade Squamous Intraepithelial Lesion">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">High-grade squamous intraepithelial lesions are considered pre-cancers that can evolve into invasive cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">malignant transformation, pre-cancerous state</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Uterine Cancer">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Although the cervix is part of the uterus, cervical cancer and uterine cancer represent distinct diagnostic and therapeutic entities.</data>
      <data key="d6">oncologic differentiation, anatomical relation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Human Papillomavirus">
      <data key="d4">19.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Human Papillomavirus is the underlying cause of cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Long-term infection with human papillomavirus causes the vast majority of cervical cancer cases.</data>
      <data key="d6">etiology, viral oncogenesis&lt;SEP&gt;viral etiology, oncogenesis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Pap Smear">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Regular Pap smear screenings allow for the early detection and prevention of cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">preventative screening, cancer prevention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Human Papillomavirus (HPV)">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Long-term infection with high-risk strains of HPV is the primary cause of almost all cervical cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease etiology, viral carcinogenesis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Cervical Dysplasia">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cervical dysplasia represents the precursor cellular changes that can progress into cervical cancer if left untreated.</data>
      <data key="d6">pre-cancerous development, disease progression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Neuroendocrine Carcinoma of the Cervix (NECC)">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NECC is recognized as the rarest and most aggressive form of cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, aggressive malignancy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV)">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">HIV-induced immunosuppression is a major risk factor contributing to the development of cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">risk factor, immunosuppression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="AIDS">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">AIDS weakens the immune system, significantly increasing the likelihood of developing cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">risk factor, immunosuppression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Cervical Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">27.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A cervical biopsy is the primary procedure performed to obtain tissue to confirm or diagnose cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A cervical biopsy is the primary procedure used to diagnose cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical biopsy is the primary diagnostic method used to confirm cervical cancer and detect dysplasia.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer diagnosis, clinical procedure&lt;SEP&gt;diagnosis, medical testing&lt;SEP&gt;diagnostic testing, cancer detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Transformation Zone">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The transformation zone of the cervix is the primary anatomical origin site for most cervical cancers and pre-cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical localization, cancer origin</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Glassy-Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Glassy-cell carcinoma is recognized as an uncommon histological type of cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">histological variant, oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Sarcoma">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Sarcoma represents an atypical non-epithelial tumor that can develop in the cervix.</data>
      <data key="d6">tumor classification, oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Cone Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cone biopsy is used to assess cancer extent and can serve as a curative treatment for early-stage cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment option, cancer assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Pelvic Examination">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pelvic exams are performed during physical evaluations to check for signs, position, and appearance of cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">physical examination, clinical staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Computed Tomography">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CT scans are used to determine the size and spread of cervical cancer to guide treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic imaging, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Positron Emission Tomography">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PET scans use radioactive sugar tracers to identify active cervical cancer cells and spread.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer detection, staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Symptoms Of Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Symptoms such as watery vaginal discharge are evaluated by physicians to diagnose cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical signs, symptomatic presentation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Cancer Staging">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cancer staging determines the anatomical extent and spread of cervical cancer based on diagnostic imaging and examinations.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease staging, clinical oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="FIGO Staging System">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The FIGO system is specifically used to classify the stage, extent, and spread of cervical cancer to guide treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, clinical evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Cancer Care Plan">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The cancer care plan is formulated to outline treatment modalities and goals specifically for a patient's cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment planning, disease management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="NCCN">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN publishes patient guides and clinical recommendations addressing the diagnosis, staging, and treatment of cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical guidelines, cancer resources</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="FIGO">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The FIGO system is specifically used to stage the extent of cervical cancer before treatment is initiated.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, clinical evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Surgery">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Surgery is often the main treatment modality utilized for early-stage cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgery, such as hysterectomy or trachelectomy, is commonly recommended for early-stage cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer treatment, therapeutic intervention&lt;SEP&gt;surgical oncology, treatment modality</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Diagnostic Imaging">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Diagnostic imaging modalities such as CT, MRI, PET, and ultrasound are used to stage and evaluate the extent of cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, radiological evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Cystoscopy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cystoscopy is performed under anesthesia to detect whether cervical cancer has spread into the bladder.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease assessment, organ invasion</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Proctoscopy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Proctoscopy is used to determine whether cervical cancer has infiltrated the bowel.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease assessment, organ invasion</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Fertility-Sparing Treatment">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Fertility-sparing treatment is considered for cervical cancer confined strictly to the cervix.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical indication, organ preservation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Systemic Therapy">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Systemic therapy delivers medication to treat cervical cancer throughout the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic therapy is used to treat cervical cancer throughout the body, particularly for recurrent or metastatic cases.</data>
      <data key="d6">oncological treatment, systemic management&lt;SEP&gt;pharmacotherapy, systemic treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Clinical Trials">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clinical trials offer investigative treatment modalities for patients managing cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical research, novel therapeutics</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Laparoscopic Surgical Staging">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Laparoscopic surgical staging is performed to evaluate lymph node involvement and guide therapy decisions when cervical cancer spreads beyond the cervix.</data>
      <data key="d6">pre-treatment staging, clinical assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Hysterectomy">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hysterectomy is considered for cervical cancer treatment, but omitted if cancer has extended beyond the cervix.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical treatment, clinical indication</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Pelvic Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pelvic lymph nodes are the nearest lymph nodes to the cervix and are typically the first site to which cervical cancer cells spread.</data>
      <data key="d6">metastatic pathway, lymphatic dissemination</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Para-Aortic Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Para-aortic lymph nodes in the abdomen can be invaded by spreading cervical cancer and are tested to determine the extent of disease.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease extent, lymphatic spread</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Locally Advanced Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Locally advanced cervical cancer is a clinical stage of cervical cancer where cancer has spread locally beyond the cervix.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease staging, clinical subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Recurrent Or Metastatic Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Recurrent or metastatic cervical cancer represents an advanced or returning form of cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease progression, oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Early-Stage Cancer">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Early-stage cancer describes an initial stage categorization of cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Early-stage cancer represents localized forms of cervical cancer confined to the cervix or upper vagina.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease staging, cancer classification&lt;SEP&gt;staging classification, clinical management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Locally Advanced Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Locally advanced cancer describes a stage category of cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease staging, cancer classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Physical Exams">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Physical examinations are an essential component of post-treatment surveillance for cervical cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer monitoring, surveillance schedule</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Imaging">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Imaging modalities like CT, MRI, and FDG-PET/CT are used across different stages of cervical cancer to identify recurrence or monitor response to therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic imaging, disease monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Pap Tests">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pap screening tests are administered annually after cervical cancer treatment to detect abnormal or precancerous cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">screening, secondary prevention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Blood Tests">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Blood tests are utilized during cervical cancer follow-up to check organ function and investigate potential recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d6">laboratory diagnostics, patient assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biomarker testing is recommended for recurrent, progressive, or metastatic cervical cancer to inform targeted therapy options.</data>
      <data key="d6">precision medicine, targeted therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Pelvic Recurrence">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pelvic recurrence refers to cervical cancer returning locally to the pelvic region after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer recurrence, disease progression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Small Cell Neuroendocrine Carcinoma Of The Cervix">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Small cell neuroendocrine carcinoma of the cervix is a specific variant of cervical cancer mentioned in surveillance guidelines.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, subtypes</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Human Papillomavirus Infection">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cervical cancer is one of the principal malignant diseases caused by human papillomavirus infection.</data>
      <data key="d6">viral etiology, oncogenesis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A patient history of cervical cancer or precancer is associated with a higher risk of developing anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">shared risk factor, cancer susceptibility</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="HPV">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">HPV infection is responsible for causing almost all cases of cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">causality, viral etiology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Uterine Cancer" target="Endometrial Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">An endometrial biopsy is performed to rule out uterine cancer during evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d6">differential diagnosis, disease exclusion</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Uterine Cancer" target="Germline Mutations">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mutations in genes such as MSH2, MSH6, MLH1, and PMS2 are linked to uterine cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer predisposition, shared genetics</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Uterine Cancer" target="MSH2">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Germline mutations in MSH2 increase susceptibility to uterine cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">mismatch repair, hereditary risk</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Uterine Cancer" target="Endometrial Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Endometrial cancer is categorized as a specific form of uterine cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease subtype, gynecologic oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Uterine Cancer" target="Uterine Sarcomas">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Uterine sarcomas are recognized as a form of uterine cancer distinct in treatment and diagnosis from cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease subtype, gynecologic oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Abdominal Exam" target="Healthcare Provider">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The healthcare provider palpates the patient's abdomen to examine organ texture, size, tenderness, and fluid accumulation.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical evaluation, examination execution</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Abdominal Exam" target="Ascites">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The abdominal exam is specifically used by the doctor to feel for signs of fluid buildup or ascites in the belly.</data>
      <data key="d6">physical findings, symptom detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pelvic Exam" target="Pap Test">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A Pap test can be conducted during a pelvic exam using a speculum to collect cell samples.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedure component, diagnostic screening</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pelvic Exam" target="Endometrial Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">An endometrial biopsy may be integrated into the pelvic exam during the initial patient evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedure component, diagnostic testing</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pelvic Exam" target="Rectovaginal Exam">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The rectovaginal exam complements the pelvic exam to check for abnormal masses between the rectum and vagina.</data>
      <data key="d6">examination procedure, clinical evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pelvic Exam" target="Healthcare Provider">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The healthcare provider performs the pelvic examination using instruments like a speculum to inspect reproductive organs.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical evaluation, patient examination</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pap Test" target="Gynecologic Exam">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A gynecologic exam recommended for anal cancer patients includes cervical cancer screening via a Pap test.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical evaluation, disease screening</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Clinical Staging" target="Surgery">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clinical staging provides an initial estimate of cancer spread to help decide whether surgery first is feasible or appropriate.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment planning, staging protocol</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Clinical Staging" target="Imaging Tests">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Findings from imaging tests inform the clinical stage to estimate cancer progression prior to treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">pre-treatment evaluation, staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Genetic Counselor" target="BRCA Mutation">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Genetic counselors assess patients with positive genetic tests or family history to explain BRCA mutations and guide medical decisions.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic counseling, clinical guidance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Genetic Counselor" target="Hereditary Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Genetic counselors assist individuals suspected of having or diagnosed with hereditary ovarian cancer in evaluating genetic risks.</data>
      <data key="d6">risk assessment, patient counseling</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Genetic Counselor" target="Doctor">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Doctors can consult with patients regarding genetic testing or refer them to genetic counselors for expert evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical referral, genetic consultation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Genetic Counselor" target="Genetic Germline Testing">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Genetic counselors assist individuals in deciding whether to undergo germline testing and help interpret the test results.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic counseling, testing guidance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Genetic Counselor" target="Hereditary Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Genetic counselors evaluate risk factors and provide guidance concerning hereditary breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic counseling, risk assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Genetic Counselor" target="Inherited Cancer Syndromes">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Patients suspected of having inherited cancer syndromes are referred to a genetic counselor for risk assessment and testing guidance.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical consultation, patient referral</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Genetic Counselor" target="Pathologist">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Genetic counselors guide patients through testing protocols while pathologists analyze blood and tissue samples for diagnostic mutations.</data>
      <data key="d6">multidisciplinary collaboration, diagnostic testing</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Genetic Counselor" target="Multidisciplinary Care Team">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Genetic counselors inform the care team how familial and hereditary factors influence treatment strategy.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic assessment, personalized care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Genetic Counselor" target="Germline Testing">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A genetic counselor assists patients in deciding whether to undergo germline testing and helps interpret the results.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic counseling, testing guidance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Genetic Counselor" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Genetic counselors work with bladder cancer patients who have risk factors like early onset or family history.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient guidance, oncology genetics</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Genetic Counselor" target="FindAGeneticCounselor.com">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">FindAGeneticCounselor.com is the recommended online platform for patients seeking to locate a genetic counselor.</data>
      <data key="d6">referral directory, specialist access</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hereditary Ovarian Cancer" target="BRCA Mutation">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mutations in BRCA1 and BRCA2 are the most common cause of hereditary ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic risk, causality</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BRCA Mutation" target="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">BRCA mutation analysis is the primary component of biomarker testing for ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">molecular testing, genetic biomarkers</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BRCA Mutation" target="Homologous Recombination Deficiency (HRD)">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A BRCA mutation is a specific form of homologous recombination deficiency that renders the cancer HRD-positive.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic classification, molecular biology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BRCA Mutation" target="Hereditary Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">BRCA gene mutations play a well-established role in the development and predisposition to hereditary cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">hereditary risk, genetic predisposition</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BRCA Mutation" target="PARP Inhibitors">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cancers bearing a BRCA mutation show heightened therapeutic sensitivity to PARP inhibitors.</data>
      <data key="d6">biomarker response, targeted therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BRCA Mutation" target="PARP Inhibitor">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Maintenance therapy with a PARP inhibitor after recurrence chemotherapy is indicated when a BRCA mutation is present.</data>
      <data key="d6">biomarker-guided therapy, genetic indication</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Diagnostic Laparoscopy" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Findings from diagnostic laparoscopy help physicians determine whether upfront surgery is appropriate or if chemotherapy must be initiated first.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment staging, therapy decision</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Sedative" target="Care Team">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The care team evaluates patient claustrophobia and administers sedatives to help nervous patients relax during enclosed procedures.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient support, medication administration</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Fine-Needle Aspiration Biopsy" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Fine-needle aspiration is a specific type of needle biopsy procedure used to obtain diagnostic tissue.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedural subtype, diagnostic method</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Fine-Needle Aspiration Biopsy" target="Pathologist">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The tissue sample harvested via fine-needle aspiration is sent directly to a pathologist for microscopic evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d6">laboratory analysis, specimen examination</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Core Biopsy" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Core biopsy is a specific needle-based biopsy technique used to retrieve tissue cores for pathology assessment.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedural subtype, tissue extraction</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Core Biopsy" target="Pathologist">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Core biopsy tissue samples are examined by a pathologist to identify malignant cells and histological markers.</data>
      <data key="d6">laboratory examination, pathological verification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Paracentesis" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Paracentesis serves as a fluid sampling biopsy method when cancer has spread within the abdomen.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedural subtype, fluid sampling</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Paracentesis" target="Pathologist">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Fluid samples extracted through paracentesis are reviewed by a pathologist to detect malignant cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">cytological analysis, fluid examination</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Maintenance Therapy" target="Homologous Recombination Deficiency (HRD)">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">HRD status in tumors informs clinical decisions regarding the administration of maintenance therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">targeted treatment, clinical decision-making</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Maintenance Therapy" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">24.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Maintenance therapy is administered sequentially following successful response to initial platinum-based chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Maintenance therapy may be offered for advanced cancers following successful response to chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Maintenance therapy represents the next sequential therapeutic step following successful response to primary chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment continuum, subsequent therapy&lt;SEP&gt;treatment protocol, disease management&lt;SEP&gt;treatment sequence, ongoing care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Maintenance Therapy" target="PARP Inhibitors">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PARP inhibitors are targeted therapeutic agents commonly prescribed as oral maintenance therapy for ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">targeted therapy, maintenance protocol</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Maintenance Therapy" target="Bevacizumab">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Bevacizumab can be continued alone as maintenance therapy following initial chemo-combination treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Bevacizumab is utilized as a maintenance therapy alone or in combination with PARP inhibitors following chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">maintenance protocol, targeted treatment&lt;SEP&gt;maintenance regimen, targeted therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Maintenance Therapy" target="Observation">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Observation is considered as an alternative approach to pharmacological maintenance therapy in selected responding patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment option, clinical observation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Maintenance Therapy" target="PARP Inhibitor">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PARP inhibitors are approved and administered as maintenance therapy options to prolong remission.</data>
      <data key="d6">maintenance treatment, clinical drug class</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Maintenance Therapy" target="Intravesical BCG">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Maintenance therapy involves continuing intravesical BCG instillation on a regular schedule for up to three years to prevent recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d6">long-term management, preventive regimen</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Maintenance Therapy" target="Intravesical Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Maintenance intravesical chemotherapy may be administered monthly for one to two years to prevent cancer return.</data>
      <data key="d6">long-term management, recurrence prevention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Homologous Recombination Deficiency (HRD)" target="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biomarker testing assesses tumor DNA for homologous recombination deficiency to identify candidates for targeted therapies.</data>
      <data key="d6">molecular profiling, targeted therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Tumor Markers" target="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Tumor markers represent a specific category of protein-based biomarker testing used in monitoring cancer progression.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic classification, protein detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Tumor Markers" target="Carcinoembryonic Antigen">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA) is an example of a protein tumor marker evaluated in breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">protein marker, clinical monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Tumor Markers" target="CA 15-3">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CA 15-3 is an established tumor marker used to monitor breast cancer growth.</data>
      <data key="d6">protein marker, disease progression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Gastrointestinal Tract Examination" target="Colonoscopy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Colonoscopy is a specific type of gastrointestinal tract examination used to view the lower bowel.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic procedure, lower endoscopy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Gastrointestinal Tract Examination" target="Upper Endoscopy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Upper endoscopy is a specific gastrointestinal endoscopic exam used to visualize the esophagus, stomach, and small bowel.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic procedure, upper endoscopy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Initial Surgery" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biopsies must precede chemotherapy if chemotherapy is designated to occur before initial surgery.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment sequencing, clinical protocol</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colonoscopy" target="Polypectomy">
      <data key="d4">44.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A polypectomy is a surgical procedure performed during a colonoscopy to extract polyps.&lt;SEP&gt;A polypectomy is carried out during a colonoscopy to safely remove polyps and prevent malignancy.&lt;SEP&gt;During a colonoscopy, polyps are frequently identified and surgically excised via a polypectomy.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypectomies are minor surgical interventions typically performed during a colonoscopy to remove polyps.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypectomy is performed during a colonoscopy to remove polyps before they develop into colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical procedure, polyp removal&lt;SEP&gt;medical procedure, screening intervention&lt;SEP&gt;preventive procedure, clinical intervention&lt;SEP&gt;screening procedure, surgical excision</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colonoscopy" target="Hyperplastic Polyp">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hyperplastic polyps can be identified and evaluated during a colonoscopy.</data>
      <data key="d6">examination, polyp detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colonoscopy" target="Inflammatory Polyp">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Inflammatory polyps are identified and distinguished from precancerous lesions during a colonoscopy.</data>
      <data key="d6">examination, polyp detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colonoscopy" target="Rectal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Colonoscopy is used as a post-treatment surveillance procedure following rectal cancer interventions.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer surveillance, medical monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colonoscopy" target="Colon Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Colonoscopy is a central surveillance tool recommended at 1, 3, and 5-year intervals following surgery for colon cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Colonoscopy is the primary procedure through which most colon cancers and polyps are discovered and evaluated.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnosis, detection&lt;SEP&gt;monitoring protocol, cancer surveillance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colonoscopy" target="Surveillance">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Colonoscopy is a key diagnostic surveillance procedure performed after surgery to assess for recurrent disease.</data>
      <data key="d6">follow-up care, diagnostic surveillance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colonoscopy" target="Stage 1 Colon Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A post-surgical colonoscopy is recommended at 1 year, 3 years, and then every 5 years for stage 1 colon cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">post-operative surveillance, schedule</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colonoscopy" target="Surgery">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A surveillance colonoscopy is scheduled beginning one year after surgical resection for stage 1 colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">post-operative schedule, clinical timeline</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Complete Blood Count (CBC)" target="Blood Tests">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CBC is a core component of the standard baseline blood testing battery in cancer evaluation.&lt;SEP&gt;The complete blood count is a core component of diagnostic blood testing used to evaluate systemic health.</data>
      <data key="d6">hematology, diagnostic testing&lt;SEP&gt;laboratory analysis, baseline workup</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Complete Blood Count (CBC)" target="CLL">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CBC tests provide vital counts of white blood cells and platelets used to detect and monitor CLL.</data>
      <data key="d6">blood analysis, cancer monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Blood Chemistry Profile" target="Blood Tests">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A blood chemistry profile is a specific blood test assessing metabolic and organ health.&lt;SEP&gt;A blood chemistry profile is an essential component of initial blood testing before cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">laboratory test, diagnostic category&lt;SEP&gt;metabolic panel, diagnostic testing</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CA-125 Test" target="Endometriosis">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Endometriosis is a benign condition that can cause elevated CA-125 levels, mimicking cancer markers.</data>
      <data key="d6">differential diagnosis, confounding factor</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CA-125 Test" target="Diverticulitis">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Diverticulitis can cause elevated CA-125 protein levels despite not being a cancerous disease.</data>
      <data key="d6">differential diagnosis, confounding factor</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Recurrent Ovarian Cancer" target="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Expanded biomarker profiling is specifically recommended when ovarian cancer recurs following initial therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">molecular profiling, disease recurrence</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Recurrent Ovarian Cancer" target="Platinum-Resistant Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Platinum-resistant cancer is a specific manifestation of recurrent ovarian cancer characterized by early relapse or lack of response to platinum agents.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, recurrence subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Recurrent Ovarian Cancer" target="Platinum-Sensitive Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Platinum-sensitive cancer is a subtype of recurrent ovarian cancer where disease recurs more than six months after initial platinum therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, recurrence subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Recurrent Ovarian Cancer" target="Clinical Trial">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Patients experiencing persistent or recurrent ovarian cancer are encouraged to consider clinical trials for alternative or novel treatments.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical research, therapy evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Recurrent Ovarian Cancer" target="NCCN">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN provides patient guidelines and resources addressing cancer treatments, side effects, and biomarker-based therapies relevant to recurrent disease.</data>
      <data key="d6">guidelines, patient education</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hysterectomy and Bilateral Salpingo-Oophorectomy (BSO)" target="Fertility-Sparing Surgery">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Fertility-sparing surgery offers an alternative to definitive hysterectomy and BSO for eligible patients desiring future pregnancy.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical alternative, fertility preservation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Fertility-Sparing Surgery" target="Unilateral Salpingo-Oophorectomy (USO)">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Unilateral salpingo-oophorectomy is a specific type of fertility-sparing surgery appropriate for certain early-stage cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical subtype, fertility preservation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Fertility-Sparing Surgery" target="In Vitro Fertilization (IVF)">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Patients undergoing fertility-preserving management or BSO may utilize IVF to achieve pregnancy.</data>
      <data key="d6">reproductive technology, family planning</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cytoreductive Surgery" target="Laparotomy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Laparotomy is the primary surgical access method recommended when cytoreductive debulking surgery is planned.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical method, tumor reduction</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surgical Staging" target="Ovarian Cancer Surgery">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Surgical staging is conducted during ovarian cancer surgery to assess tumor spread through tissue and lymph node removal.</data>
      <data key="d6">intraoperative assessment, staging evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surgical Staging" target="FIGO Staging System">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Data collected during surgical staging directly inform the categorization of ovarian cancer under the FIGO staging system.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathological staging, classification system</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surgical Staging" target="Pathologist">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The pathologist determines the pathologic cancer stage (Stage I, II, or III) based on tumor depth and nodal spread.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, diagnostic classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surgical Staging" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Surgical staging results directly inform whether post-operative chemotherapy or chemoradiation is required.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment planning, adjuvant therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surgical Staging" target="Stage 1 Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 1 is one of the pathologic classifications determined by surgical staging.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathologic diagnosis, staging hierarchy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surgical Staging" target="Stage 2 Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 2 is a pathologic classification assigned via surgical staging based on tumor invasion through the rectal wall.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathologic diagnosis, staging hierarchy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surgical Staging" target="Stage 3 Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 3 is defined during surgical staging when cancer cells are identified in resected lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathologic diagnosis, staging hierarchy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surgical Staging" target="Locally Advanced Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Surgical staging may be performed prior to treatment in locally advanced cervical cancer to assess lymph node involvement.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic procedure, disease management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Laparotomy" target="Surgery">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Laparotomy is the open abdominal approach employed to perform major cervical cancer surgeries.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical technique, operative approach</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BRCA Genes" target="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
