      <data key="d6">mutation analysis, diagnostic molecular testing</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Microsatellite Instability (MSI)" target="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biomarker testing assesses tumor tissue for microsatellite instability to inform therapy options.&lt;SEP&gt;MSI is evaluated during biomarker testing to direct targeted treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d6">biomarker assessment, molecular analysis&lt;SEP&gt;molecular assessment, diagnostic profiling</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mismatch Repair (MMR)" target="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MMR status is assessed as part of comprehensive molecular profiling in recurrent cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Mismatch repair status is analyzed during tumor biomarker testing.</data>
      <data key="d6">genomic profiling, biomarker evaluation&lt;SEP&gt;pathology evaluation, molecular profiling</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HER2 Expression" target="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">HER2 expression is one of the features tested on tumor tissue during biomarker testing.</data>
      <data key="d6">target identification, biomarker evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Tumor Mutational Burden (TMB)" target="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">TMB is evaluated through genomic testing to identify potential targeted therapeutic options.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor mutational burden is evaluated as part of tumor tissue biomarker analysis.</data>
      <data key="d6">genomic profiling, therapy selection&lt;SEP&gt;genomic sequencing, predictive biomarkers</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BRAF V600E Mutation" target="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biomarker analysis includes screening tumor tissue for the BRAF V600E mutation.</data>
      <data key="d6">mutation analysis, targeted oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Folate Receptor Alpha (FRα) Expression" target="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biomarker testing assesses folate receptor alpha expression on removed ovarian tumor tissue.</data>
      <data key="d6">receptor profiling, therapeutic target</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RET Mutations" target="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Tumor biomarker testing includes identification of RET mutations.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic screening, precision medicine</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NTRK Gene Fusion" target="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NTRK gene fusion testing is performed, particularly when cervical sarcoma is diagnosed.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor tissue is analyzed for NTRK gene fusions to guide targeted therapy decisions.</data>
      <data key="d6">gene fusion analysis, precision oncology&lt;SEP&gt;genomic alteration, precision medicine</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NTRK Gene Fusion" target="Cervical Sarcoma">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cervical sarcoma patients are evaluated for NTRK gene fusions to guide targeted therapies.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease-specific markers, molecular oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="American Joint Committee on Cancer" target="AJCC Staging System">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The American Joint Committee on Cancer created a staging system for evaluating ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">system development, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="American Joint Committee on Cancer" target="Breast Cancer Staging">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The American Joint Committee on Cancer created the cancer staging system used to determine cancer extent and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical standards, cancer classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="American Joint Committee on Cancer" target="Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The American Joint Committee on Cancer provides the standard staging system used to classify nasopharyngeal carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical standards, cancer guidelines</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics" target="FIGO Staging System">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics developed the FIGO staging system widely used to classify ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics developed the system used to stage cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">system development, clinical standards&lt;SEP&gt;system development, medical standards</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FIGO Staging System" target="Stage 1 Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 1 is an initial stage category defined within the FIGO staging system.</data>
      <data key="d6">classification hierarchy, disease staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FIGO Staging System" target="Stage 2 Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 2 is a stage category within the FIGO staging framework indicating localized extension.</data>
      <data key="d6">classification hierarchy, disease staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FIGO Staging System" target="Stage 3 Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 3 is a stage classification within the FIGO system indicating deeper pelvic and nodal spread.</data>
      <data key="d6">classification hierarchy, disease staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FIGO Staging System" target="Stage 4 Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 4 represents the most advanced category of disease within the FIGO staging system.</data>
      <data key="d6">classification hierarchy, disease staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surgical Menopause" target="Ovarian Cancer Surgery">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Bilateral ovary removal during ovarian cancer surgery directly triggers premature surgical menopause.</data>
      <data key="d6">postoperative complication, hormonal effect</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surgical Menopause" target="Menopausal Symptom Team">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Patients experiencing surgical menopause are recommended to consult the menopausal symptom team for management strategies.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient consultation, specialized care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surgical Menopause" target="Cardiovascular Disease">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Estrogen depletion following surgical menopause increases the long-term risk of developing cardiovascular disease.</data>
      <data key="d6">health risk, hormonal deficiency</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surgical Menopause" target="Osteoporosis">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lack of estrogen from surgical menopause accelerates bone loss, leading to osteoporosis.</data>
      <data key="d6">health risk, hormonal deficiency</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surgical Menopause" target="Vaginal Atrophy">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Surgical menopause induces rapid declines in estrogen, causing vaginal atrophy.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgical menopause induces vaginal atrophy due to the abrupt reduction in systemic estrogen levels.</data>
      <data key="d6">hormonal symptom, clinical complication&lt;SEP&gt;symptom manifestation, hormonal deficiency</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surgical Menopause" target="Hormone Replacement Therapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hormone replacement therapy is considered as a therapeutic treatment to mitigate symptoms of surgical menopause.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical treatment, symptom relief</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surgical Menopause" target="Hysterectomy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Removing both ovaries during a hysterectomy causes surgical menopause due to an abrupt drop in estrogen levels.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical complications, hormonal effect</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Deep Vein Thrombosis" target="Ovarian Cancer Surgery">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Undergoing abdominal surgery for ovarian cancer increases the patient's risk of developing deep vein thrombosis.</data>
      <data key="d6">postoperative complication, medical risk</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Ovarian Cancer Surgery" target="Peritoneal Washing">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Peritoneal washing is performed as a diagnostic step during surgery to inspect for microscopic metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d6">intraoperative procedure, diagnostic sampling</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Ovarian Cancer Surgery" target="Interval Cytoreductive Surgery">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Interval cytoreductive surgery is a specific form of ovarian cancer surgery scheduled after initial cycles of neoadjuvant chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical subtype, treatment protocol</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Interval Cytoreductive Surgery" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy is administered prior to interval cytoreductive surgery to shrink tumors when immediate surgery is not viable.</data>
      <data key="d6">neoadjuvant therapy, sequential treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Interval Cytoreductive Surgery" target="Hyperthermic Intraperitoneal Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hyperthermic intraperitoneal chemotherapy can be delivered directly into the abdomen during interval cytoreductive surgery for stage 3 disease.</data>
      <data key="d6">intraoperative procedure, combined modality</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Peritoneal Washing" target="Microscopic Metastases">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Peritoneal washing samples abdominal fluid to inspect for microscopic metastases that are invisible to the naked eye.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic testing, cancer detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Menopausal Symptom Team" target="Hormone Replacement Therapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The menopausal symptom team evaluates patients experiencing surgical menopause to determine if hormone replacement therapy is appropriate.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical consultation, symptom management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cardiovascular Disease" target="Premature Menopause">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lack of estrogen resulting from premature menopause increases long-term risks of cardiovascular disease.</data>
      <data key="d6">systemic health risk, hormonal decline</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Osteoporosis" target="Premature Menopause">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Premature menopause and estrogen deprivation are associated with accelerated bone loss and osteoporosis.</data>
      <data key="d6">endocrine deficiency, long-term complications</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Vaginal Atrophy" target="Hysterectomy">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Vaginal atrophy is associated with loss of estrogen following surgeries such as hysterectomies where ovaries are removed.</data>
      <data key="d6">estrogen deficiency, surgical consequence</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hormone Replacement Therapy" target="Endocrine Therapy">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Endocrine therapy for cancer is explicitly distinguished from hormone replacement therapy used for menopause.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical distinction, hormonal treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hormone Replacement Therapy" target="Menopause">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hormone replacement therapy is specifically indicated to manage menopause.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment indication, reproductive biology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FIGO" target="Diagnostic Imaging">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Imaging findings inform the anatomical evaluation required for clinical staging under the FIGO system.</data>
      <data key="d6">staging correlation, clinical evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 1 Ovarian Cancer" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy is recommended after surgery for most patients diagnosed with Stage 1 ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical protocol, adjuvant treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 1 Ovarian Cancer" target="Stage 1C Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 1C is a specific advanced substage within the Stage 1 ovarian cancer category.</data>
      <data key="d6">subclassification, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 2 Ovarian Cancer" target="PARP Inhibitor">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PARP inhibitors have limited clinical research regarding their use as maintenance therapy following initial treatment for stage 2 ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment limitation, maintenance therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 4 Ovarian Cancer" target="Stage 4A Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 4A represents a pleural-involvement substage of metastatic Stage 4 ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">subclassification, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 4 Ovarian Cancer" target="Stage 4B Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 4B represents extra-abdominal and visceral organ metastasis within Stage 4 ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">subclassification, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Taxanes" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Taxanes are a specific drug category commonly administered as part of combination chemotherapy regimens.&lt;SEP&gt;Taxanes represent a specific drug family within chemotherapy treatments.</data>
      <data key="d6">drug classification, medical treatment&lt;SEP&gt;drug classification, pharmacology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Taxanes" target="Paclitaxel">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Paclitaxel is categorized as a taxane medication.</data>
      <data key="d6">classification, pharmacology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Taxanes" target="Docetaxel">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Docetaxel is a taxane class chemotherapy agent.</data>
      <data key="d6">classification, pharmacology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Paclitaxel" target="Peripheral Neuropathy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Paclitaxel administration frequently induces peripheral neuropathy as a prominent side effect.</data>
      <data key="d6">adverse effect, drug toxicity</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bevacizumab" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Bevacizumab is an anti-angiogenic agent that may be combined with standard chemotherapy regimens for advanced ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Bevacizumab may be combined with chemotherapy to prevent tumor blood vessel formation.</data>
      <data key="d6">adjuvant drug, combination regimen&lt;SEP&gt;combination therapy, anti-angiogenesis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bevacizumab" target="Olaparib">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Bevacizumab is combined with olaparib as maintenance therapy for patients with HRD-positive and BRCA-mutated cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">drug combination, maintenance regimen</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bevacizumab" target="Platinum-Resistant Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Bevacizumab is a preferred option that can be combined with chemotherapy for platinum-resistant recurrent cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">combination therapy, treatment option</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Neoadjuvant Therapy" target="Surgery">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Neoadjuvant therapy is defined as systemic treatment given prior to cancer surgery.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment sequencing, clinical protocol</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Neoadjuvant Therapy" target="Systemic Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Neoadjuvant therapy is preoperative systemic therapy designed to shrink tumors prior to surgery.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment staging, preoperative care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adjuvant Chemotherapy" target="Surgery">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Adjuvant chemotherapy is administered after surgical intervention to eliminate residual disease.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment sequencing, postoperative care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="High-Grade Serous Tumors" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Patients with high-grade serous tumors are prescribed six cycles of chemotherapy, differing from other stage 1 tumors.</data>
      <data key="d6">dosage, treatment regimen</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surgical Spill" target="Stage 1C Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">An intraoperative surgical spill directly classifies an ovarian tumor into stage 1C1.</data>
      <data key="d6">staging criterion, surgical outcome</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Intravenous Chemotherapy" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Intravenous administration is the primary method of delivering systemic chemotherapy drugs to patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">drug delivery, treatment method</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Intraperitoneal Chemotherapy" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Intraperitoneal chemotherapy is an alternative delivery method that introduces chemotherapy directly into the abdomen.</data>
      <data key="d6">drug delivery, localized treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PARP Inhibitors" target="Myelodysplastic Syndrome">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Myelodysplastic syndrome is recognized as a rare but serious adverse effect associated with PARP inhibitor treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">adverse effect, drug toxicity</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PARP Inhibitors" target="Olaparib">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Olaparib is an oral targeted drug categorized as a PARP inhibitor.</data>
      <data key="d6">drug classification, targeted therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PARP Inhibitors" target="Niraparib">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Niraparib is an oral targeted drug belonging to the class of PARP inhibitors.</data>
      <data key="d6">drug classification, targeted therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PARP Inhibitors" target="Rucaparib">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Rucaparib is a targeted therapeutic agent classified as a PARP inhibitor.</data>
      <data key="d6">drug classification, targeted therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PARP Inhibitors" target="Homologous Recombination Deficiency">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">HRD-positive status indicates tumors that respond preferentially to maintenance therapy with PARP inhibitors.</data>
      <data key="d6">therapeutic target, biomarker sensitivity</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PARP Inhibitors" target="Tumor Testing">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Tumor molecular testing identifies specific DNA repair mutations that determine eligibility for targeted treatments such as PARP inhibitors.</data>
      <data key="d6">targeted therapy, molecular diagnostics</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PARP Inhibitors" target="BRCA2">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PARP inhibitors are targeted agents specifically designed to treat cancers harboring mutations in DNA repair genes like BRCA2.</data>
      <data key="d6">targeted therapy, gene marker</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PARP Inhibitors" target="ATM">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">ATM gene defects are tested to identify tumors that can be treated with PARP inhibitors.</data>
      <data key="d6">targeted therapy, therapeutic vulnerability</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PARP Inhibitors" target="HER2-Negative Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">HER2-negative breast cancer status is a clinical prerequisite for considering PARP inhibitor treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment eligibility, oncology indication</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Olaparib" target="PARP Inhibitor">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Olaparib belongs to the PARP inhibitor class of maintenance therapies.</data>
      <data key="d6">drug classification, maintenance regimen</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Niraparib" target="PARP Inhibitor">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Niraparib is an agent categorized as a PARP inhibitor.</data>
      <data key="d6">drug classification, maintenance regimen</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rucaparib" target="PARP Inhibitor">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Rucaparib is a targeted therapy within the PARP inhibitor class.</data>
      <data key="d6">drug classification, maintenance regimen</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CA-125 Testing" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CA-125 testing is carried out prior to each cycle of chemotherapy to monitor therapeutic efficacy.</data>
      <data key="d6">monitoring procedure, biomarker surveillance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CA-125 Testing" target="Doctor">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Doctors order CA-125 tests and imaging to evaluate patient response during chemotherapy cycles.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical evaluation, patient monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Platinum-Resistant Cancer" target="Non-Platinum Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Non-platinum chemotherapy is preferred as the primary recurrence treatment for platinum-resistant ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment protocol, drug selection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Platinum-Resistant Cancer" target="Mirvetuximab Soravtansine-Gynx">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mirvetuximab soravtansine-gynx is preferred for recurrent platinum-resistant tumors expressing folate receptor alpha.</data>
      <data key="d6">targeted treatment, recurrence therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Platinum-Sensitive Cancer" target="Secondary Cytoreductive Surgery">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Secondary cytoreductive surgery may be recommended for certain patients with platinum-sensitive cancer before initiating recurrence chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical treatment, clinical intervention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Platinum-Sensitive Cancer" target="PARP Inhibitor">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PARP inhibitors are utilized as maintenance therapy options after successful platinum-based chemotherapy for recurrent platinum-sensitive cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">maintenance therapy, pharmacological treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Throat Cancer" target="Oropharynx">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Throat cancer frequently develops within the oropharynx region of the throat.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical localization, cancer origin</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Throat Cancer" target="Head and Neck Cancers">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Throat cancer is classified as a specific form of head and neck cancer that arises in the oropharynx.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, anatomical localization</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Throat Cancer" target="Squamous Cell Carcinoma of the Head and Neck">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Squamous cell carcinoma is the primary histological type that constitutes throat cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathology, cancer classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Throat Cancer" target="NCCN">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN publishes patient guidelines and educational resources addressing throat cancer and related treatment pathways.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN publishes specialized patient resources and guidelines covering throat cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical guidelines, patient education&lt;SEP&gt;patient education, clinical guidelines</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Throat Cancer" target="Testing for Throat Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Diagnostic testing procedures are essential for identifying, characterizing, and staging throat cancer prior to treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnosis, clinical evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Throat Cancer" target="Human Papillomavirus">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Human papillomavirus, particularly HPV16, is identified as the major causative agent of throat cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Throat cancers are categorized and staged according to whether they are related to or mediated by human papillomavirus.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease etiology, viral association&lt;SEP&gt;viral etiology, cancer classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Throat Cancer" target="p16 Testing">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biopsy samples from throat cancer are evaluated using p16 testing for disease staging and treatment determination.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic testing, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Throat Cancer" target="HPV Type 16">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">HPV Type 16 is directly responsible for nine out of ten throat cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathogenesis, viral etiology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Throat Cancer" target="Squamous Cell Carcinoma of the Throat">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Squamous cell carcinoma of the throat, especially involving the tonsils and base of the tongue, is the specific form of throat cancer described.</data>
      <data key="d6">histopathology, oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Throat Cancer" target="HPV Testing">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Testing for p16 HPV is specifically recommended for all patients diagnosed with throat cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical guideline, diagnostic testing</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Throat Cancer" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A biopsy is performed on throat tumor tissue or lymph nodes to diagnose the cancer, conduct p16 testing, and inform staging.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic procedure, pathology testing</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Throat Cancer" target="Tumor P16 (HPV) Testing">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Tumor p16 testing is universally recommended for individuals diagnosed with throat (oropharyngeal) cancer as a key component of staging.</data>
      <data key="d6">biomarker testing, diagnostic standard</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Throat Cancer" target="P16- Throat Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">P16- throat cancer is a subcategory of throat cancer differentiated by the absence of human papillomavirus involvement.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtyping, biological classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Throat Cancer" target="Tumor HPV Testing">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Tumor HPV testing is frequently used in throat cancer because throat malignancies are most often HPV-related.</data>
      <data key="d6">viral testing, disease association</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Throat Cancer" target="HPV-Related Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Throat cancer represents a primary form of HPV-related cancer in the head and neck region.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, viral etiology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mirvetuximab Soravtansine-Gynx" target="Folate Receptor Alpha">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mirvetuximab soravtansine-gynx targets tumors with positive folate receptor alpha biomarker expression.</data>
      <data key="d6">biomarker targeting, therapeutic indication</data>
