    <edge source="Primary Tumor" target="Invasive Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d4">26.0</data>
      <data key="d5">An invasive breast cancer forms a primary tumor within breast tissue that can breach ducts or lobules to infiltrate surrounding tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;Invasive breast cancer develops when abnormal cells from the primary tumor break through ducts or lobules into surrounding breast tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;The primary tumor is the initial breast mass from which invasive cancer cells spread into surrounding tissue.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer invasion, tumor progression&lt;SEP&gt;tumor biology, local invasion&lt;SEP&gt;tumor growth, disease progression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Primary Tumor" target="Metastatic Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cancer cells break away from the primary tumor and travel via blood or lymph vessels to form metastatic secondary tumors.</data>
      <data key="d6">metastatic cascade, tumor spread</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
    </edge>
