    <edge source="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia" target="Infant ALL">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Infant ALL is a distinct presentation of acute lymphoblastic leukemia diagnosed in patients under 12 months old.</data>
      <data key="d6">age-specific classification, cancer subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia" target="Pediatric Leukemia Center">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pediatric leukemia centers are experienced specialized facilities recommended for the diagnosis and treatment of children with ALL.</data>
      <data key="d6">specialized treatment, medical care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-ALL" target="ALL">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">B-ALL is a major biological lineage subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, leukemia subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-ALL" target="BCR::ABL1-Negative B-ALL">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">BCR::ABL1-negative B-ALL is the most prevalent genetic category of B-ALL.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic subtype, disease classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-ALL" target="BCR::ABL1-Positive B-ALL">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL is a specific molecular subtype of B-ALL defined by the BCR::ABL1 gene.&lt;SEP&gt;BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL is a subtype category of B-ALL defined by a specific genetic alteration.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic subtype, targeted therapy&lt;SEP&gt;subtype classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-ALL" target="Burkitt Leukemia">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Burkitt leukemia is a mature B-cell subtype of B-ALL requiring differentiated treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Burkitt leukemia is a rare mature B-cell subtype of B-ALL with distinct treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, disease variant&lt;SEP&gt;disease subtype, clinical variation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-ALL" target="B-Cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">B-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma occurs when B-cell lymphoblasts grow primarily in lymph nodes instead of bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease manifestation, anatomical variation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-ALL" target="B Cell">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">B-ALL specifically originates from developing B-cell lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d6">cellular origin, malignant transformation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-ALL" target="Mature B-cell ALL">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mature B-cell ALL is treated differently from other forms of B-ALL despite both affecting B-lineage cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment distinction, comparative oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-ALL" target="B-cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">B-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma shares pathological origins with B-cell ALL but presents with lymphoblast proliferation primarily in lymph nodes rather than bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathological variation, clinical manifestation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-ALL" target="BCR::ABL1-Negative Or BCR::ABL1-Like B-ALL">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">BCR::ABL1-negative or BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL is the most common subtype category of B-ALL discussed in the guide.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="T-ALL" target="ALL">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">T-ALL is a T-cell lineage subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, leukemia subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="T-ALL" target="Clinical Trial">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clinical trials are listed among primary treatment pathways for individuals diagnosed with T-ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical trials represent one of the primary treatment options available for patients diagnosed with T-ALL.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment option, therapeutic research&lt;SEP&gt;treatment recommendation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="T-ALL" target="T-Cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">T-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma arises when T-cell lymphoblasts localize primarily in the thymus rather than bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease manifestation, anatomical variation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="T-ALL" target="T Cell">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">T-ALL specifically originates from developing T-cell lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d6">cellular origin, malignant transformation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="T-ALL" target="T-cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">T-ALL and T-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma involve T-cell lymphoblasts, distinguished primarily by whether growth occurs predominantly in bone marrow or the thymus.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathological presentation, disease manifestation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-Cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia" target="Burkitt Leukemia">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Burkitt leukemia is recognized as a rare, mature subtype of B-cell ALL.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype classification, hematology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-Cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia" target="B-Cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">B-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma is a manifestation of B-ALL where lymphoblasts proliferate in the lymph nodes rather than bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease variation, localization</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-Cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia" target="B Cells">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia specifically arises within B-cell lymphocyte lineages.</data>
      <data key="d6">cellular origin, leukemia subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="T-Cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia" target="T-Cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">T-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma is a related presentation where T-cell lymphoblasts proliferate predominantly in the thymus instead of bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease variation, localization</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="T-Cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia" target="T Cells">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia specifically originates from T-cell lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d6">cellular origin, leukemia subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Burkitt Leukemia" target="B-Cell ALL">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Burkitt leukemia represents a specific rare subtype of mature B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease subtype, taxonomy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adolescents and Young Adults" target="Pediatric Patients">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Adolescents and young adults share overlapping medical care pathways with pediatric patients, often being treated in pediatric oncology centers.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical care, patient age group</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adolescents and Young Adults" target="Pediatric ALL">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pediatric ALL classifications and treatment protocols often encompass select adolescent and young adult patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient demographics, pediatric oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adolescents and Young Adults" target="Pediatric Cancer Centers">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Certain adolescents and young adults diagnosed with cancer are treated at pediatric cancer centers.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment setting, patient management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adolescents and Young Adults" target="Adult Cancer Centers">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Adolescents and young adults with ALL may be referred to adult cancer centers for specialized treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment setting, oncology care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B Cells" target="B-Cell ALL">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia originates within B-cell lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d6">cellular origin, lineage</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="T Cells" target="T-Cell ALL">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia originates within T-cell lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d6">cellular origin, lineage</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ALL" target="Infant ALL">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Infant ALL is an age-specific clinical presentation of acute lymphoblastic leukemia requiring distinctive management.</data>
      <data key="d6">pediatric oncology, disease classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ALL" target="Multiagent Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Multiagent chemotherapy serves as the universal foundational systemic therapy regimen for individuals with ALL.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment regimen, systemic therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ALL" target="Steroids">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Steroids are universally administered to patients being treated for ALL.</data>
      <data key="d6">leukemia treatment, systemic therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ALL" target="Pediatric Leukemia Center">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pediatric leukemia centers are recommended institutions for treating children diagnosed with ALL.</data>
      <data key="d6">specialized treatment, pediatric oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BCR::ABL1-Negative B-ALL" target="Clinical Trial">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clinical trials represent the preferred first-line treatment approach for patients with BCR::ABL1-negative or BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL.</data>
      <data key="d6">preferred treatment, oncology research</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rectal Cancer" target="Colorectal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">26.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Rectal cancer is a primary subtype of colorectal cancer affecting the rectum.&lt;SEP&gt;Rectal cancer is an anatomical and clinical subset of colorectal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Rectal cancer is an anatomical subtype categorized under colorectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, anatomical site&lt;SEP&gt;disease classification, oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rectal Cancer" target="Colon Cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Colon cancer and rectal cancer both fall under colorectal cancers and share similar tissue origin, but their clinical treatments differ.</data>
      <data key="d6">comparative oncology, therapeutic distinction</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rectal Cancer" target="NCCN">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN publishes patient guidelines and resources for the screening, detection, and management of colorectal and rectal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN publishes specialized guidelines detailing treatment and care for rectal cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical guidelines, oncology resources&lt;SEP&gt;medical guidelines, patient education</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rectal Cancer" target="Mismatch Repair Testing">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mismatch repair testing is performed on rectal tumor specimens to guide treatment planning and identify hereditary risks.</data>
      <data key="d6">tumor profiling, biomarker analysis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rectal Cancer" target="Liquid Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Liquid biopsies detect circulating tumor DNA in the bloodstream to evaluate residual disease and monitor for rectal cancer recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d6">recurrence monitoring, diagnostic innovation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rectal Cancer" target="CEA Test">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The CEA test measures carcinoembryonic antigen levels in the blood to detect rectal cancer spread and monitor disease activity.</data>
      <data key="d6">tumor marker, cancer surveillance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rectal Cancer" target="Pelvic MRI">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pelvic MRI is the preferred staging protocol used to evaluate tumor invasion into the rectal wall and lymph node involvement in rectal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Pelvic MRI utilizing a staging technique evaluates the local extent of rectal cancer and involvement of nearby lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, diagnostic imaging&lt;SEP&gt;diagnostic imaging, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rectal Cancer" target="Endorectal Ultrasound">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Endorectal ultrasound is used to determine the local extent of rectal cancer or guide biopsies when pelvic MRI cannot be performed.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic alternative, tumor assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rectal Cancer" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy, including external beam radiation therapy, is commonly employed to treat rectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer treatment, therapeutic oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rectal Cancer" target="Mismatch Repair Deficiency">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">All rectal cancer tumors are recommended to be tested for mismatch repair deficiency or microsatellite instability.</data>
      <data key="d6">molecular pathology, biomarker testing</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rectal Cancer" target="Sessile Polyp">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Sessile polyps are flat rectal growths that carry a higher risk of recurrence after treatment, requiring precise imaging and evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer recurrence, diagnostic imaging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rectal Cancer" target="Pedunculated Polyp">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pedunculated polyps are mushroom-shaped growths on the bowel wall that can develop into rectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">polyp morphology, cancer origin</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rectal Cancer" target="CT Scan">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CT scans of the chest and abdomen are performed to check if rectal cancer has spread to distant organs such as the liver or lungs.</data>
      <data key="d6">metastasis staging, diagnostic imaging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rectal Cancer" target="Microsatellite Stable (MSS)">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Microsatellite stable status represents a molecular category evaluated in patients diagnosed with rectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">molecular pathology, biomarker evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rectal Cancer" target="Transabdominal Surgery">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Transabdominal surgery is a primary surgical intervention used to treat muscle-invasive and locally advanced rectal cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical treatment, oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rectal Cancer" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy regimens containing oxaliplatin or irinotecan are administered to treat rectal cancer and prevent recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d6">pharmacotherapy, systemic therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rectal Cancer" target="Abdominoperineal Resection (APR)">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Abdominoperineal resection is a surgical procedure shared in the treatment of both recurrent anal cancer and certain rectal cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">shared surgical application, oncology care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rectal Cancer" target="Abdominoperineal Resection">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Abdominoperineal resection is a surgical procedure used for treating both anal cancer and certain rectal cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical indication, oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colorectal Cancer" target="Colon Cancer">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Colon cancer is a major subtype of colorectal cancer referring specifically to malignancies of the colon.&lt;SEP&gt;Colon cancer is one of the primary forms of colorectal cancer, differentiated from rectal cancer by its specific therapeutic approach.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, comparative treatment&lt;SEP&gt;disease classification, oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colorectal Cancer" target="Germline Mutations">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Germline mutations in MSH2, MSH6, MLH1, and PMS2 are directly associated with colorectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic risk, mutation linkage</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colorectal Cancer" target="MLH1">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Inherited mutations in MLH1 are known contributors to colorectal cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d6">mismatch repair, hereditary cancer</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colorectal Cancer" target="NCCN">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN publishes specialized guidelines and resources to inform patients on colorectal cancer diagnosis and care.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient guidance, oncology standards</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colorectal Cancer" target="ctDNA Testing">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">ctDNA testing detects residual microscopic disease in patients with early-stage colon cancer after colectomy.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic testing, post-surgical monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colorectal Cancer" target="Computed Tomography">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CT with contrast serves as the primary imaging modality used to assess the staging and extent of colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic imaging, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colorectal Cancer" target="Mismatch Repair Deficiency">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">All colon cancers are recommended to be tested for mismatch repair deficiency or microsatellite instability.</data>
      <data key="d6">biomarker testing, tumor characterization</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colorectal Cancer" target="Carcinoembryonic Antigen Test">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The CEA test measures tumor-associated antigens to evaluate disease state in colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">tumor marker, diagnostic testing</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colorectal Cancer" target="Non-Metastatic Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Non-metastatic cancer represents a clinical staging category of colon cancer that has not spread to distant sites.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, clinical classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colorectal Cancer" target="Cowden Syndrome">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Personal or familial history of colorectal cancer is monitored in patients evaluated for syndromes like Cowden syndrome.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical evaluation, hereditary cancer</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colorectal Cancer" target="Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Anal cancer is clinically contrasted with colorectal cancer based on differing primary cell types and therapeutic approaches.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease comparison, clinical distinction</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colon Cancer" target="Polypectomy">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Performing a polypectomy to remove polyps prevents the onset and progression of colon cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Removing polyps via polypectomy prevents colon cancer from developing.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer prevention, intervention&lt;SEP&gt;cancer prevention, surgical treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colon Cancer" target="NCCN">
      <data key="d4">15.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN provides clinical and patient guidelines for colorectal and colon cancer care and screening.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN provides clinical guidelines, apps, and educational resources for colon cancer screening, diagnosis, and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical guidelines, patient education&lt;SEP&gt;medical guidelines, patient education</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colon Cancer" target="Non-Metastatic Colon Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Non-metastatic colon cancer represents a stage of colon cancer confined to the colon without distant metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d6">sub-classification, disease stage</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colon Cancer" target="Metastatic Colon Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Metastatic colon cancer represents an advanced stage of colon cancer that has spread to organs such as the liver and lungs.</data>
      <data key="d6">sub-classification, disease stage</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colon Cancer" target="Mismatch Repair Testing">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mismatch repair testing is recommended for all diagnosed colon cancers to determine biomarker status and guide therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic testing, biomarker evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colon Cancer" target="CEA Blood Test">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CEA blood tests are used to monitor cancer progression, recurrence, and potential metastatic spread in colon cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">biomarker monitoring, disease tracking</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colon Cancer" target="Circulating Tumor DNA">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Circulating tumor DNA represents an emerging biomarker modality for colon cancer diagnosis and tracking.</data>
      <data key="d6">biomarker testing, emerging diagnostics</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colon Cancer" target="Polyps">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Almost all colon cancers originate from precancerous polyps that develop on the inner lining of the colon.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease origin, oncogenesis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colon Cancer" target="Diagnostic Colonoscopy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A diagnostic colonoscopy is conducted to locate, verify, and examine suspected colon cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic procedure, cancer detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colon Cancer" target="Colorectal Cancer Survivor">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The colorectal cancer survivor shares personal experiences of diagnostic delays, resilience, and advocacy following a colorectal cancer diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient experience, advocacy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colon Cancer" target="Surveillance Follow-Up Testing">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Follow-up surveillance testing including CT scans, CEA tests, and exams monitors colon cancer patients for recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d6">recurrence detection, clinical surveillance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colon Cancer" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy treatment durations of 3 to 6 months depend on regimen and recurrence risk prior to post-treatment follow-up.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment regimen, cancer therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hyperplastic Polyps" target="Polyps">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hyperplastic polyps represent a specific non-cancerous subset of colorectal polyps.</data>
      <data key="d6">polyps subtype, benign pathology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hyperplastic Polyps" target="The Colon">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hyperplastic polyps develop along the inner lining of the colon but rarely transition into cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">polyp growth, benign condition</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inflammatory Polyps" target="Polyps">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Inflammatory polyps are a subtype of colorectal polyps with minimal risk of malignant transformation.</data>
      <data key="d6">polyps subtype, benign pathology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inflammatory Polyps" target="The Colon">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Inflammatory polyps are benign tissue growths located in the lining of the colon.</data>
      <data key="d6">benign condition, colon pathology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pediatric Leukemia Center" target="ALL Specialist">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">ALL specialists commonly provide consultation and care within specialized pediatric leukemia centers.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical expertise, medical consultation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Patient Advocacy Groups" target="NCCN">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The patient resource guidance references patient advocacy groups to help families find support and information alongside NCCN materials.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient resources, support networks</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Patient Advocacy Groups" target="Bone Cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Patient advocacy groups offer external support and resource navigation for individuals facing bone cancer diagnoses.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient support, health resources, advocacy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Oncologist" target="Care Team">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The oncologist serves as a primary specialist within the patient's cancer care team.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical team, oncology care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Oncologist" target="Physical Exam">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The oncologist performs routine physical examinations during surveillance visits to monitor for signs of recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical evaluation, patient care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Oncologist" target="Physical Exams">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Oncologists perform surveillance physical examinations to assess patient recovery and detect early signs of recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical surveillance, clinical assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Oncologist" target="Blood Smear">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">An oncologist evaluates the blood smear with the hematopathologist to confirm a cancer diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical evaluation, diagnostic pathology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Polypectomy" target="Malignant Polyp">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A malignant polyp can often be resected during a polypectomy if discovered early before deep invasion occurs.&lt;SEP&gt;Most malignant polyps can be excised during a colonoscopy using a polypectomy.</data>
      <data key="d6">early detection, polyp excision&lt;SEP&gt;medical treatment, surgical excision</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Polypectomy" target="Doctor">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The doctor evaluates tissue and test results obtained from a polypectomy to determine if additional surgery is needed.</data>
      <data key="d6">consultation, diagnostic review</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mismatch Repair Testing" target="Biomarker">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mismatch repair status serves as a key biomarker identified during laboratory analysis of rectal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d6">classification, therapeutic guidance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Liquid Biopsy" target="Biopsy of the Metastasis">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Liquid biopsy functions as a form of metastatic biopsy by testing blood samples for spreading cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">minimally invasive testing, metastatic screening</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Liquid Biopsy" target="ctDNA Testing">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">ctDNA testing is also commonly referred to and categorized as a liquid biopsy.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic classification, synonymy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Biomarker" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Tissue and cell specimens collected via biopsy are utilized to evaluate biomarkers and tumor histology.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathological analysis, molecular profiling</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Endometrial Cancer" target="Cowden Syndrome">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Endometrial cancer risk is linked to Cowden syndrome alongside thyroid cancer susceptibility.</data>
      <data key="d6">syndromic association, cancer predisposition</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pelvic MRI" target="Contrast Agent">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pelvic MRI procedures may utilize or omit contrast agents depending on the imaging protocol and patient tolerance.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic enhancement, imaging protocol</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pelvic MRI" target="Sessile Polyp">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pelvic MRI is recommended to image flat sessile polyps and guide decisions regarding resection.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic imaging, surgical planning</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pelvic MRI" target="Stage 1 Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pelvic MRI is indicated 6 months after fertility-sparing surgery for stage 1 disease and yearly for 2 to 3 years.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease staging, post-surgical monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pelvic MRI" target="Fertility-Sparing Treatment">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pelvic MRI is scheduled at 6 months and annually after fertility-sparing treatment to monitor pelvic tissue.</data>
      <data key="d6">fertility preservation, post-operative surveillance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Ovarian Transposition" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Ovarian transposition is performed prior to pelvic radiation therapy to surgically reposition ovaries away from radiation beams.</data>
      <data key="d6">fertility preservation, surgical intervention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Ovarian Transposition" target="Reproductive Endocrinologist">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Reproductive endocrinologists discuss ovarian transposition as an option to preserve ovarian function prior to radiation.</data>
      <data key="d6">fertility preservation, surgical consultation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Contrast Agent" target="Computed Tomography">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Computed tomography scans of the chest and abdomen require the administration of oral or intravenous contrast agents for image clarity.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedural requirement, imaging enhancement</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Premature Ovarian Insufficiency" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pelvic radiation therapy damages ovarian tissue and hormone synthesis, directly causing premature ovarian insufficiency.</data>
      <data key="d6">iatrogenic side effect, gonadotoxicity</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Egg Freezing" target="Reproductive Endocrinologist">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Reproductive endocrinologists evaluate and perform egg freezing as a fertility preservation strategy for cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">fertility preservation, reproductive endocrinology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Egg Freezing" target="Surrogate">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stored frozen eggs can later be fertilized and carried by a surrogate if the patient cannot sustain pregnancy.</data>
      <data key="d6">surrogacy, reproductive planning</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Google" target="Primary CNS Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">5.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Patients diagnosed with primary CNS lymphoma are advised not to look up their diagnosis on Google to avoid misleading statistics and unnecessary fear.</data>
      <data key="d6">information seeking, patient advice</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Sessile Polyp" target="Endoscopic Ultrasound (EUS)">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Endoscopic ultrasound is recommended to evaluate sessile polyps and inform surgical decision-making.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic imaging, clinical evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="T1 Tumor" target="Endoscopic Submucosal Dissection">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Endoscopic Submucosal Dissection is a recommended, minimally invasive treatment option for removing T1 tumors.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment method, tumor staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="T1 Tumor" target="Transanal Local Excision">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Transanal local excision is an operative option for T1 tumors situated at the end of the rectum.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical procedure, tumor location</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="T1 Tumor" target="Transabdominal Surgery">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Transabdominal surgery is a recommended operative option for treating smaller T1 tumors.</data>
      <data key="d6">staging, surgical management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="T2 Tumor" target="Transanal Local Excision">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">If testing after a local excision reveals a T2 tumor that has invaded the muscle layer, further transabdominal surgery is typically required.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment escalation, pathology findings</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="T2 Tumor" target="Transabdominal Surgery">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Transabdominal surgery is the preferred subsequent intervention if pathology after local excision reveals a T2 tumor.&lt;SEP&gt;Transabdominal surgery is the preferred treatment recommended for T2 rectal tumors invading the muscle layer.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical escalation, disease management&lt;SEP&gt;treatment indication, surgical management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Endoscopic Submucosal Dissection" target="Flexible Sigmoidoscopy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Flexible sigmoidoscopy is performed regularly for at least 5 years as part of post-ESD surveillance to detect local recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d6">post-operative surveillance, endoscopic monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Transanal Local Excision" target="Transanal Endoscopic Microsurgery">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Transanal Endoscopic Microsurgery is an advanced approach related to transanal local excision for removing tumors higher in the rectum.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical advancement, excision technique</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Transanal Local Excision" target="Transanal Minimally Invasive Surgery">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Transanal Minimally Invasive Surgery is an advanced excision method used for tumors positioned higher in the rectum than standard transanal local excision can reach.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical advancement, excision technique</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Transanal Local Excision" target="Proctoscopy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Proctoscopy is recommended every 3 to 6 months during surveillance following transanal local excision to detect recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d6">post-operative surveillance, medical monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Proctoscopy" target="Examination Under Anesthesia">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Proctoscopy is an examination under anesthesia conducted to inspect the rectum and anus for malignant extension.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical examination, anesthesia procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Proctoscopy" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">During a proctoscopy, biopsies are performed on suspicious lesions identified in the rectum or anus.</data>
      <data key="d6">tissue sampling, pathological diagnosis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Transabdominal Surgery" target="Low Anterior Resection">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Low anterior resection is a specific classification and method of transabdominal surgery indicated for upper and mid-rectal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical subtype, operative procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Transabdominal Surgery" target="Abdominoperineal Resection">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Abdominoperineal resection is a form of transabdominal surgery performed for lower rectal tumors invading sphincter muscles.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical subtype, operative procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Transabdominal Surgery" target="Pathologist">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A pathologist evaluates the rectal tissue and lymph nodes excised during transabdominal surgery to determine staging.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathology examination, clinical evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Transabdominal Surgery" target="Local Excision">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Local excision results revealing high-risk features or T2 tumors indicate the need for transabdominal surgery.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment progression, surgical indication</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Transabdominal Surgery" target="T3 Cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Transabdominal surgery is recommended for larger T3 rectal cancers that have not spread to the lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">staging, surgical management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Low Anterior Resection" target="Colostomy Creation">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A temporary colostomy may be created during low anterior resection to allow the anastomotic site time to heal.</data>
      <data key="d6">temporary intervention, surgical healing</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Low Anterior Resection" target="Colorectal Anastomosis">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Colorectal anastomosis is the reconnection method performed during low anterior resection when rectum remains.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical technique, reconnection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Low Anterior Resection" target="Coloanal Anastomosis">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Coloanal anastomosis is performed during low anterior resection if the colon must be attached directly to the anus.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical technique, reconnection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Abdominoperineal Resection" target="Colostomy Creation">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">An abdominoperineal resection permanently requires colostomy creation because the rectum and anus are removed.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical necessity, post-operative outcome</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Abdominoperineal Resection" target="Recurrent Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Abdominoperineal resection is recommended when anal cancer returns to the anal area or does not respond to initial treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical treatment, disease recurrence</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Abdominoperineal Resection" target="Permanent Colostomy">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Abdominoperineal resection removes the anus, necessitating a permanent colostomy for waste elimination.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical consequence, anatomical alteration</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Abdominoperineal Resection" target="Muscle Flap Reconstruction">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Muscle flap reconstruction is considered alongside abdominoperineal resection to rebuild tissue removed during surgery.&lt;SEP&gt;Muscle flap reconstruction is frequently utilized to aid perineal wound closure and healing following an abdominoperineal resection.</data>
      <data key="d6">reconstructive surgery, postoperative healing&lt;SEP&gt;surgical reconstruction, recovery</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Abdominoperineal Resection" target="Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Abdominoperineal resection (APR) is a major surgical option considered for anal cancer treatment or recurrence.&lt;SEP&gt;Abdominoperineal resection is a surgical option for anal cancer that does not respond to initial treatment or recurs.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical intervention, cancer treatment&lt;SEP&gt;surgical treatment, disease intervention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Abdominoperineal Resection" target="Colostomy">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Abdominoperineal resection involves surgical removal of the rectum and anus, requiring patients to adapt to a colostomy.&lt;SEP&gt;Abdominoperineal resection permanently removes the anus and rectum, necessitating a permanent colostomy.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical outcome, post-operative care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Abdominoperineal Resection" target="Anus">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">An abdominoperineal resection involves surgical excision of the anus.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical procedure, anatomical resection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Local Excision" target="Perianal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Local excision is a surgical treatment option specifically evaluated for certain presentations of perianal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Local excision serves as a treatment option for selected early-stage, low-risk perianal cancers that have not invaded the sphincter muscle.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical treatment, clinical indication</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Local Excision" target="Surgical Margin">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Successful local excision requires obtaining a clear surgical margin of normal tissue of at least 1 cm around the lesion.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical standard, oncology procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Local Excision" target="G1 (Low Grade)">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Local excision is specifically considered and recommended for low-grade (G1) perianal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment eligibility, tumor grading</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Local Excision" target="G2 (Intermediate Grade)">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Local excision is considered for intermediate-grade (G2) perianal cancer provided adequate margins can be achieved.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment eligibility, tumor grading</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Local Excision" target="Re-Excision">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Re-excision is the preferred follow-up surgery if the initial local excision yields positive margins or margins under 1 cm.</data>
      <data key="d6">secondary surgical intervention, margin clearance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Local Excision" target="Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Local excision is a surgical option for selected slow-growing stage I and II perianal cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical treatment, early-stage intervention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Watch And Wait" target="Doctor">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The doctor and patient jointly decide whether to pursue a watch and wait approach following successful chemoradiation.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment decision, clinical monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 2 Cancer" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Patients diagnosed with Stage 2 rectal cancer usually require chemotherapy or chemoradiation.</data>
      <data key="d6">adjuvant therapy, cancer management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 3 Cancer" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 3 rectal cancer with lymph node involvement almost always requires post-surgical chemotherapy or chemoradiation.</data>
      <data key="d6">adjuvant therapy, systemic treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="OX Regimen" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">OX regimens are a specific category of chemotherapy regimens containing oxaliplatin used in rectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">chemotherapy classification, pharmacology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IRI Regimen" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">IRI regimens represent chemotherapy combinations containing irinotecan for rectal cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">chemotherapy classification, pharmacology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Cell Carcinoma" target="Clear Cell RCC">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clear Cell RCC is the most prevalent histological subtype of Renal Cell Carcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Clear cell RCC is the most prevalent histological subtype of renal cell carcinoma, occurring in roughly 7 out of 10 RCC cases.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer subtype, histology classification&lt;SEP&gt;histological subtype, disease classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Cell Carcinoma" target="Non-Clear Cell RCC">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Non-Clear Cell RCC is a primary classification group of Renal Cell Carcinoma subtypes.&lt;SEP&gt;Non-clear cell RCC encompasses multiple other histological subtypes of renal cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer subtype, classification&lt;SEP&gt;histological classification, disease subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Cell Carcinoma" target="Translocation RCC">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Translocation RCC is an uncommon subtype of Renal Cell Carcinoma driven by gene fusions.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer subtype, genetic fusion</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Cell Carcinoma" target="Unclassified RCC">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Unclassified RCC represents Renal Cell Carcinoma cases where histology does not fit defined subtypes.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic categorization, histology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Cell Carcinoma" target="Sarcomatoid RCC">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Almost any subtype of Renal Cell Carcinoma can develop sarcomatoid features, resulting in more aggressive behavior.</data>
      <data key="d6">tumor differentiation, disease progression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Cell Carcinoma" target="Kidney Cancer Diagnosis">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Diagnostic testing identifies Renal Cell Carcinoma, its histological subtype, and stage to plan treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical diagnosis, treatment planning</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Cell Carcinoma" target="Clear Cell Papillary Renal Cell Tumor">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clear Cell Papillary Renal Cell Tumor is an emerging distinct subtype categorized among renal cell neoplasms.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype classification, renal oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Cell Carcinoma" target="Fumarate Hydratase-Deficient RCC">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Fumarate hydratase-deficient RCC is a recently defined genetic subtype of renal cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic subtype, oncology classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Cell Carcinoma" target="ELOC-Mutated RCC">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">ELOC-mutated RCC represents a defined subtype of renal cell carcinoma characterized by TCEB1/ELOC mutations.</data>
      <data key="d6">molecular subtype, tumor classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Cell Carcinoma" target="Histology">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Histology under microscopic analysis is the fundamental method used to classify RCC into specific cellular subtypes.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathologic classification, microscopic analysis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Non-Clear Cell RCC" target="Papillary RCC">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Papillary RCC is the most common subtype within the Non-Clear Cell RCC category.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype categorization, histology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Non-Clear Cell RCC" target="Chromophobe RCC">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chromophobe RCC is a distinct subtype of Non-Clear Cell RCC.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype categorization, histology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Non-Clear Cell RCC" target="Collecting Duct RCC">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Collecting Duct RCC is a rare subtype categorized under Non-Clear Cell RCC.</data>
      <data key="d6">rare subtype, kidney anatomy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Non-Clear Cell RCC" target="Renal Medullary Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Renal Medullary Carcinoma is a rare type classified under Non-Clear Cell RCC.</data>
      <data key="d6">rare subtype, oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urothelial Carcinoma" target="NCCN">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN provides patient guidelines relevant to urothelial and bladder cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical guidelines, patient education</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urothelial Carcinoma" target="Transitional Cell Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Transitional cell cancer is the previous medical terminology used to describe urothelial carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">nomenclature, medical classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urothelial Carcinoma" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Urothelial carcinoma tumors share many diagnostic and clinical features with bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical similarity, oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Sarcoma" target="NCCN">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN publishes patient guidelines for soft tissue sarcomas, which include renal sarcomas.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical guidelines, patient education</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Sarcoma" target="Soft Tissue Sarcoma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Renal sarcoma is a specific form of sarcoma arising in connective tissue, classified under soft tissue sarcomas.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, sarcoma subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder Cancer" target="NCCN">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN provides patient guidance, resources, and collects feedback concerning bladder cancer education.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN publishes specialized guidelines and educational resources for patients with bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient education, clinical guidelines&lt;SEP&gt;patient education, healthcare guidance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder Cancer" target="Follow-Up Testing">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Regular follow-up testing for many years is vital for bladder cancer patients due to the high likelihood of cancer recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer surveillance, disease recurrence</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder Cancer" target="Urothelial Cancer">
      <data key="d4">19.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Most bladder cancer originates in urothelial cells and is categorized as urothelial cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Urothelial cancer represents the most prevalent histological type of bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, histology&lt;SEP&gt;cancer classification, pathology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder Cancer" target="Hematuria">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hematuria is the most frequent and initial symptom that prompts the diagnosis of bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">symptom, clinical indicator</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder Cancer" target="Non-Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Non-muscle-invasive bladder cancer describes the earliest of the three phases of bladder cancer progression.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease phase, progression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder Cancer" target="Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">27.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Muscle-invasive bladder cancer is a more advanced stage of bladder cancer that involves deeper tissue and complex treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Muscle-invasive bladder cancer is a specific, advanced category of bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Muscle-invasive bladder cancer is an aggressive phase where bladder cancer penetrates the muscle wall.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, cancer stage&lt;SEP&gt;disease phase, tumor invasion&lt;SEP&gt;disease progression, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder Cancer" target="Metastatic Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Metastatic bladder cancer is the advanced form of bladder cancer that has spread to distant body sites.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic bladder cancer is the advanced phase where cancer originates in the bladder and spreads to other body sites.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease phase, metastasis&lt;SEP&gt;metastasis, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder Cancer" target="Chemical Exposure">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Exposure to industrial chemicals in textiles, paints, and rubber increases the risk of developing bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">occupational risk factor, environmental hazard</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder Cancer" target="Cancer of the Urothelium">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Bladder cancer is predominantly a cancer of the urothelium, which can also occur in the kidneys, ureters, and urethra.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, anatomical origin</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder Cancer" target="Urinary Tract Infection">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Urinary tract infections share symptoms with bladder cancer, requiring testing to differentiate between them.</data>
      <data key="d6">differential diagnosis, symptom overlap</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder Cancer" target="Frequent Urination">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Frequent urination is a recognized secondary symptom of bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">symptom, clinical presentation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder Cancer" target="Urgency to Urinate">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">An urgent need to urinate can signal the presence of bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">symptom, clinical presentation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder Cancer" target="Painful Urination">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Painful urination is one of the clinical symptoms associated with bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">symptom, clinical presentation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder Cancer" target="Back Pain">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Back pain is identified as a potential symptom of bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">symptom, clinical presentation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder Cancer" target="Cystoscopy">
      <data key="d4">36.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cystoscopy is a key diagnostic procedure used to directly view and detect cancer inside the bladder.&lt;SEP&gt;Cystoscopy is a key diagnostic test used to directly inspect the bladder wall for tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Cystoscopy is the primary visual and diagnostic examination used to identify bladder cancer within the urethra and bladder.&lt;SEP&gt;Cystoscopy is used to visually inspect the bladder for signs of bladder cancer and remove small growths.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer diagnosis, surveillance&lt;SEP&gt;diagnostic procedure, cancer detection&lt;SEP&gt;diagnostic procedure, visualization&lt;SEP&gt;primary detection, clinical screening</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder Cancer" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">27.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biopsies are performed on suspicious growths to confirm or rule out the presence of bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Biopsy and tissue resection are essential procedures to definitively confirm a bladder cancer diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Biopsy findings are evaluated by clinicians to establish the diagnosis and define treatment plans for bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer detection, pathological confirmation&lt;SEP&gt;diagnostic confirmation, treatment planning&lt;SEP&gt;diagnostic procedure, tissue pathology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder Cancer" target="Genetic Mutations">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Bladder cancer originates when genetic mutations disrupt cell instructions and cause uncontrolled cell growth.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathogenesis, cellular biology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder Cancer" target="Secondhand Smoke">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Exposure to secondhand smoke increases an individual's risk of developing bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Exposure to secondhand smoke increases the risk of bladder cancer among a smoker's family and friends.</data>
      <data key="d6">environmental risk factor, disease etiology&lt;SEP&gt;environmental risk, disease transmission</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder Cancer" target="Clinical Team">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The clinical team evaluates symptoms, performs diagnostic tests, and recommends treatment options for bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient care, medical treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder Cancer" target="Surgery">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Surgery is used at multiple stages of bladder cancer to remove tumors or the entire bladder.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer treatment, surgical intervention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder Cancer" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">25.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy is administered intravesically or systemically to treat various stages of bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemotherapy is used as a systemic treatment option to eliminate bladder cancer cells throughout the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemotherapy is used as a treatment for bladder cancer and can result in specific side effects.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer therapy, medical oncology&lt;SEP&gt;cancer treatment, side effect management&lt;SEP&gt;systemic therapy, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder Cancer" target="Immunotherapy">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Immunotherapy is deployed as a key therapeutic approach to manage localized and metastatic bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is used as a therapeutic approach to treat bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer treatment, medical therapy&lt;SEP&gt;immunotherapy, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder Cancer" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy is a localized treatment option used to target bladder cancer, sometimes alongside chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiation therapy serves as one of the common treatment modalities for bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation oncology, cancer treatment&lt;SEP&gt;radiation oncology, localized therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder Cancer" target="Targeted Therapy">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Targeted therapy is identified as an established treatment option for bladder cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">precision medicine, cancer therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder Cancer" target="Clinical Trial">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clinical trials test and approve new therapies for bladder cancer patients, serving as an ongoing treatment pathway.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical research, treatment development</data>
