      <data key="d7">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PSMA/PET Scan" target="Prostate-Specific Membrane Antigen">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PSMA/PET scans utilize specialized tracers designed specifically to bind to the prostate-specific membrane antigen on cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">antigen targeting, molecular imaging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PSA Test" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PSA test results are considered alongside biopsy results to assess cancer severity and metastatic risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Results from a PSA test are used to determine whether a patient requires a follow-up biopsy.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic correlation, risk stratification&lt;SEP&gt;diagnostic pathway, clinical escalation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PSA Test" target="Risk Groups">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PSA test results are one of the core clinical criteria used to categorize prostate cancer into specific risk groups.</data>
      <data key="d6">risk calculation, prognostic scoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PSA Test" target="PSA Level">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The PSA test is the diagnostic blood test performed to measure the level of prostate-specific antigen.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic testing, measurement</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PSA Test" target="Doctor">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A doctor advises patients on preparatory precautions, such as pausing exercise and sexual activity, before taking a PSA test.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical guidance, test preparation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate-Specific Membrane Antigen" target="PSMA-PET Scan">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Tracer molecules used in PSMA-PET scans specifically bind to prostate-specific membrane antigen on cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">molecular targeting, diagnostic imaging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate-Specific Membrane Antigen" target="Targeted Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Targeted therapy leverages PSMA expression on cancer cells to treat selected cases of metastatic prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">targeted treatment, molecular therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urologist" target="Prostate Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A urologist performs the prostate biopsy procedure by inserting the needle and collecting core tissue samples.&lt;SEP&gt;The urologist performs the prostate biopsy procedure by inserting a needle to obtain tissue core samples.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical procedure, clinical execution&lt;SEP&gt;medical procedure, clinical role</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urologist" target="Cystoscopy">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A urologist performs cystoscopy procedures to inspect the urethra and bladder and collect samples.&lt;SEP&gt;The urologist performs the cystoscopy procedure to inspect the bladder, take biopsies, and remove small growths.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical examination, procedure execution&lt;SEP&gt;medical procedure, diagnostic examination</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urologist" target="Urine Cytology">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A urologist can collect urine samples for cytology analysis during an examination or cystoscopy procedure.</data>
      <data key="d6">sample collection, diagnostic workflow</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urologist" target="Ureteroscopy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The urologist conducts a ureteroscopy to evaluate abnormalities in the ureters or kidneys.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical examination, diagnostic testing</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urologist" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The urologist uses instruments on the cystoscope to collect tissue samples for biopsy testing.</data>
      <data key="d6">tissue sampling, procedure execution</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urologist" target="Tumor Removal">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Urologists can resect small cancer growths during cystoscopy or participate in subsequent tumor removal.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical intervention, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urologist" target="TURBT">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A urologist performs the TURBT procedure by inserting an instrument through the urethra into the bladder.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical procedure, medical treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urologist" target="Radical Cystectomy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Urologists perform radical cystectomies and discuss the associated risks, benefits, and complications with patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical consultation, clinical care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Testing Lab" target="Pathologist">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The pathologist works within a laboratory to analyze prostate biopsy samples under a microscope.</data>
      <data key="d6">professional affiliation, clinical analysis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Biopsy" target="Pathologist">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A pathologist analyzes the core tissue samples obtained during a prostate biopsy to evaluate and report on cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;The pathologist examines the core tissue samples obtained during a biopsy to measure the extent of cancer and compile a report.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic evaluation, specimen analysis&lt;SEP&gt;tissue analysis, laboratory diagnostics</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Biopsy" target="Transperineal Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A transperineal biopsy is a specific form of prostate biopsy where the needle enters through the perineum.&lt;SEP&gt;Transperineal biopsy is an anatomical approach to performing a prostate biopsy through the perineal skin.</data>
      <data key="d6">biopsy technique, procedural classification&lt;SEP&gt;procedure subtype, medical technique</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Biopsy" target="Confirmatory Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A confirmatory biopsy is a secondary prostate biopsy conducted within a year to track any progression.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedural recurrence, disease surveillance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Biopsy" target="PSA Level">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">High PSA levels prompt doctors and patients to consider proceeding with a prostate biopsy to confirm cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic trigger, clinical decision</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Biopsy" target="Rectum">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Prostate biopsies use a probe inserted through the rectum, and needles may also pass through rectal tissue.</data>
      <data key="d6">instrumentation, anatomical access</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Biopsy" target="Transrectal Ultrasound">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Transrectal ultrasound is inserted into the rectum to guide the needle during a prostate biopsy.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedural guidance, diagnostic imaging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Biopsy" target="MRI-TRUS Fusion">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MRI-TRUS fusion integrates MRI data with ultrasound to improve needle precision during a prostate biopsy.</data>
      <data key="d6">technological enhancement, biopsy guidance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Biopsy" target="Care Team">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The care team collaborates with the patient to decide on the necessity of performing an invasive prostate biopsy.</data>
      <data key="d6">shared decision-making, clinical care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Transperineal Biopsy" target="Perineum">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A transperineal biopsy involves inserting a needle directly through the perineum into the prostate.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical approach, needle guidance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Transperineal Biopsy" target="Transrectal Ultrasound">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Transperineal biopsies utilize an ultrasound probe in the rectum to guide needle insertion into the prostate.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedural guidance, diagnostic imaging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Germline Testing" target="Germline Mutation">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Germline testing directly searches for inherited germline mutations present in every cell.</data>
      <data key="d6">detection, genetic screening</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Germline Testing" target="BRCA1">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Germline testing analyzes the BRCA1 gene to identify mutations related to prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">gene testing, biomarker identification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Germline Testing" target="BRCA2">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Germline testing analyzes the BRCA2 gene to check for inherited mutations conferring cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d6">gene testing, biomarker identification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Germline Testing" target="ATM">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">ATM is one of the characteristic genes screened during germline testing for prostate cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic screening, gene mutation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Germline Testing" target="CHEK2">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CHEK2 is included in the panel of genes evaluated during germline testing for hereditary cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic screening, gene mutation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Germline Testing" target="MLH1">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MLH1 is assessed during germline testing to identify potential inherited risk of prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic screening, gene mutation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Germline Testing" target="MSH2">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MSH2 is tested in germline diagnostic procedures to identify inherited cancer mutations.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic screening, gene mutation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Germline Testing" target="MSH6">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MSH6 is screened during prostate cancer germline testing to check for hereditary mutations.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic screening, gene mutation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Germline Testing" target="PALB2">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PALB2 is evaluated in germline genetic tests for mutations that elevate prostate cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic screening, gene mutation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Germline Testing" target="PMS2">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PMS2 is among the genes analyzed during germline testing for prostate cancer risk assessment.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic screening, gene mutation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Germline Testing" target="Upper Urinary Tract Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Having cancer of the upper urinary tract is a qualifying factor recommending the patient undergo germline testing.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic screening criteria, hereditary cancer</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Genetic Mutation" target="Germline Mutation">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Germline mutations represent inherited variations within the broader category of genetic mutations.</data>
      <data key="d6">classification, genetics</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Genetic Mutation" target="Somatic Mutation">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Somatic mutations represent acquired changes within the broader classification of genetic mutations.</data>
      <data key="d6">classification, genetics</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Germline Mutation" target="Genetic Cancer Risk Testing">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Genetic cancer risk testing checks blood or saliva specifically to detect inherited germline mutations.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic testing, mutation identification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Somatic Mutation" target="Tumor Testing">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Tumor testing identifies somatic mutations acquired within tumor tissue or metastatic lesions.</data>
      <data key="d6">molecular testing, acquired mutation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Somatic Mutation" target="Chromosomal Translocation">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">In Ewing sarcoma, chromosomal translocation represents a non-inherited somatic mutation occurring during cell division.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic mechanism, mutation type</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Somatic Mutation" target="Tumor Mutational Burden-High (TMB-H)">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Tumor mutational burden measures the frequency of somatic mutations within tumor DNA.</data>
      <data key="d6">genomic burden, mutation measurement</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BRCA1" target="BRCA Tests">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">BRCA tests examine the BRCA1 gene to identify harmful inherited mutations.</data>
      <data key="d6">gene testing, mutation detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BRCA2" target="BRCA Tests">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">BRCA tests screen the BRCA2 gene to evaluate inherited susceptibility to cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">gene testing, mutation detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pelvis" target="Pelvic Lymph Node">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pelvic lymph nodes are located within the pelvic area and can harbor metastatic prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical location, cancer metastasis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pelvis" target="TNM System">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The metastasis (M) category of the TNM staging system evaluates whether prostate cancer has spread beyond the pelvis.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical staging, metastasis criteria</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pelvis" target="T Category">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Tumors in the pelvis follow distinct T staging criteria categorized by pelvic segment invasion.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical staging, pelvic oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pelvis" target="Spine">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The pelvis physically supports the spinal column and connects to it across the sacroiliac joint.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical structure, skeletal support</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pelvis" target="Sacroiliac Joint">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The sacroiliac joint connects the pelvic framework to the spine and serves as a anatomical boundary for stage T4 pelvic tumors.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical boundary, pelvic staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Molecular Biomarker Testing" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Molecular biomarker testing utilizes cell and tissue samples obtained during a biopsy procedure.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic sampling, molecular profiling</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Molecular Biomarker Testing" target="Surgery">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Molecular biomarker test results can indicate lower risk, enabling patients to avoid or defer surgery and its complications.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment deferral, surgical avoidance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Molecular Biomarker Testing" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biomarker testing assists in determining whether radiation therapy is urgently needed or can be postponed.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment selection, clinical decision-making</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PSA Level" target="Risk Group">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PSA levels are calculated alongside Grade Group, tumor stage, and biopsy findings to determine a patient's prostate cancer risk group.</data>
      <data key="d6">risk assessment, cancer diagnosis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PSA Level" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Undergoing a prostate biopsy causes a temporary rise in a patient's PSA level.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedural effect, diagnostic variation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PSA Level" target="PSA Density">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PSA density is calculated directly by dividing the PSA level by the measured volume of the prostate.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic calculation, risk adjustment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PSA Level" target="Vigorous Exercise">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Engaging in vigorous exercise can temporarily boost PSA concentrations in blood samples.</data>
      <data key="d6">lifestyle factor, biomarker fluctuation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PALB2" target="Tumor Testing">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PALB2 is evaluated in somatic tumor testing to detect DNA repair defects.</data>
      <data key="d6">molecular profiling, DNA repair</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Germline Mutations" target="Genetic Germline Testing">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Germline testing is performed to identify inherited mutations across specific genes such as BRCA1, BRCA2, and ATM.</data>
      <data key="d6">mutation detection, genetic screening</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Genetic Germline Testing" target="Ashkenazi Jewish Ancestry">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Ashkenazi Jewish ancestry is a specific criterion indicating the recommendation for germline genetic testing in prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">screening indication, genetic risk</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Genetic Germline Testing" target="Intestinal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A family history of intestinal cancer is an established criterion for recommending germline genetic testing.</data>
      <data key="d6">family risk, diagnostic indication</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Risk Groups" target="Care Team">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The care team classifies the patient's prostate cancer into one of five risk groups based on test results and tumor characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient classification, prognostic evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Risk Groups" target="Grade Group">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Grade Group designations derived from pathology calculations are combined with PSA and tumor stage to categorize patients into risk groups.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathology grading, risk categorization</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Risk Groups" target="Tumor Stage">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Tumor stage is an essential diagnostic factor combined with biopsy data and PSA to assign a prostate cancer risk group.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, risk categorization</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Ashkenazi Jewish Ancestry" target="Hereditary Gene Testing">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Having Ashkenazi Jewish ancestry is a clinical criterion for recommending hereditary gene testing in individuals with prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic risk, testing criteria</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Grade Group" target="Risk Group">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Grade Group is a critical component used in combination with other clinical markers to establish the patient's cancer risk group.</data>
      <data key="d6">risk classification, prognosis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Grade Group" target="Gleason Score">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Grade Groups are structured to interpret and simplify Gleason scores to communicate cancer aggressiveness.</data>
      <data key="d6">scoring system, cancer grading</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Tumor Stage" target="Risk Group">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Tumor stage is combined with PSA levels, Grade Group, and biopsy results to calculate the overall risk group.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical staging, risk assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Risk Group" target="Prognosis">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A patient's risk group serves as the direct foundation for establishing their prognosis and guiding treatment pathways.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical prognosis, risk stratification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PSA Density" target="Transrectal Ultrasound">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Transrectal ultrasound is used to measure prostate volume, which is required to calculate PSA density.</data>
      <data key="d6">imaging measurement, clinical calculation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Gleason Score" target="Gleason Pattern">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The two most prevalent Gleason patterns identified in a biopsy sample are added together to form the Gleason score.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathology, scoring metric</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Gleason Score" target="Grade Group System">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Gleason scores directly determine the assigned Grade Group, reflecting cancer aggressiveness and risk level.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathology grading, risk stratification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Gleason Score" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The biopsy sample tissue is examined to determine the cancer pattern that dictates the Gleason score.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic procedure, scoring system</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Gleason Score" target="Grade Group 1">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Grade Group 1 is assigned specifically to cancer with a Gleason score of 6 to denote the lowest risk level.</data>
      <data key="d6">risk assignment, scoring categorization</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Gleason Score" target="Grade Group 5">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Grade Group 5 represents the highest score category, designated for biopsy samples with Gleason scores of 9 or 10.</data>
      <data key="d6">high risk classification, severity scoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Gleason Pattern" target="Pathologist">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A pathologist evaluates biopsy samples under a microscope and assigns Gleason pattern numbers based on cell appearance.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical analysis, cell grading</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prognosis" target="Diagnosis">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Diagnosis identifies the specific illness, whereas prognosis forecasts the likely course and outcome of that diagnosed disease.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical distinction, disease management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Grade Group System" target="Grade Group 4">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Grade Group 4 is an integral category within the Grade Group system that denotes high risk.</data>
      <data key="d6">classification system, risk staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Grade Group 2" target="Grade Group 3">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Grade Group 2 and Grade Group 3 both reflect a Gleason score of 7, but Grade Group 3 is more serious because its primary pattern has a higher score.</data>
      <data key="d6">comparative grading, prognosis differentiation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BRCA2 Mutation" target="Family History Of Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A family history of cancer is linked to inherited mutations such as the BRCA2 gene that elevate cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d6">hereditary risk, genetic testing</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Top Cancer Centers" target="Care Team">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The care team follows best practices established by research and methods developed at top cancer centers.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical standards, clinical guidelines</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Family Member" target="Care Team">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The care team gathers medical and cancer histories concerning blood family members to evaluate genetic risk.</data>
      <data key="d6">hereditary risk assessment, patient evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Health History Assessment" target="Care Team">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The care team collects and reviews a patient's health history to plan testing and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical evaluation, clinical workflow</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PSMA-PET Scan" target="Targeted Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PSMA-PET imaging is used to establish eligibility and suitability for targeted therapy in metastatic cases.</data>
      <data key="d6">therapeutic guidance, treatment eligibility</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PSMA-PET Scan" target="University of Chicago Medicine">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">University of Chicago Medicine provided comparative imaging demonstrating the detection capabilities of PSMA-PET scans.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical imaging, diagnostic reference</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Quality Of Life" target="Doctor">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The doctor asks patients detailed symptom questions to assess the impact of disease on their quality of life.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical evaluation, patient assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Quality Of Life" target="Multidisciplinary Care">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Multidisciplinary team care focuses on addressing patient health comprehensively to maintain and enhance overall quality of life.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient-centered care, holistic health</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hereditary Gene Testing" target="Hereditary Mutation">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hereditary gene testing directly looks for germline mutations present in all cells of the patient.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic testing, germline mutation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Tumor Testing" target="FANCA">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">FANCA abnormalities are sought during tumor profiling to assess suitability for targeted cancer therapies.</data>
      <data key="d6">tumor profiling, biomarker identification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM System" target="Cancer Staging">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The TNM system is the standard tool used by healthcare providers to assess and determine cancer staging.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical classification, diagnostic framework</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM System" target="Imaging Tests">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Results from imaging tests provide critical data on tumor size, node involvement, and metastases to calculate a TNM score.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic evaluation, staging data</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM System" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biopsies provide tissue samples whose analysis contributes to establishing a patient's cancer diagnosis and TNM staging score.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic testing, histological staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM System" target="Doctor">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Doctors utilize the TNM system to assign numerical scores and establish an overall stage for bone cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical assessment, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM System" target="T Category">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The T category serves as the primary tumor evaluation component of the broader TNM staging system.</data>
      <data key="d6">classification component, tumor assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM System" target="N Category">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The N category is the lymph node involvement component within the TNM staging system.</data>
      <data key="d6">classification component, nodal disease</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM System" target="M Category">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The M category is the metastasis indicator component within the TNM staging system.</data>
      <data key="d6">classification component, metastasis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM System" target="Bone Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The TNM system is the standard classification framework used to stage and evaluate bone cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">oncology staging, clinical management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Androgen Deprivation Therapy" target="Orchiectomy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Orchiectomy is a surgical method of achieving androgen deprivation therapy by removing the testicles to drastically reduce testosterone.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical intervention, castration method</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Androgen Deprivation Therapy" target="LHRH Agonist">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">LHRH agonists are a recognized pharmacological class utilized to achieve androgen deprivation.</data>
      <data key="d6">pharmacotherapy, hormone suppression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Androgen Deprivation Therapy" target="LHRH Antagonist">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">LHRH antagonists are pharmaceutical agents used to deliver androgen deprivation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">pharmacotherapy, hormone suppression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Androgen Deprivation Therapy" target="Anti-Androgen">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Anti-androgens are combined with LHRH agonists within androgen deprivation regimens to block testosterone action.</data>
      <data key="d6">combination pharmacotherapy, androgen blockade</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Androgen Deprivation Therapy" target="LHRH Agonists">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">LHRH agonists represent a major class of treatments utilized within androgen deprivation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment classification, drug class</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Androgen Deprivation Therapy" target="LHRH Antagonists">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">LHRH antagonists are a category of therapeutic agents utilized in androgen deprivation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment classification, drug class</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Androgen Deprivation Therapy" target="Anti-Androgens">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Anti-androgens are administered alongside or as part of androgen deprivation therapy regimens to block androgen activity.</data>
      <data key="d6">combination therapy, hormone suppression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Androgen Deprivation Therapy" target="Surgical Castration">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Surgical castration serves as a foundational form of androgen deprivation therapy by halting testicular testosterone synthesis.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical therapy, testosterone ablation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Non-Hormone Therapy" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy is classified as one of the primary non-hormonal treatment options for advanced prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment class, medical oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Non-Hormone Therapy" target="Immunotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Immunotherapy is recognized as a non-hormone therapeutic option for managing advanced prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment class, immune system therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Non-Hormone Therapy" target="Targeted Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Targeted therapy is a non-hormonal approach used in treating advanced prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment class, precision medicine</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Non-Hormone Therapy" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy serves as a core non-hormonal modality for managing advanced prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment class, radiation oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Orchiectomy" target="Testicles">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Orchiectomy is the surgical removal of one or both testicles to prevent testosterone production.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical procedure, organ removal</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Staging" target="TNM Scores">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cancer staging relies on TNM scores to describe tumor size, lymph node spread, and distant metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d6">staging classification, clinical evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Staging" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Results from biopsies, alongside imaging tests, provide essential information for staging cancer and formulating treatment plans.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic input, clinical assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Staging" target="Bone Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Bone cancer severity and extent are classified using the T, N, M, and G staging scores.</data>
      <data key="d6">oncological staging, clinical scoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Staging" target="Care Team">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The care team uses cancer staging to understand how far the cancer has advanced and to guide treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;The care team uses staging results to determine how far the cancer has grown and to create an individualized treatment plan.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical evaluation, treatment planning</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Staging" target="Cancer Grading">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cancer staging and grading are complementary measures used jointly to assess cancer spread and cellular growth speed.</data>
      <data key="d6">tumor classification, prognostic profiling</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Staging" target="Non-Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cancer staging distinguishes non-muscle-invasive bladder cancer, comprising stage 0 and stage 1, from deeper invasive forms.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical taxonomy, disease classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Staging" target="Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cancer staging categorizes stages 2, 3, and 4 as muscle-invasive bladder cancer requiring more intensive care.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical taxonomy, disease classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LHRH Agonists" target="Endocrine Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">LHRH agonists are utilized in endocrine therapy to suppress hormone production in premenopausal patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">ovarian suppression, hormone therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LHRH Agonists" target="Ovarian Suppression">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Ovarian suppression is clinically achieved through the administration of LHRH agonist medications.</data>
      <data key="d6">mechanism of action, therapeutic intervention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Invasive Breast Cancer" target="Metastatic Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Invasive breast cancer can progress into metastatic breast cancer as malignant cells spread to lymph nodes and distant organs.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic breast cancer represents the advanced spread of invasive breast cancer outside the breast and axilla to other organs.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease progression, cancer staging&lt;SEP&gt;disease progression, metastasis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Invasive Breast Cancer" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biopsy procedures provide critical diagnostic test results required to detect invasive breast cancer and formulate treatment plans.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic testing, treatment planning</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Invasive Breast Cancer" target="Imaging Tests">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Imaging test results are evaluated alongside biopsy findings to guide treatment planning for breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic evaluation, medical assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Invasive Breast Cancer" target="Care Team">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The care team gathers diagnostic information, interprets test results, and determines the personalized treatment plan for breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient care, clinical management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Invasive Breast Cancer" target="Genetic Cancer Risk Testing">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Genetic cancer risk testing is performed to determine hereditary risks associated with breast cancer occurrence.</data>
      <data key="d6">hereditary risk, genetic testing</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Invasive Breast Cancer" target="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biomarker testing on biopsy tissue determines specific biological features of invasive breast cancer to establish tailored treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathology assessment, individualized care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Invasive Breast Cancer" target="Ductal Carcinoma In Situ">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Ductal carcinoma in situ is treated proactively to prevent it from progressing into invasive breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease progression, preventive treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Invasive Breast Cancer" target="NCCN">
