      <data key="d7">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Breast Cancer Screening" target="Physical Exam">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Physical examinations form a core part of comprehensive breast health assessments and screening.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical assessment, screening routine</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Breast Cancer Screening" target="Health Checkup">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Breast cancer screenings are frequently ordered or performed as part of routine regular health checkups.</data>
      <data key="d6">routine checkup, early detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Invasive Ductal Carcinoma" target="Inflammatory Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Most cases of inflammatory breast cancer are classified histologically as invasive ductal carcinomas.&lt;SEP&gt;Most inflammatory breast cancers are pathologically classified as invasive ductal carcinomas.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathological classification, disease subtype&lt;SEP&gt;pathology, cancer classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inflammatory Breast Cancer" target="Peau d'Orange">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Peau d'orange is a distinct clinical manifestation and symptom of inflammatory breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical signs, symptomatic manifestation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inflammatory Breast Cancer" target="Edema">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Edema causing breast swelling is a primary physical sign of inflammatory breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">symptomatology, clinical presentation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inflammatory Breast Cancer" target="Erythema">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Erythema causing redness and warmth on breast skin is a common presentation of inflammatory breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">symptomatology, dermatological signs</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inflammatory Breast Cancer" target="HER2-Positive Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Inflammatory breast cancer frequently overexpresses HER2 proteins, often exhibiting HER2-positive characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease correlation, pathological association</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inflammatory Breast Cancer" target="Breast Cancer Staging">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Inflammatory breast cancer is evaluated and staged within the breast cancer staging framework, presenting at stage 3 or 4 at diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, clinical staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inflammatory Breast Cancer" target="Stage 3 Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Inflammatory breast cancer is typically classified as stage 3 or stage 4 disease upon initial diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, clinical presentation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inflammatory Breast Cancer" target="Stage 4 Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Inflammatory breast cancer often presents as stage 4 when metastasis to other organs has occurred.</data>
      <data key="d6">metastatic disease, cancer progression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inflammatory Breast Cancer" target="Tumor Size (T)">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Inflammatory breast cancer is classified under the primary tumor size category as T4d.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, clinical categorization</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inflammatory Breast Cancer" target="Systemic Therapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Inflammatory breast cancer is initially treated with systemic therapy to shrink the tumor prior to surgical intervention.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer treatment, therapeutic sequence</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inflammatory Breast Cancer" target="Modified Radical Mastectomy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Modified radical mastectomy is often the surgical procedure used to treat inflammatory breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical treatment, clinical management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inflammatory Breast Cancer" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy is administered following surgery as part of the standard multimodal treatment plan for inflammatory breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">postoperative care, multimodal treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inflammatory Breast Cancer" target="Care Team">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The care team assesses tumor characteristics and develops individualized treatment plans for patients with inflammatory breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient care, clinical oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inflammatory Breast Cancer" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy regimens, often multi-agent combinations, are used to treat inflammatory breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease management, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Imaging Technologists" target="Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI)">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Imaging technologists run MRI machines to capture diagnostic images.</data>
      <data key="d6">equipment operation, clinical diagnostics</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Edema" target="Glioma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">As gliomas expand into brain tissue, they can cause swelling and edema around the tumor.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathophysiology, tumor effect</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inflammatory Breast Cancer (IBC)" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A skin biopsy is a critical diagnostic procedure used to confirm the presence of inflammatory breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical diagnosis, pathology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inflammatory Breast Cancer (IBC)" target="Imaging Tests">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Imaging tests are used to stage inflammatory breast cancer and assess tumor spread.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical imaging, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inflammatory Breast Cancer (IBC)" target="Clinical Breast Exam (CBE)">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A clinical breast exam is used to evaluate physical signs of inflammatory breast cancer, including redness, swelling, and lymph node enlargement.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical evaluation, symptom assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inflammatory Breast Cancer (IBC)" target="Nurse Patient">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The nurse patient experienced delayed diagnosis before being confirmed to have inflammatory breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient experience, diagnosis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inflammatory Breast Cancer (IBC)" target="Care Team">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The care team manages treatment planning, clinical communication, and ongoing diagnostic testing for IBC patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient management, clinical care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Clinical Breast Exam (CBE)" target="Physical Exam">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A clinical breast exam is a specific component of a physical examination focused on breast and underarm tissue.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical examination, clinical procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Primary Care Physician (PCP)" target="Care Team">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Patients are advised to coordinate between their primary care physician and their oncology care team.</data>
      <data key="d6">healthcare coordination, communication</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hormone Receptor Status" target="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biomarker testing on biopsy tissue determines estrogen and progesterone receptor status to dictate targeted therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormone receptor status is measured alongside other biomarkers to direct breast cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">biomarker evaluation, breast cancer characterization&lt;SEP&gt;receptor profiling, targeted treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HER2 Status" target="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biomarker testing measures HER2 overexpression to identify candidates for targeted anti-HER2 therapies.</data>
      <data key="d6">biomarker assessment, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HER2 Status" target="Prognostic Stage">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Prognostic stage calculations include HER2 biomarker evaluation alongside anatomic metrics.</data>
      <data key="d6">biomarker assessment, clinical prognosis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (CMP)" target="Blood Tests">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CMP is a key diagnostic blood panel used to evaluate metabolic function and liver enzymes before and during treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">biochemical testing, organ assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Breast MRI" target="Imaging Tests">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Breast MRI is indicated as an advanced imaging study to further visualize breast tissue abnormalities in IBC.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic imaging, soft tissue evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET-CT" target="Imaging Tests">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PET-CT is used as part of systemic imaging to stage inflammatory breast cancer and detect distant metastases.</data>
      <data key="d6">staging, oncology imaging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Nurse" target="Care Team">
      <data key="d4">15.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Nurses are members of the care team who facilitate patient communication and care delivery.&lt;SEP&gt;Nurses work alongside providers as part of the patient's care team during examinations and procedures.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical collaboration, patient care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Nurse" target="Breast Exam">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A nurse or assistant may be present in the examination room while the provider conducts a breast exam.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical assistance, examination support</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Alkaline Phosphatase Test" target="Blood Tests">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Alkaline phosphatase testing is a specific blood test performed to check for disease markers.&lt;SEP&gt;The alkaline phosphatase test is a specific laboratory blood test described for detecting bone or liver disease.</data>
      <data key="d6">laboratory diagnostics, blood analysis&lt;SEP&gt;laboratory diagnostics, blood evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bone X-ray" target="Care Team">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The care team may order bone x-rays if a patient experiences bone pain or displays abnormalities on a bone scan.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical order, clinical decision</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET-CT Scan" target="Sodium Fluoride PET-CT">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Sodium fluoride PET-CT is a specialized variation of a PET-CT scan.</data>
      <data key="d6">modality variant, diagnostic subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PET-CT Scan" target="CT Scan">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PET-CT combines PET imaging with a CT scan to produce detailed diagnostic images.</data>
      <data key="d6">imaging combination, diagnostic procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Sodium Fluoride PET-CT" target="FDG PET-CT">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clear bone findings on an FDG PET-CT may eliminate the need for a sodium fluoride PET-CT or bone scan.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic alternative, imaging comparison</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FDG PET-CT" target="FES PET-CT">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">FES PET-CT can be used as an alternative to FDG PET-CT for estrogen receptor-positive cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic alternative, radiotracer substitution</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Vacuum-Assisted Core Biopsy" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Vacuum-assisted core biopsy is a specialized procedure under biopsy methodologies.</data>
      <data key="d6">sub-type, diagnostic procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Excisional Biopsy" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Excisional biopsy is an invasive subtype of biopsy where an entire lesion is removed.</data>
      <data key="d6">sub-type, surgical biopsy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Excisional Biopsy" target="Small Lymphocytic Lymphoma (SLL)">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Excisional biopsy removes a whole lymph node to test and confirm small lymphocytic lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">lymph node examination, surgical confirmation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Excisional Biopsy" target="Inguinal Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">An excisional biopsy may be performed to excise all or part of a lymph node for pathology analysis.</data>
      <data key="d6">biopsy procedure, tissue removal</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Incisional Biopsy" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Incisional biopsy is a subtype of biopsy where a section of abnormal tissue is surgically sampled.</data>
      <data key="d6">sub-type, surgical biopsy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Incisional Biopsy" target="CLL">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Incisional biopsy removes a section of a lymph node to assist in confirming CLL when blood tests are not conclusive.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical biopsy, tissue testing</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mastectomy" target="Surgical Oncologist">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Surgical oncologists perform surgical resections such as mastectomy to treat breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical oncology, operative intervention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mastectomy" target="Local Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Local therapy includes mastectomy as a primary surgical method to target cancer in the breast.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment classification, surgical procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mastectomy" target="Modified Radical Mastectomy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Modified radical mastectomy is a specific form of mastectomy involving the removal of the breast and axillary lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedure variation, surgical classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mastectomy" target="Breast Reconstruction">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Breast reconstruction is offered as an elective procedure following a mastectomy, usually after cancer treatments are complete.</data>
      <data key="d6">reconstructive surgery, postoperative sequence</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Open Biopsy" target="Pathologist">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Tissue removed through an open biopsy is evaluated by a pathologist to establish tumor histology.</data>
      <data key="d6">histological analysis, diagnostic surgery</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Open Biopsy" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Open biopsy is classified as one of two standard forms of biopsy for gliomas when resection cannot be carried out.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedural subtype, diagnostic surgery</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Open Biopsy" target="Neurosurgeon">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The neurosurgeon performs an open biopsy by cutting the scalp, removing bone, and excising tumor tissue.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical procedure, medical execution</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Open Biopsy" target="Craniotomy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">An open biopsy involves performing a craniotomy to access brain tissue.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical procedure, operative technique</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Open Biopsy" target="Resection">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">An open biopsy is compared methodologically to a surgical resection because both involve craniotomy.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical comparison, operative method</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hormone Receptor-Positive Breast Cancer" target="Endocrine Therapy">
      <data key="d4">19.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Endocrine therapy is designed and prescribed specifically to treat hormone receptor-positive breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Endocrine therapy specifically treats hormone receptor-positive breast cancer by blocking hormone receptor signaling.</data>
      <data key="d6">targeted treatment, cancer therapy&lt;SEP&gt;therapeutic indication, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hormone Receptor-Positive Breast Cancer" target="Hormone Receptor Testing">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hormone receptor testing detects estrogen and progesterone receptors to classify tumors as hormone receptor-positive.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathology diagnostic, cancer classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hormone Receptor-Negative Breast Cancer" target="Hormone Receptor Testing">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hormone receptor testing verifies the absence of both estrogen and progesterone receptors to identify hormone receptor-negative cases.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathology diagnostic, cancer classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Estrogen Receptor-Positive Breast Cancer" target="Endocrine Therapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Endocrine therapy inhibits estrogen receptor signaling to starve and eradicate estrogen receptor-positive cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">hormone deprivation, therapeutic intervention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HER2-Positive Breast Cancer" target="In Situ Hybridization">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">In situ hybridization determines HER2 gene copy numbers to assess HER2-positivity when immunohistochemistry results are equivocal.</data>
      <data key="d6">genomic testing, confirmatory diagnostic</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HER2-Positive Breast Cancer" target="HER2-Targeted Therapy">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">HER2-targeted therapy is specifically designed to treat breast cancer that overexpresses the HER2 protein.</data>
      <data key="d6">targeted therapy, biomarker alignment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Endocrine Therapy" target="ER-Low-Positive Invasive Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Endocrine therapy may not be recommended for ER-low-positive invasive breast cancer depending on receptor quantification.</data>
      <data key="d6">therapeutic indication, clinical management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Endocrine Therapy" target="Systemic Therapy">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Endocrine therapy is categorized as a systemic treatment administered according to hormone receptor expression.&lt;SEP&gt;Endocrine therapy is classified as a major component of systemic cancer therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">hormone therapy, systemic classification&lt;SEP&gt;treatment classification, oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Endocrine Therapy" target="CDK4/6 Inhibitors">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CDK4/6 inhibitors are combined with endocrine therapy to extend cancer control in hormone receptor-positive disease.</data>
      <data key="d6">combination therapy, cancer control</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Endocrine Therapy" target="mTOR Inhibitors">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">mTOR inhibitors are used to restore sensitivity to endocrine therapy when hormone receptor-positive cancer develops resistance.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment resistance, endocrine sensitizing</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Endocrine Therapy" target="MTOR Inhibitors">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">mTOR inhibitors are combined with endocrine therapy to help restore its effectiveness when resistance develops.</data>
      <data key="d6">combination therapy, drug synergy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Endocrine Therapy" target="Aromatase Inhibitors">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Aromatase inhibitors represent a primary drug class used within endocrine therapy regimens.</data>
      <data key="d6">drug category, treatment subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Endocrine Therapy" target="Estrogen Receptor Modulators">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Estrogen receptor modulators serve as a core therapeutic class in endocrine therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">drug category, treatment subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Endocrine Therapy" target="Bilateral Oophorectomy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Bilateral oophorectomy is a permanent surgical method of endocrine therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical therapy, ovarian ablation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Endocrine Therapy" target="Ovarian Ablation">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Ovarian ablation is a non-surgical, radiation-based method of endocrine therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation therapy, hormone suppression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Endocrine Therapy" target="Testosterone-Suppressing Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">For patients assigned male at birth, endocrine therapy combines tamoxifen or aromatase inhibitors with testosterone-suppressing therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">combination therapy, hormonal suppression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Tumor Marker Testing" target="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Tumor marker testing in blood or biopsy tissue forms part of broader biomarker and molecular assessments in cancer care.</data>
      <data key="d6">biomarker assessment, oncology profiling</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Tumor Mutational Burden" target="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Tumor mutational burden is an assessment within genomic and biomarker testing used to evaluate cancer treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d6">genomic profiling, biomarker measurement</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Tumor Mutational Burden" target="PD-1 And PD-L1 Testing">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">High tumor mutational burden and PD-1/PD-L1 expression are both used to evaluate response to immune checkpoint inhibitor therapies.</data>
      <data key="d6">immunotherapy markers, treatment response</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PD-1 And PD-L1 Testing" target="PD-1">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PD-1 and PD-L1 testing checks whether programmed death protein 1 is expressed on tumor cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">protein expression, diagnostic assay</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PD-1 And PD-L1 Testing" target="PD-L1">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Testing assesses programmed death-ligand 1 levels to guide the use of checkpoint inhibitor therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">protein expression, diagnostic assay</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FISH" target="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">FISH is a specialized molecular method utilized within biomarker testing to analyze DNA in cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">laboratory technique, diagnostic method</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FISH" target="CLL">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">FISH is a required diagnostic genetic test used to plan personalized treatment for patients with CLL.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic testing, treatment planning</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Targeted Therapies" target="PIK3CA">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Specific targeted therapies are selected to treat cancers harboring PIK3CA mutations.</data>
      <data key="d6">targeted treatment, mutation therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Targeted Therapies" target="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biomarker testing reveals tumor properties that dictate patient eligibility for targeted therapies.</data>
      <data key="d6">molecular diagnostics, targeted therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Targeted Therapies" target="Systemic Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Targeted therapies are systemic agents administered based on biomarker profiles.</data>
      <data key="d6">systemic oncology, targeted intervention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HER2" target="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">HER2 expression is assessed via biomarker testing in recurrent cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;HER2 status is evaluated as a core component of breast cancer biomarker testing.</data>
      <data key="d6">biomarker assessment, clinical evaluation&lt;SEP&gt;molecular analysis, targeted therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PD-L1" target="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biomarker testing evaluates PD-L1 expression to qualify patients for targeted therapies or immunotherapies.</data>
      <data key="d6">molecular testing, biomarker identification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PD-L1" target="Immunotherapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Elevated PD-L1 expression enables treatment with specialized immunotherapies.</data>
      <data key="d6">immune checkpoint, targeted treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PD-L1" target="Targeted Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PD-L1 positivity makes targeted therapeutic options available for recurrent or metastatic disease.</data>
      <data key="d6">biomarker targeting, medical oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Breast Cancer Staging" target="Stage 3 Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 3 is an advanced category within the breast cancer staging system.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Breast Cancer Staging" target="Stage 4 Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 4 represents metastatic disease within the breast cancer staging hierarchy.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, metastasis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hereditary Genetic Testing" target="Hereditary Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hereditary genetic testing identifies the gene mutations responsible for hereditary breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic testing, hereditary disease</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Biomarker Status" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Tissue obtained during a biopsy is tested to establish estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor, and HER2 biomarker status.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic testing, tissue pathology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prognostic Stage" target="Progesterone Receptor Status">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Progesterone receptor status provides biomarker data required to define the overall prognostic stage.</data>
      <data key="d6">biomarker assessment, clinical prognosis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prognostic Stage" target="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biomarker testing reveals genetic and protein markers that determine the prognostic stage of breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">laboratory diagnostics, prognostic profiling</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Tumor Size (T)" target="Tumor Micrometastasis">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Primary tumor micrometastases smaller than 1 mm are classified under the T criteria as T1mi.</data>
      <data key="d6">tumor measurement, micrometastasis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lymph Node Status (N)" target="Axillary Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Axillary nodes under the arm are key regional lymph nodes examined to determine N staging.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical site, lymphatic metastasis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lymph Node Status (N)" target="Internal Mammary Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cancer presence in internal mammary nodes near the breastbone informs the regional N score.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical site, clinical evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lymph Node Status (N)" target="Infraclavicular Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Involvement of infraclavicular nodes is factored into the regional lymph node staging classification.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical site, lymphatic involvement</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lymph Node Status (N)" target="Supraclavicular Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Supraclavicular lymph node involvement near the collarbone is evaluated during clinical lymph node staging.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical site, advanced regional spread</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lymph Node Status (N)" target="Isolated Tumor Cells (ITCs)">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The presence of isolated tumor cells is a specific histological finding classified under N0 status.</data>
      <data key="d6">histopathological classification, lymph node status</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lymph Node Status (N)" target="Lymph Node Micrometastasis">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lymph node micrometastases represent small malignant cell clusters categorized as N1mi.</data>
      <data key="d6">micrometastasis, lymph node classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HER2-Targeted Therapy" target="Systemic Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">HER2-targeted therapy is a component of systemic therapy based on the patient's HER2 expression.</data>
      <data key="d6">targeted treatment, systemic classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HER2-Targeted Therapy" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">HER2-targeted therapies are frequently administered alongside chemotherapy regimens.</data>
      <data key="d6">combination therapy, treatment protocol</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HER2-Targeted Therapy" target="HER2 Antibodies">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">HER2 antibodies represent a primary subcategory of HER2-targeted therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">drug classification, targeted therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HER2-Targeted Therapy" target="HER2 Inhibitors">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">HER2 inhibitors represent an intracellular-acting category of HER2-targeted therapies.</data>
      <data key="d6">drug classification, targeted therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HER2-Targeted Therapy" target="Antibody Drug Conjugates">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Antibody-drug conjugates are advanced therapeutic delivery vehicles classified under HER2-targeted therapies.</data>
      <data key="d6">drug classification, targeted therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HER2-Targeted Therapy" target="Left Ventricular Ejection Fraction Test">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">HER2-targeted agents such as trastuzumab necessitate left ventricular ejection fraction testing to monitor cardiac safety.</data>
      <data key="d6">cardiac monitoring, patient safety</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Local Therapy" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Local therapy includes radiation therapy to treat localized areas including the breast, chest wall, and lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment classification, localized therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Local Therapy" target="Metastatic Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Local therapies are used to treat targeted tumor lesions in organs like the liver, lungs, or bone in metastatic cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease management, local treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Modified Radical Mastectomy" target="Regional Nodal Irradiation">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Regional nodal irradiation and modified radical mastectomy address breast tissue and draining lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">locoregional control, cancer surgery</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inhibitor Therapy" target="Systemic Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Inhibitor therapy, targeting pathways like CDK4/6, mTOR, and PARP, is an established form of systemic therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">therapeutic category, systemic treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Breast Reconstruction" target="Plastic Surgeon">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A plastic surgeon is consulted to plan and execute staged or delayed breast reconstruction.</data>
      <data key="d6">specialized surgery, reconstruction consultation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Centers and Hospitals" target="Care Team">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cancer centers and hospitals house the clinical teams and specialized surgeons that deliver multimodal treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">institutional care, clinical expertise</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Myeloid Growth Factors" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Myeloid growth factors are administered to counteract infection risk and restore white blood cells depleted by chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">supportive care, side effect management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Myeloid Growth Factors" target="NCCN">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN publishes patient guidelines and resources covering the use of myeloid growth factors for anemia and neutropenia.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical guidelines, patient education</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CDK4/6 Inhibitors" target="Systemic Therapy">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CDK4/6 inhibitors are a class of targeted systemic drugs used in combination with endocrine therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">targeted therapy, systemic treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CDK4/6 Inhibitors" target="Ovarian Suppression">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Premenopausal patients prescribed CDK4/6 inhibitor regimens must also receive ovarian suppression or ablation.</data>
      <data key="d6">hormone management, therapeutic protocol</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="mTOR Inhibitors" target="Systemic Therapy">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">mTOR inhibitors are systemic targeted therapies used to re-sensitize hormone receptor-positive cancer to endocrine treatments.</data>
      <data key="d6">targeted therapy, treatment resistance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Herbal Supplements" target="Drug Interaction">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Herbal supplements can interfere with systemic therapies, leading to problematic drug interactions.</data>
      <data key="d6">pharmacology, adverse interaction</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Ovarian Suppression" target="Premenopause">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Ovarian suppression is applied to premenopausal patients to temporarily halt ovarian hormone secretion during cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">hormonal control, oncology management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Drug Interaction" target="Systemic Therapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Systemic therapies are vulnerable to drug interactions caused by supplements, antacids, blood pressure medications, or antidepressants.</data>
      <data key="d6">safety monitoring, drug metabolism</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Aromatase Inhibitors" target="Steroidal Aromatase Inhibitors">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Steroidal aromatase inhibitors constitute a specific pharmacological subdivision of aromatase inhibitors.</data>
      <data key="d6">drug subcategory, pharmacology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Aromatase Inhibitors" target="Non-Steroidal Aromatase Inhibitors">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Non-steroidal aromatase inhibitors constitute a specific pharmacological subdivision of aromatase inhibitors.</data>
      <data key="d6">drug subcategory, pharmacology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Estrogen Receptor Modulators" target="Selective Estrogen Receptor Modulators">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Selective estrogen receptor modulators (SERMs) are a major subclass of estrogen receptor modulators.</data>
      <data key="d6">pharmacological classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Estrogen Receptor Modulators" target="Selective Estrogen Receptor Degraders">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Selective estrogen receptor degraders (SERDs) are a major subclass of estrogen receptor modulators.</data>
      <data key="d6">pharmacological classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Primary CNS Lymphoma" target="Secondary CNS Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Both are forms of central nervous system lymphoma, differentiated by whether the cancer originates directly within the CNS or metastasizes there from another body site.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, origin differentiation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Primary CNS Lymphoma" target="Immunodeficiency Disorder">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Having an immunodeficiency disorder weakens the immune system and serves as a significant risk factor for developing primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">risk factor, immune suppression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Primary CNS Lymphoma" target="HIV">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">HIV is an immunodeficiency virus frequently linked to an increased risk of developing primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">viral association, disease risk</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Primary CNS Lymphoma" target="Epstein-Barr Virus">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Carrying the Epstein-Barr virus is identified as a risk factor associated with the development of primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathogen association, risk factor</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Primary CNS Lymphoma" target="Autoimmune Disease">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Autoimmune diseases are classified as risk factors that can increase the likelihood of developing primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease risk, immune dysfunction</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Primary CNS Lymphoma" target="Organ Transplant">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Undergoing an organ transplant requires immunosuppressive medication, increasing a patient's risk of developing primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment consequence, risk factor</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Primary CNS Lymphoma" target="Older Adults">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Older adults, particularly those in their 60s and 70s, comprise the primary demographic group diagnosed with primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">demographic prevalence, age risk</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Primary CNS Lymphoma" target="Brain Tumor">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Primary CNS lymphoma represents a type of brain tumor presentation affecting the central nervous system.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical classification, disease subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Primary CNS Lymphoma" target="Central Nervous System">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Primary CNS lymphoma develops within and can extend throughout parts of the central nervous system.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS lymphoma is a malignancy that originates directly within tissues of the central nervous system.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical localization, disease origin&lt;SEP&gt;disease localization, oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Primary CNS Lymphoma" target="Immune System">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Impairment or weakening of the immune system significantly elevates the risk of developing primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">immune deficiency, disease risk</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Primary CNS Lymphoma" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">44.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy is a core therapeutic modality employed to treat primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemotherapy is a core treatment modality for primary central nervous system lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemotherapy is a key treatment modality used, either alone, in combinations, or intrathecally, to treat primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemotherapy is a major therapeutic modality used to treat primary CNS lymphoma across various treatment protocols.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemotherapy is one of the main standard treatment modalities utilized to manage and treat primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer therapy, treatment protocol&lt;SEP&gt;cancer treatment, medical therapy&lt;SEP&gt;cancer treatment, therapeutic option&lt;SEP&gt;medical treatment, therapeutic intervention&lt;SEP&gt;oncological treatment, drug therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Primary CNS Lymphoma" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">41.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy is a primary treatment modality employed to treat tumors associated with primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiation therapy is an established treatment option for primary CNS lymphoma during induction or consolidation phases.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiation therapy is one of the key treatment options available for managing primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiation therapy is used as a therapeutic modality to treat primary CNS lymphoma in the brain and eyes.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiation therapy represents a key therapeutic modality used to target and control primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer treatment, therapeutic intervention&lt;SEP&gt;medical treatment, therapeutic intervention&lt;SEP&gt;oncology treatment, radiation&lt;SEP&gt;oncology, treatment option&lt;SEP&gt;radiotherapy, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Primary CNS Lymphoma" target="Stem Cell Transplant">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stem cell transplantation is listed as a major therapeutic option for patients with primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical treatment, intensive procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Primary CNS Lymphoma" target="Clinical Trials">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
