      <data key="d7">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Central Nervous System" target="Glioma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Gliomas originate within the central nervous system as determined by histologic analysis.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical origin, tumor pathology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AIDS" target="Human Papillomavirus (HPV)">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">AIDS impairs the body's immune capacity to clear HPV infections.</data>
      <data key="d6">immunosuppression, infection persistence</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Neurological Exam" target="Eye Exam">
      <data key="d4">15.0</data>
      <data key="d5">An eye exam is conducted alongside or within the neurological exam to evaluate nerve health.&lt;SEP&gt;An eye exam is performed as part of or in conjunction with the neurological evaluation to inspect the cranial and optic nerves.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical examination, sensory evaluation&lt;SEP&gt;diagnostic evaluation, medical exam</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Neurological Exam" target="Physical Examination">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The physical examination encompasses initial general assessments including the neurological exam.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic protocol, medical examination</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Neurological Exam" target="Care Team">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Healthcare providers conduct neurological examinations to evaluate patient cognitive and physical functions.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical assessment, symptom evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Eye Exam" target="Ophthalmologist">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">An ophthalmologist conducts the eye exam using a slit lamp to closely examine the interior structures of the patient's eyes.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical examination, clinical specialist</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Relapse" target="Stem Cell Rescue">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stem cell rescue is specifically considered when primary CNS lymphoma relapses after earlier therapies.</data>
      <data key="d6">relapse protocol, salvage therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Magnetic Resonance Imaging" target="Gadolinium">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Gadolinium is the specific contrast agent injected during an MRI scan to enhance visualization of CNS tumors.</data>
      <data key="d6">contrast enhancement, diagnostic procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Magnetic Resonance Imaging" target="Computed Tomography">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Computed tomography acts as a secondary imaging alternative when magnetic resonance imaging is contraindicated or unavailable.&lt;SEP&gt;MRI is ordered as an alternative or complementary scan to CT when CT results are inconclusive or when specific areas like the rectum or liver require detailed imaging.</data>
      <data key="d6">comparative diagnostic imaging, secondary assessment&lt;SEP&gt;imaging alternatives, diagnostics</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Magnetic Resonance Imaging" target="Stereotactic Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Computerized surgical navigation systems use MRI scans to guide the stereotactic biopsy needle precisely to the brain tumor.</data>
      <data key="d6">image-guided surgery, procedural targeting</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Magnetic Resonance Imaging" target="Positron Emission Tomography">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PET scans may be combined with MRI to evaluate the spread of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Positron emission tomography can be combined with MRI to evaluate cancerous tissue.</data>
      <data key="d6">imaging modalities, diagnostic evaluation&lt;SEP&gt;multimodal imaging, diagnostic assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Magnetic Resonance Imaging" target="Anal Squamous Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Magnetic resonance imaging is utilized to evaluate soft tissue and determine the local extent of anal cancer in the pelvis.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic imaging, tumor assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Computed Tomography" target="Positron Emission Tomography">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PET scans are frequently combined with CT scans (PET/CT) to produce clearer imaging of cancer activity and anatomy.&lt;SEP&gt;Positron emission tomography can be combined with CT scans to improve detection of malignant cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">multimodal imaging, diagnostic combination&lt;SEP&gt;multimodal imaging, hybrid diagnostic</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Computed Tomography" target="Technician">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The technician conducts the CT scan and communicates with the patient during the procedure.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedure operation, patient communication</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Computed Tomography" target="Imaging Tests">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Computed tomography is an imaging test modality utilized to determine the spread of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic modality, medical imaging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Computed Tomography" target="Anal Squamous Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Computed tomography is recommended to evaluate the extent of anal squamous cell carcinoma in the pelvis and detect distant spread.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic imaging, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stereotactic Biopsy" target="Neurosurgeon">
      <data key="d4">27.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A neurosurgeon conducts the stereotactic biopsy in an operating room to collect brain tumor samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Neurosurgeons carry out stereotactic biopsies using needles and precision markers in an operating room.&lt;SEP&gt;The neurosurgeon carries out the stereotactic biopsy using specialized navigation systems and instruments to extract tumor tissue.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical procedure, medical execution&lt;SEP&gt;surgical procedure, medical practitioner&lt;SEP&gt;surgical procedure, tissue sampling</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stereotactic Biopsy" target="Pathologist">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The tissue sample harvested during a stereotactic biopsy is analyzed by a pathologist to verify malignancy, cancer type, and grade.&lt;SEP&gt;Tissue removed during stereotactic biopsy is sent to a pathologist for microscopic evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d6">tissue analysis, laboratory pathology&lt;SEP&gt;tissue analysis, pathology review</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stereotactic Biopsy" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stereotactic biopsy is a specific form of standard biopsy used when tumors are located in vital or hard-to-reach areas.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedural subtype, surgical technique</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stereotactic Biopsy" target="Neuropathologist">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Tissue samples removed during a stereotactic biopsy are sent directly to the neuropathologist for microscopic and molecular evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d6">specimen analysis, diagnostic pathology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Neurosurgeon" target="Resection">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Neurosurgeons perform resection surgeries to remove tumor tissue from the brain.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical execution, treatment delivery</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Neurosurgeon" target="Neuropathologist">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The neuropathologist analyzes tissue samples and relays preliminary diagnostic results to the neurosurgeon during surgery to guide decision-making.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical collaboration, surgical guidance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Neurosurgeon" target="Craniotomy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The neurosurgeon executes the craniotomy during an open biopsy or surgical resection.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical execution, clinical provider</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Vitreoretinal Lymphoma" target="Vitrectomy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A vitrectomy is used to sample vitreous fluid to test for cancer cells and can confirm vitreoretinal lymphoma involvement without brain biopsy.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic procedure, tissue analysis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN Guidelines For Patients" target="NCCN">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN produces and conducts survey evaluations for the NCCN Guidelines for Patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Guidelines for Patients is produced, surveyed, and maintained by the National Comprehensive Cancer Network.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient resources, publication</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HIV Blood Test" target="Antiretroviral Therapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">HIV blood tests identify infections that necessitate concurrent administration of antiretroviral therapy during lymphoma care.</data>
      <data key="d6">infection screening, concurrent therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hepatitis B Testing" target="Immunotherapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hepatitis B testing is necessary before administering immunotherapy because the treatment can reactivate dormant virus.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment safety, viral reactivation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stem Cell Rescue" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">26.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy is an essential clinical component of undergoing a stem cell rescue.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemotherapy serves as an essential preliminary component of undergoing stem cell rescue therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Stem cell rescue directly follows high-dose chemotherapy to reconstitute the bone marrow destroyed during cancer cell eradication.</data>
      <data key="d6">combination therapy, treatment protocol&lt;SEP&gt;treatment dependency, medical procedure&lt;SEP&gt;treatment protocol, bone marrow restoration</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stem Cell Rescue" target="High-Dose Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">High-dose chemotherapy is a critical intermediate stage of stem cell rescue that destroys lymphoma cells and depletes the bone marrow prior to stem cell reinfusion.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedural phase, therapeutic protocol</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stem Cell Rescue" target="Stem Cell Removal">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stem cell removal is the first crucial phase of a stem cell rescue protocol, securing stem cells before high-dose therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedural step, stem cell collection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stem Cell Rescue" target="Stem Cell Replacement">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stem cell replacement is the final restorative step of a stem cell rescue that reinfuses healthy stem cells into the patient.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedural step, cellular reconstitution</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Psychological Distress" target="NCCN">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN publishes specialized guidelines for patients to address distress and mental health issues during cancer care.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient support, clinical resources</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Informed Consent" target="Clinical Trial">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Participants in clinical trials undergo an informed consent process and sign an agreement before enrolling.</data>
      <data key="d6">ethical protocol, patient onboarding</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Placebo" target="Clinical Trial">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Placebos are sometimes incorporated as comparison agents alongside standard treatments within cancer clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d6">trial design, comparative control</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Placebo" target="Clinical Trials">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Placebos are utilized in controlled clinical trials alongside standard treatments to assess true drug efficacy.</data>
      <data key="d6">control group, comparative methodology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Intraocular Chemotherapy" target="Eye Surgeon">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Intraocular chemotherapy can lead to cataracts that require surgical treatment performed by an eye surgeon.</data>
      <data key="d6">adverse effect management, surgical intervention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Intraocular Chemotherapy" target="Cataracts">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cataracts are a recognized adverse side effect that can develop as a complication of intraocular chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">drug toxicity, adverse complication</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Whole-Brain Radiation Therapy" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Whole-brain radiation therapy is a specific technique that falls under the broader category of radiation therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Whole-brain radiation therapy represents a specific technique and application of radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">therapy classification, treatment technique&lt;SEP&gt;treatment subtype, medical procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Whole-Brain Radiation Therapy" target="Ocular Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Whole-brain radiation therapy can be expanded to encompass ocular radiation therapy, or the eyes can receive focused, lower-dose radiation independently.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment adaptation, targeted radiotherapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Eye Surgeon" target="Cataracts">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">An eye surgeon conducts routine surgical procedures to treat and remove cataracts.&lt;SEP&gt;Cataracts caused by chemotherapy can be routinely corrected through surgery performed by an eye surgeon.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical treatment, surgical intervention&lt;SEP&gt;surgical treatment, clinical correction</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Induction Phase" target="Consolidation Phase">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The induction phase directly precedes the consolidation phase in the staging and sequencing of lymphoma treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment sequence, therapeutic phases</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Induction Phase" target="Ibrutinib">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Ibrutinib serves as an alternative induction therapy drug when high-dose methotrexate cannot be administered.</data>
      <data key="d6">alternative therapy, induction regimen</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Induction Phase" target="Temozolomide">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Temozolomide is used either as part of combination induction therapy or as an alternative agent.</data>
      <data key="d6">alternative medication, chemotherapy combination</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Consolidation Phase" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy can be employed during the consolidation phase to kill residual cancer cells following induction treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">secondary treatment, disease clearance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cataracts" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy applied to the eyes carries the side effect of causing cataracts.</data>
      <data key="d6">adverse effect, ocular complication</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chemotherapy Cycle" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy is structured into specific cycles alternating drug infusion and rest to permit bodily recovery.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment scheduling, therapeutic regimen</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="WBRT" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">WBRT is a specific form of radiation therapy that can be widened to encompass ocular lymphoma treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment classification, radiotherapy method</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rituximab" target="Immunotherapy">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Rituximab is the primary immunotherapy drug administered for primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Rituximab is the primary immunotherapy drug and monoclonal antibody used in treating primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">drug classification, immunotherapy&lt;SEP&gt;immunotherapeutic drug, targeted oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rituximab" target="Monoclonal Antibody">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Rituximab is classified as a monoclonal antibody designed to seek out cancer-specific proteins.</data>
      <data key="d6">drug type, biological therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rituximab" target="CD20">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Rituximab attaches to CD20 proteins on cancerous blood cells, marking them for destruction by the immune system.</data>
      <data key="d6">molecular targeting, drug mechanism</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="High-Dose Chemotherapy" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">High-dose chemotherapy is an intensified application of chemotherapy used to eliminate all lymphoma and bone marrow cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">therapy subtype, treatment intensity</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="The Colon" target="The Rectum">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The colon and rectum form continuous parts of the large bowel, collectively susceptible to colorectal cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical structure, digestive tract</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="The Colon" target="The Cecum">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The cecum forms the anatomical starting pouch of the colon.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical connection, digestive system</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="The Cecum" target="The Appendix">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The appendix is a small tube-shaped structure extending from the cecum.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical attachment, digestive tract</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Non-Metastatic Colon Cancer" target="Colectomy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Colectomy surgery is a primary treatment modality for patients diagnosed with non-metastatic colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical treatment, clinical intervention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Non-Metastatic Colon Cancer" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy is prescribed following surgical colectomy for non-metastatic stage 3 and selected stage 2 colon cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">adjuvant therapy, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Metastatic Colon Cancer" target="Systemic Therapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Systemic therapy is used to treat widespread disease in patients with metastatic colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer therapy, disease management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Metastatic Colon Cancer" target="Local Therapies">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Local therapies are utilized to target and treat metastatic tumors that have spread to the liver and lungs.</data>
      <data key="d6">targeted treatment, metastases management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Metastatic Colon Cancer" target="Secondary Liver Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Metastatic colon cancer is highlighted as an example of secondary liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease manifestation, cancer spread</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colectomy" target="ctDNA Testing">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">ctDNA tests evaluate whether microscopic cancer remains in the body following a colectomy.</data>
      <data key="d6">post-operative evaluation, residual disease</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colectomy" target="Invasive Colon Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A colectomy is the standard surgical resection required when colon cancer is invasive and cannot be treated with polypectomy alone.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical treatment, cancer resection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colectomy" target="Pathologist">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Tissue and lymph nodes removed during a colectomy are sent to a pathologist for staging and microscopic evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathology assessment, surgical analysis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colectomy" target="Colostomy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A colostomy may be performed alongside or instead of immediate reconnection during a colectomy if joining the colon is unsafe.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical options, stool diversion</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colectomy" target="Open Colectomy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Open colectomy is a conventional surgical technique used to carry out a colectomy through a large abdominal cut.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical technique, colectomy variant</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colectomy" target="Minimally Invasive Colectomy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Minimally invasive colectomy is an approach for conducting a colectomy using small incisions and specialized instruments.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical technique, laparoscopy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colectomy" target="Care Team">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The care team counsels patients about expectations, preparation, recovery, and complications related to a colectomy.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient care, postoperative guidance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colectomy" target="Anastomotic Leak">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">An anastomotic leak is a recognized short- or long-term surgical risk occurring at the colon reconnection site following a colectomy.&lt;SEP&gt;Anastomotic leak is a significant surgical complication that can occur after a colectomy procedure.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical complication, adverse effect&lt;SEP&gt;surgical complication, post-operative risk</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colectomy" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy may be indicated or withheld following a colectomy depending on the cancer stage and risk factors.</data>
      <data key="d6">post-operative care, treatment pathway</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colectomy" target="Stage 1 Colon Cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Patients with Stage 1 colon cancer typically undergo colectomy followed by periodic surveillance colonoscopies.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical treatment, surveillance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ctDNA Testing" target="Clinical Trial">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">ctDNA testing is currently recommended specifically within the framework of a clinical trial.</data>
      <data key="d6">research requirement, clinical protocol</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Non-Metastatic Cancer" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy after surgery is utilized as a treatment strategy for non-metastatic colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">adjuvant therapy, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="The American Joint Committee" target="Invasive Colon Cancer">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The American Joint Committee provides criteria and staging frameworks for evaluating the extent and progression of colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, medical guidelines</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colostomy" target="Bowel Blockage">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A colostomy is one of the surgical methods used to bypass a bowel blockage caused by an obstructing tumor.</data>
      <data key="d6">diversion, bowel obstruction</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colostomy" target="Pelvic Exenteration">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pelvic exenteration involving resection of the rectum or anus requires the creation of a colostomy.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical procedure, fecal diversion</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colostomy" target="Double-Barreled Wet Colostomy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A double-barreled wet colostomy is a specialized colostomy technique combining fecal and urinary diversions into one stoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical variant, stoma creation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bowel Blockage" target="One-Stage Colectomy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A one-stage colectomy is performed to remove the tumor and relieve a severe bowel blockage simultaneously.</data>
      <data key="d6">obstruction management, surgical resection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bowel Blockage" target="Stent Placement">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A stent may be placed across a bowel blockage to keep the passage open before definitive cancer resection.</data>
      <data key="d6">palliative intervention, obstruction relief</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 1 Colon Cancer" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy is explicitly not recommended after surgery for Stage 1 colon cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment contraindication, clinical protocol</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 2 Colon Cancer" target="FOLFOX">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">FOLFOX is recommended for high-risk stage 2 colon cancer following surgical resection.</data>
      <data key="d6">adjuvant therapy, treatment recommendation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 2 Colon Cancer" target="CAPEOX">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CAPEOX is recommended as a 3-month treatment course for high-risk stage 2 colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">adjuvant therapy, treatment recommendation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 3 Colon Cancer" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy is recommended following surgical resection for all Stage 3 colon cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">adjuvant therapy, treatment recommendation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 3 Colon Cancer" target="FOLFOX">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">FOLFOX is one of the preferred adjuvant chemotherapy regimens for all stage 3 colon cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">standard of care, adjuvant regimen</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 3 Colon Cancer" target="CAPEOX">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CAPEOX is a preferred adjuvant chemotherapy regimen for patients with stage 3 colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">standard of care, adjuvant regimen</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="dMMR/MSI-H Cancers" target="Immunotherapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Immunotherapy using checkpoint inhibitors is the preferred systemic treatment for dMMR/MSI-H colon cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">targeted therapy, oncology treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="dMMR/MSI-H Cancers" target="Checkpoint Inhibitors">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Checkpoint inhibitors are the preferred systemic therapy for patients diagnosed with dMMR/MSI-H colon cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">targeted therapy, biomarker matching</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="pMMR/MSS Cancers" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy is specifically recommended over immunotherapy for pMMR/MSS colon cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment protocol, systemic therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Checkpoint Inhibitors" target="Immunotherapy">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Checkpoint inhibitors are a major subclass of immunotherapy utilized in bladder cancer management.&lt;SEP&gt;Checkpoint inhibitors represent the specific class of immunotherapy recommended for colon cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">pharmacology, immunotherapy classification&lt;SEP&gt;therapy classification, drug mechanism</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Checkpoint Inhibitors" target="Pembrolizumab (Keytruda)">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pembrolizumab is a specific immune checkpoint inhibitor recommended prior to colectomy.</data>
      <data key="d6">drug classification, immunotherapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Checkpoint Inhibitors" target="Nivolumab (Opdivo)">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Nivolumab is an immune checkpoint inhibitor indicated for presurgical treatment of colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">drug classification, immunotherapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Checkpoint Inhibitors" target="NCCN">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN publishes specialized guidelines for patients addressing side effects caused by immune checkpoint inhibitors.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical documentation, patient education</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Nivolumab (Opdivo)" target="Ipilimumab (Yervoy)">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Nivolumab is frequently prescribed in combination with ipilimumab as an immune checkpoint inhibitor regimen.</data>
      <data key="d6">combination therapy, drug regimen</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FOLFOX" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">FOLFOX is a recognized standard adjuvant chemotherapy regimen used for colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">regimen classification, medical oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FOLFOX" target="Metastatic Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">FOLFOX is a recommended first-line platinum-based chemotherapy option for metastatic anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">chemotherapy regimen, metastatic treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CAPEOX" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CAPEOX is an established chemotherapy regimen recommended for adjuvant colon cancer therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">regimen classification, medical oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Capecitabine" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Capecitabine is an individual chemotherapy drug recommended for 6-month adjuvant treatment protocols.</data>
      <data key="d6">pharmacology, drug regimen</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Capecitabine" target="Mitomycin">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Capecitabine is combined with mitomycin as a recommended chemotherapy regimen during radiation for anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Mitomycin is paired with capecitabine to create a standard chemotherapy regimen used with radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">combination therapy, drug pairing&lt;SEP&gt;drug combination, chemotherapy regimen</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Capecitabine" target="Hand-Foot Syndrome">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Capecitabine can cause hand-foot syndrome, which may require medical dose adjustments.</data>
      <data key="d6">adverse effect, drug toxicity</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="5-Fluorouracil (5-FU)" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">5-Fluorouracil combined with leucovorin is a recommended chemotherapy protocol for colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">pharmacology, drug regimen</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="North Africa" target="Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">15.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Nasopharyngeal cancer has a higher incidence in people originating from North Africa.&lt;SEP&gt;Nasopharyngeal carcinoma is notably prevalent in parts of North Africa.</data>
      <data key="d6">epidemiology, geographic prevalence</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Native America" target="Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">15.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Nasopharyngeal cancer frequently occurs in people originating from Native America.&lt;SEP&gt;Nasopharyngeal carcinoma is known to occur more often among individuals from Native America.</data>
      <data key="d6">epidemiology, demographic prevalence&lt;SEP&gt;epidemiology, geographic prevalence</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma" target="Comprehensive Cancer Center">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Patients diagnosed with nasopharyngeal carcinoma are advised to seek evaluation and care at comprehensive cancer centers with disease experts.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical treatment, specialized care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma" target="Care Team">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The care team guides the patient through diagnostic testing and creates an individualized treatment plan for nasopharyngeal carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer management, treatment planning</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma" target="Nasopharyngeal Fiberoptic Exam">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The nasopharyngeal fiberoptic exam is a primary diagnostic test used to view internal throat structures and evaluate nasopharyngeal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic testing, tumor visualization</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A biopsy of the tumor or neck lymph nodes is essential to confirm the diagnosis and cellular characteristics of nasopharyngeal carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathology, diagnostic confirmation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma" target="MRI With Contrast">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MRI with contrast from collarbone to skull base is used to assess the local extent of nasopharyngeal tumors for treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical imaging, treatment planning</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma" target="NCCN">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN provides patient education, guidance, and staging explanations for nasopharyngeal carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient education, healthcare guidance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma" target="Squamous Cell Carcinoma Of The Nasopharynx">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Squamous cell carcinoma of the nasopharynx is a primary form of nasopharyngeal carcinoma that follows TNM staging guidelines.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype, disease classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma" target="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biomarker testing is conducted to identify mutations and genes in cancer tissue that guide targeted treatments for nasopharyngeal carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic testing, treatment selection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma" target="Head and Neck Tumors">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Nasopharyngeal carcinoma is categorized among head and neck tumors and measured clinically in centimeters.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, tumor measurement</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surveillance Follow-Up Testing" target="Cancer Survivors">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Surveillance testing forms an essential part of the care plan designed for cancer survivors.</data>
      <data key="d6">survivorship care, post-treatment monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Birth Control" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Because chemotherapy can harm fetal development and damage reproductive cells, birth control is recommended during treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">pregnancy prevention, patient safety</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Biopsy of Metastasis" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biopsy of metastasis is a specialized application of biopsy techniques to evaluate distant disease spread.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedural categorization, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cannabis" target="Surgery">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cannabis consumption can alter the dosage requirements of anesthesia administered during surgery.</data>
      <data key="d6">anesthesia interaction, surgical risk</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EBV-Related Cancer" target="Infectious Mononucleosis">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Both infectious mononucleosis and EBV-related cancers stem from infection with the Epstein-Barr virus.</data>
      <data key="d6">viral pathogen, related conditions</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TMB-H" target="Immunotherapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cancers identified with high tumor mutational burden (TMB-H) are frequently treated with immunotherapy targeting PD-1 or PD-L1.</data>
      <data key="d6">targeted treatment, immunotherapy eligibility</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Intraepithelial Neoplasia" target="Low-Grade Squamous Intraepithelial Lesion">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Low-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion is the diagnostic category corresponding to mild dysplasia or CIN 1.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathology staging, dysplasia classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Intraepithelial Neoplasia" target="High-Grade Squamous Intraepithelial Lesion">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">High-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion corresponds to CIN 2 and CIN 3, representing advanced cervical dysplasia.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathology staging, pre-cancer classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Intraepithelial Neoplasia" target="Cervical Dysplasia">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cervical dysplasia is synonymous with cervical intraepithelial neoplasia (CIN), indicating abnormal cellular proliferation.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic terminology, histological equivalence</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Low-Grade Squamous Intraepithelial Lesion" target="Anal Dysplasia">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Low-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion (LSIL) is an alternative clinical classification for mild anal dysplasia.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical taxonomy, dysplasia classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="High-Grade Squamous Intraepithelial Lesion" target="Anal Dysplasia">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">High-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion (HSIL) is an alternative term for moderate or severe anal dysplasia.</data>
