    </edge>
    <edge source="Cystoscopy" target="Pyelogram">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A pyelogram is typically performed in the operating room during a cystoscopy procedure.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedural combination, medical testing</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="X-Ray" target="Imaging Tests">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">An x-ray is a primary imaging test using radiation to capture pictures of body structures.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic procedure, radiography</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="X-Ray" target="CT Scan">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CT scans compile multiple x-ray images taken from varying angles into cross-sectional views.</data>
      <data key="d6">imaging technique, technological foundation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 1 Cervical Cancer" target="Stage 1A Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 1A is a specific sub-stage under stage 1 cervical cancer classified by tumor dimension.</data>
      <data key="d6">sub-stage classification, clinical staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 1 Cervical Cancer" target="Stage 1B Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 1B is a sub-stage under stage 1 cervical cancer categorized into 1B1, 1B2, and 1B3.</data>
      <data key="d6">sub-stage classification, clinical staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 2 Cervical Cancer" target="Stage 2A Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 2A is a clinical subdivision of stage 2 indicating tumor spread to the upper vagina.</data>
      <data key="d6">sub-stage classification, anatomical spread</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 2 Cervical Cancer" target="Stage 2B Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 2B is a clinical subdivision of stage 2 indicating extension into the parametrium.</data>
      <data key="d6">sub-stage classification, anatomical spread</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 3 Cervical Cancer" target="Stage 3A Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 3A is a subdivision of stage 3 indicating growth into the lower third of the vagina.</data>
      <data key="d6">sub-stage classification, anatomical spread</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 3 Cervical Cancer" target="Stage 3B Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 3B is a subdivision of stage 3 involving the pelvic wall or kidney involvement.</data>
      <data key="d6">sub-stage classification, disease severity</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 3 Cervical Cancer" target="Stage 3C Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 3C is a subdivision of stage 3 characterized by pelvic or para-aortic lymph node involvement.</data>
      <data key="d6">sub-stage classification, lymphatic spread</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 4 Cervical Cancer" target="Stage 4A Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 4A represents local advanced extension of stage 4 cervical cancer into nearby organs.</data>
      <data key="d6">sub-stage classification, organ invasion</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 4 Cervical Cancer" target="Stage 4B Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 4B represents distant metastatic spread of stage 4 cervical cancer to organs like the lungs, liver, or bone.</data>
      <data key="d6">sub-stage classification, distant metastasis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 4B Cervical Cancer" target="Systemic Therapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Patients with stage 4B cervical cancer receive systemic therapy, with imaging monitoring therapeutic response.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment response, advanced oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Care Plan" target="Clinical Trials">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clinical trials are one of the therapeutic options that may be incorporated into a patient's cancer care plan.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment options, investigational therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Trachelectomy" target="Surgery">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Trachelectomy is a specialized fertility-sparing surgical operation used to treat cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Trachelectomy is a specialized surgical procedure removing the cervix while preserving the uterus.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical procedure, fertility preservation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hysterectomy" target="Surgery">
      <data key="d4">19.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hysterectomy is a core surgical treatment option for cervical cancer involving the removal of the uterus and cervix.&lt;SEP&gt;Hysterectomy is a primary surgical operation removing the uterus and cervix.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical procedure, gynecologic surgery&lt;SEP&gt;surgical treatment, tumor resection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hysterectomy" target="Extrafascial Hysterectomy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Extrafascial hysterectomy is a simple form of hysterectomy commonly indicated for early-stage cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical subtype, procedure variation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hysterectomy" target="Modified Radical Hysterectomy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Modified radical hysterectomy is an intermediate resection of the uterus, cervix, and surrounding tissue.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical subtype, procedure variation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hysterectomy" target="Radical Hysterectomy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radical hysterectomy represents an extensive surgical resection of the uterus, cervix, parametria, and upper vagina.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical subtype, procedure variation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hysterectomy" target="Ovary Preservation">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Ovary preservation is evaluated during a hysterectomy to prevent the onset of surgical menopause.</data>
      <data key="d6">hormonal preservation, surgical strategy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hysterectomy" target="Stage 1A1">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">An extrafascial simple hysterectomy is recommended for stage 1A1 patients who do not wish to preserve fertility.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical treatment, non-fertility sparing</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Minimally Invasive Surgery" target="Surgery">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Minimally invasive or laparoscopic surgery is an operative approach used to reduce pain and recovery time in cervical cancer procedures.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical approach, procedural technique</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Extrafascial Hysterectomy" target="Stage 1A1">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Extrafascial hysterectomy is the primary non-fertility-sparing surgical treatment choice for stage 1A1 cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical standard, definitive treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Extrafascial Hysterectomy" target="Stage 1A2">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Low-risk stage 1A2 cancers lacking LVSI and meeting tumor size criteria can be treated with extrafascial hysterectomy.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical indication, treatment protocol</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Extrafascial Hysterectomy" target="Stage 1B1">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 1B1 cancers smaller than 2 cm without LVSI or positive margins can undergo extrafascial hysterectomy.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical management, treatment eligibility</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Modified Radical Hysterectomy" target="Stage 1A1">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 1A1 disease with LVSI is treated with modified radical hysterectomy.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment protocol, surgical management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radical Hysterectomy" target="Stage 1B1">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 1B1 tumors that do not meet low-risk criteria are treated with radical hysterectomy and pelvic lymph node evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical standard, risk-based care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radical Hysterectomy" target="Stage 1B2">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radical hysterectomy with pelvic lymph node dissection is the recommended primary surgical therapy for stage 1B2 cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical intervention, clinical guideline</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radical Hysterectomy" target="Stage 2A1">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 2A1 cervical cancer is typically treated with radical hysterectomy and pelvic lymph node evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical protocol, oncologic surgery</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radical Hysterectomy" target="Stage 1B3">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radical hysterectomy with pelvic lymph node dissection is an alternate surgical option for stage 1B3 cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical alternative, multimodal care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radical Hysterectomy" target="Pelvic Lymph Node Dissection">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pelvic lymph node dissection is performed in conjunction with radical hysterectomy to evaluate for nodal metastases.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical combination, staging procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radical Hysterectomy" target="Pelvic Exenteration">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Both pelvic exenteration and radical hysterectomy are surgical treatment choices evaluated for localized cancer recurrence in the pelvis.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical options, recurrence management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Diagnostic Imaging" target="CT Scan">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CT scan is a prominent modality within the broader category of diagnostic imaging.</data>
      <data key="d6">classification, diagnostic modality</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Diagnostic Imaging" target="Urogram">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A urogram is a specialized type of urinary tract evaluation categorized under diagnostic imaging.</data>
      <data key="d6">classification, diagnostic modality</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Diagnostic Imaging" target="Renal Ultrasound">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Renal ultrasound is a non-invasive procedure categorized under diagnostic imaging.</data>
      <data key="d6">classification, diagnostic modality</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Locally Advanced Cervical Cancer" target="Stage 2B">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 2B is categorized under locally advanced cervical cancer and managed non-surgically.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, disease categorization</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Locally Advanced Cervical Cancer" target="Stage 3">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 3 cervical cancer represents locally advanced disease managed with chemoradiation and brachytherapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, disease stage</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Locally Advanced Cervical Cancer" target="Stage 4A">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 4A belongs to the spectrum of locally advanced cervical cancer without distant spread.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical staging, disease categorization</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Metastatic Cervical Cancer" target="Stereotactic Body Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stereotactic body radiation therapy is specifically used to treat metastatic cervical tumors in the liver, lungs, or bone.</data>
      <data key="d6">metastasis management, targeted therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Metastatic Cervical Cancer" target="Stage 4B">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 4B classifies cervical cancer that is already metastatic upon initial presentation.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease staging, cancer categorization</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Metastatic Cervical Cancer" target="Systemic Therapy">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Systemic therapy is standard treatment for metastatic cervical cancer following or alongside local therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic therapy, commonly platinum-based chemotherapy, is the primary treatment modality for controlling metastatic cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease management, systemic treatment&lt;SEP&gt;medical oncology, disease management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Metastatic Cervical Cancer" target="Ablative Therapies">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Ablative therapies can be employed to treat or destroy specific metastatic lesions in organs like the liver, lungs, or bone.</data>
      <data key="d6">local intervention, metastatic treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Metastatic Cervical Cancer" target="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biomarker testing is performed on metastatic tumors to identify eligibility for targeted therapies or immunotherapies.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic testing, precision medicine</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Metastatic Cervical Cancer" target="Distant Recurrence">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Distant recurrence is a form of metastatic disease where cervical cancer returns in distant anatomical sites after initial therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, recurrence pattern</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Metastatic Cervical Cancer" target="Resection">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Resection is an option to surgically remove new metastatic cancer growths.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical intervention, oligometastatic management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Metastatic Cervical Cancer" target="Clinical Trial">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clinical trials are presented as potential treatment avenues for individuals with metastatic cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment options, clinical research</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Metastatic Cervical Cancer" target="Advance Care Planning">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Advance care planning assists patients with metastatic cervical cancer in defining treatment goals and end-of-life wishes.</data>
      <data key="d6">prognosis, end-of-life planning</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Laparoscopic Surgical Staging" target="Para-Aortic Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Laparoscopic surgical staging is used to sample and evaluate para-aortic lymph nodes prior to definitive therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">minimally invasive surgery, node evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy" target="Pelvic Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Intensity-modulated radiation therapy allows targeted radiation to the cervical tumor and nearby pelvic lymph nodes while sparing healthy adjacent structures.</data>
      <data key="d6">targeted irradiation, anatomical precision</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">27.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Intensity-modulated radiation therapy (IMRT) is an advanced subtype of external radiation therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Intensity-modulated radiation therapy (IMRT) is an advanced technique within radiation therapy preferred for anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Intensity-modulated radiation therapy is designated as the preferred radiation method for treating anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical technique, therapeutic specialization&lt;SEP&gt;preferred technique, radiation delivery&lt;SEP&gt;therapy classification, treatment modality</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stereotactic Body Radiation Therapy" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">SBRT is an advanced, targeted subtype of radiation therapy applied for oligometastatic disease.</data>
      <data key="d6">specialized treatment, radiation oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Simulation" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Simulation is a required treatment planning session conducted before radiation therapy commences.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment preparation, planning session</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="High Dose-Rate Brachytherapy" target="Tandem And Ring Applicator">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The tandem and ring applicator is a common device utilized to administer HDR brachytherapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical device, radiation delivery</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Recurrent Or Metastatic Cervical Cancer" target="Targeted Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Targeted therapy serves as a systemic treatment option for cervical cancer that recurs or metastasizes.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment indication, advanced disease management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Recurrent Or Metastatic Cervical Cancer" target="Immunotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Immunotherapy is indicated as a systemic therapy option for recurrent or metastatic cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment indication, advanced disease management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Early-Stage Cancer" target="Surgery">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Surgery is the standard treatment option recommended for early-stage cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">stage-specific therapy, surgical oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Early-Stage Cancer" target="Stage 1">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 1 cervical cancer represents cancers localized strictly to the cervix within the early-stage categorization.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, disease stage</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Early-Stage Cancer" target="Stage 2A">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 2A cervical cancer involves extension into the upper vagina and falls within early-stage cancer management recommendations.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, disease stage</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Phase 1 Trial" target="Clinical Trial">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Phase 1 trials represent the initial step in the sequential phases of clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d6">trial phase, research pipeline</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Phase 2 Trial" target="Clinical Trial">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Phase 2 trials are clinical trial phases testing efficacy against specific cancer types.</data>
      <data key="d6">trial phase, research pipeline</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Phase 3 Trial" target="Clinical Trial">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Phase 3 trials compare investigational treatments to standard therapy within the clinical trial process.</data>
      <data key="d6">trial phase, comparative study</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Phase 4 Trial" target="Clinical Trial">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Phase 4 trials investigate post-approval, long-term safety and outcomes within clinical research.</data>
      <data key="d6">trial phase, post-market surveillance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 1A1" target="Lymphovascular Space Invasion">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Presence of LVSI guides whether cone biopsy alone is sufficient or whether radical trachelectomy and pelvic lymph node evaluation are necessary.</data>
      <data key="d6">risk stratification, surgical guidance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 1A1" target="Vaginal Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Brachytherapy is recommended for stage 1A1 cervical cancer with positive margins when surgery is not feasible.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation alternative, margin management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 1A1" target="LVSI">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The presence of LVSI in stage 1A1 cancer dictates the recommendation of a modified radical hysterectomy with lymph node evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathology assessment, risk stratification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 1A2" target="Radical Trachelectomy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radical trachelectomy is recommended for stage 1A2 cervical cancers with lymphovascular space invasion.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical treatment, fertility preservation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 1A2" target="Lymphovascular Space Invasion">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">LVSI presence determines whether stage 1A2 patients can receive a cone biopsy or require radical trachelectomy with pelvic lymph node assessment.</data>
      <data key="d6">risk factor, treatment selection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 1A2" target="Pelvic Lymph Node Evaluation">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pelvic lymph node evaluation is routinely performed alongside cone biopsy or trachelectomy for stage 1A2 cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">staging procedure, surgical management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 1B1" target="Radical Trachelectomy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radical trachelectomy with pelvic lymph node evaluation is recommended for stage 1B1 cancers not qualifying for cone biopsy.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical treatment, lymph node dissection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 1B1" target="Pelvic Lymph Node Evaluation">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pelvic lymph node evaluation is performed alongside cone biopsy or radical trachelectomy in stage 1B1 cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedural staging, lymphatic assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lymphovascular Space Invasion" target="Pelvic Lymph Node Evaluation">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cancers exhibiting LVSI have a higher risk of nodal involvement, necessitating pelvic lymph node evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic indication, metastatic risk</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 1" target="Connective Tissue Layer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 1 bladder cancer penetrates past the inner lining into the connective tissue layer.</data>
      <data key="d6">tissue invasion, disease progression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 1" target="Non-Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 1 is classified as non-muscle-invasive bladder cancer because it has not penetrated the muscular wall.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical classification, staging depth</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 3" target="Outer Fatty Layer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 3 bladder cancer breaks through the muscle layer and reaches the surrounding outer fatty layer.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical invasion, disease advancement</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 3" target="Stage 3A">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 3 bladder cancer is subdivided into Stage 3A based on regional spread to pelvic organs or a single lymph node.</data>
      <data key="d6">subgroup classification, clinical staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 3" target="Stage 3B">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 3 bladder cancer is subdivided into Stage 3B based on spread to multiple pelvic lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">subgroup classification, clinical staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 4A" target="Stage 4">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 4 includes subgroup Stage 4A, characterized by pelvic wall invasion or distant lymph node involvement.</data>
      <data key="d6">subgroup classification, advanced staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Physical Exams" target="Cancer Survivor">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cancer survivors undergo surveillance physical examinations to detect potential recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d6">survivorship, clinical surveillance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pelvic Recurrence" target="EBRT">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">EBRT is recommended after surgical removal of recurrent cancer if the pelvic area was not previously irradiated.</data>
      <data key="d6">recurrent cancer treatment, radiation therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pelvic Recurrence" target="Pelvic Exenteration">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pelvic exenteration is considered for local pelvic recurrence when prior radiation therapy precludes further irradiation.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical oncology, salvage therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stages 2, 3, And 4A Cervical Cancer" target="CT With Contrast">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CT with contrast is an accepted alternative imaging scan 3 to 6 months after treating stages 2, 3, and 4A.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic alternatives, surveillance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pelvic Exenteration" target="Urinary Diversion">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pelvic exenteration may involve bladder removal, necessitating a urinary diversion such as an ileal conduit or continent pouch.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical reconstruction, post-operative diversion</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pelvic Exenteration" target="Double-Barreled Wet Colostomy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A double-barreled wet colostomy may be utilized following pelvic exenteration when both the bowel and bladder are removed.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical technique, organ resection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pelvic Exenteration" target="Local Or Regional Recurrence">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pelvic exenteration is evaluated as an operative treatment choice for local or regional recurrence when prior radiation precludes further radiotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">recurrence management, salvage surgery</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pelvic Exenteration" target="Vaginal Reconstruction">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Vaginal reconstruction is often performed immediately following or in conjunction with pelvic exenteration using abdominal muscle flaps.</data>
      <data key="d6">reconstructive surgery, post-surgical care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RET Gene Fusion" target="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">RET gene fusion is tested in cervical cancer to detect actionable mutations.</data>
      <data key="d6">mutation analysis, genomic profiling</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 4B" target="Stage 4">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 4 includes subgroup Stage 4B, which designates full metastatic dissemination.</data>
      <data key="d6">subgroup classification, metastasis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 4B" target="Metastatic Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 4B is explicitly defined as metastatic bladder cancer spreading to distant organs like the liver, lungs, or bones.</data>
      <data key="d6">metastatic disease, stage definition</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urinary Diversion" target="Ileal Conduit">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">An ileal conduit is a specific type of urinary diversion creating an incontinent stoma.&lt;SEP&gt;The ileal conduit is one of the three primary types of urinary diversion procedures.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedural subtype, surgical routing&lt;SEP&gt;surgical variation, urinary diversion technique</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urinary Diversion" target="Continent Urinary Diversion">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A continent urinary diversion (Indiana pouch) represents an alternative internal reservoir approach to urinary diversion.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedural subtype, surgical reconstruction</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urinary Diversion" target="Radical Cystectomy">
      <data key="d4">19.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A urinary diversion is routinely performed in conjunction with a radical cystectomy to create a new route for urine drainage.&lt;SEP&gt;Because a radical cystectomy removes the entire bladder, an accompanying urinary diversion is performed to reroute urine exit.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical co-procedure, reconstructive surgery&lt;SEP&gt;surgical reconstruction, operative procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urinary Diversion" target="Neobladder">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A neobladder is a primary type of urinary diversion that reconstructs an internal bladder substitute.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical variation, urinary diversion technique</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urinary Diversion" target="Continent Cutaneous Pouch">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A continent cutaneous pouch is a specific type of urinary diversion procedure.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical variation, urinary diversion technique</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urinary Diversion" target="Indiana Pouch">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The Indiana pouch is an alternative form of urinary diversion that eliminates the need for an external ostomy bag.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical technique, medical diversion</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Immunotherapies" target="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biomarker testing assesses tumor markers to determine candidates for immunotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">predictive biomarkers, immunotherapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Immunotherapies" target="Systemic Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Immunotherapies fall within the broader umbrella of systemic therapies indicated for advanced cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">systemic oncology, immuno-oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Resection" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A surgical resection acts simultaneously as a therapeutic tumor removal and a biopsy yielding tissue for testing.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical procedure, dual-purpose intervention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mediastinal Gray Zone Lymphoma" target="Primary Mediastinal B-Cell Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mediastinal gray zone lymphoma exhibits overlapping clinical and pathological features with PMBL but follows distinct treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease overlap, diagnostic comparison</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mediastinal Gray Zone Lymphoma" target="Non-Mediastinal Gray Zone Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mediastinal and non-mediastinal gray zone lymphomas represent the two primary classifications of gray zone lymphomas.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype classification, clinical presentation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mediastinal Gray Zone Lymphoma" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy is commonly used as the primary treatment for mediastinal gray zone lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment regimen, oncology care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mediastinal Gray Zone Lymphoma" target="Cancer Centers">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Patients diagnosed with mediastinal gray zone lymphoma are best managed at experienced cancer centers.</data>
      <data key="d6">specialized treatment, medical management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mediastinal Gray Zone Lymphoma" target="Hematopathologist">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">An expert hematopathologist review is required to confirm the diagnosis of mediastinal gray zone lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic confirmation, pathology review</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mediastinal Gray Zone Lymphoma" target="Classical Hodgkin Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mediastinal gray zone lymphoma exhibits features that overlap directly with classical Hodgkin lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Mediastinal gray zone lymphoma shares clinical and biological features with classical Hodgkin lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease overlap, histopathology&lt;SEP&gt;pathological overlap, disease classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mediastinal Gray Zone Lymphoma" target="DLBCL-NOS">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cases presenting with disease outside the mediastinum are classified as DLBCL-NOS rather than mediastinal gray zone lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">differential diagnosis, disease classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mediastinal Gray Zone Lymphoma" target="Non-Hodgkin PMBL">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mediastinal gray zone lymphoma exhibits clinical and biological characteristics of non-Hodgkin primary mediastinal B-cell lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease overlap, diagnostic criteria</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mediastinal Gray Zone Lymphoma" target="Chemoimmunotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mediastinal gray zone lymphoma is treated using chemoimmunotherapy regimens, often combining chemotherapy with rituximab.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical treatment, therapeutic approach</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mediastinal Gray Zone Lymphoma" target="Involved-Site Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Involved-site radiation therapy may be integrated into treatment plans for localized mediastinal gray zone lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation therapy, oncology treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Non-Mediastinal Gray Zone Lymphoma" target="DLBCL-NOS">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cases presenting with gray zone lymphoma features outside the mediastinum are diagnosed as DLBCL-NOS.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic classification, disease reclassification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Centers" target="NCCN">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The NCCN develops clinical care guidelines based on research and established practices from leading cancer centers.</data>
      <data key="d6">guideline development, institutional collaboration</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Centers" target="Integrative Oncology">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Many cancer centers offer dedicated integrative oncology programs combining conventional therapies with complementary care.</data>
      <data key="d6">institutional care, integrative programs</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="First-Line Therapy" target="Systemic Therapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">First-line therapy represents the initial systemic regimen administered for metastatic cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment staging, systemic protocol</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Second-Line Therapy" target="Systemic Therapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Second-line therapy provides alternative systemic treatment options if first-line chemotherapy fails or cannot be sustained.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment escalation, alternative therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hematopathologist" target="Blood Smear">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A hematopathologist examines a blood smear under a microscope to identify abnormal cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;The hematopathologist evaluates the blood smear under a microscope to identify abnormal cell characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic testing, clinical pathology&lt;SEP&gt;microscopic analysis, diagnosis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hematopathologist" target="CLL">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The hematopathologist analyzes blood smears and tissue samples under a microscope to assist in diagnosing CLL.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathology, diagnostic assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Cancer" target="NCCN">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN provides patient guidelines and resources concerning cancers prevalent in adolescents and young adults, including thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical guidelines, patient education</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Cancer" target="Cowden Syndrome">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cowden syndrome is a hereditary condition that predisposes individuals to follicular thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic predisposition, risk factor</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Cancer" target="Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Multiple endocrine neoplasia is an inherited syndrome recognized as a risk factor for thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic predisposition, endocrine disorder</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Cancer" target="Thyroid Nodules">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Thyroid cancer originates and presents as nodules or abnormal lumps in the thyroid gland.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease manifestation, clinical presentation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Cancer" target="Follicular Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Follicular thyroid cancer is a specific histological type of thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype classification, oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Cancer" target="Adolescent And Young Adult Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Thyroid cancer is the single most common cancer identified within the adolescent and young adult demographic.</data>
      <data key="d6">epidemiology, demographic prevalence</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Cancer" target="Radiation Exposure">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Prior radiation exposure to the head or neck significantly raises the risk of acquiring thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">etiology, risk factor</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Cancer" target="Targeted Therapy">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Targeted therapies are used to treat thyroid cancers that are recurrent, metastatic, or unresponsive to surgery and RAI.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy is an option for metastatic thyroid cancer that cannot be treated with surgery or RAI.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer therapy, advanced treatment&lt;SEP&gt;treatment option, therapeutic intervention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Cancer" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy is utilized to treat tumors, manage spread, and alleviate pain in patients with thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease management, symptom relief</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Cancer" target="RAI Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">RAI therapy is a standard treatment modality for thyroid cancer; targeted therapies are typically used when RAI therapy is no longer viable.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease treatment, standard therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Cancer" target="Surgery">
      <data key="d4">15.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Surgery is a primary modality evaluated for treating metastatic thyroid cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgery serves as a primary standard treatment for thyroid cancer prior to systemic or targeted therapies.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease treatment, surgical intervention&lt;SEP&gt;surgical treatment, clinical care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Cancer" target="Differentiated High-Grade Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Differentiated high-grade carcinoma is a subtype and pathological classification of thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, pathology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Cancer" target="Total Thyroidectomy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Total thyroidectomy is a standard surgical treatment for thyroid cancer, dictating specific post-operative monitoring procedures.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical treatment, clinical management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Cancer" target="Lobectomy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lobectomy is a surgical treatment option for thyroid cancer with separate follow-up care requirements.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical treatment, clinical management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Cancer" target="Recurrence">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Thyroid cancer patients remain at risk for local or distant cancer recurrence after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease progression, oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Cancer" target="Clinical Trial">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Participation in a clinical trial is strongly recommended for individuals managing metastatic thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment recommendation, medical research</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Cancer" target="Radioactive Iodine Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Metastatic thyroid cancer may fail to take up iodine, impacting the feasibility of RAI treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">therapeutic intervention, cancer care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cowden Syndrome" target="Follicular Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cowden syndrome is an inherited disorder that directly causes an elevated risk of developing follicular thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic risk, inherited syndrome</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Nodules" target="TSH Test">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A TSH blood test is routinely ordered when thyroid nodules are identified to assess whether the nodule produces thyroid hormone.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical evaluation, diagnostic workflow</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Nodules" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Physicians use ultrasound and TSH findings to decide whether to perform a biopsy on suspicious thyroid nodules.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic procedure, histology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hyperthyroidism" target="TSH Test">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A low TSH level detected during blood testing typically indicates hyperthyroidism.&lt;SEP&gt;A low TSH level detected on testing usually indicates elevated thyroid hormone levels, identifying hyperthyroidism.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic indication, hormonal status&lt;SEP&gt;endocrine diagnosis, laboratory testing</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hyperthyroidism" target="Radioactive Iodine Uptake Test">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A radioactive iodine uptake test may be administered to patients showing symptoms or laboratory signs of hyperthyroidism.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic confirmation, functional testing</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TSH Test" target="Provider">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Providers order TSH testing to assess whether a patient's thyroid nodule is actively producing hormone.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic ordering, clinical assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radioactive Iodine Uptake Test" target="Provider">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A provider may order a radioactive iodine uptake test to investigate abnormal thyroid hormone levels and nodule function.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical testing, clinical investigation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radiation Exposure" target="Catastrophic Event">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Environmental radiation exposure often results from catastrophic events, increasing population cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d6">environmental hazard, radiation source</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Papillary Carcinoma" target="Differentiated Thyroid Cancers">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Papillary carcinoma is the most common subtype within the differentiated thyroid cancer category.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, cancer subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Follicular Carcinoma" target="Differentiated Thyroid Cancers">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Follicular carcinoma is classified as a differentiated thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, cancer subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Follicular Carcinoma" target="Molecular Tests">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Molecular tests assist in clarifying whether follicular lesions identified on initial biopsy are malignant carcinomas requiring surgery.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic clarification, cancer differentiation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Oncocytic Carcinoma" target="Differentiated Thyroid Cancers">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Oncocytic carcinoma is classified under the differentiated thyroid cancers category.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, cancer subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Oncocytic Carcinoma" target="Total Thyroidectomy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Total thyroidectomy is indicated for oncocytic carcinoma extending beyond the thyroid or presenting high-risk features.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical treatment, oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Oncocytic Carcinoma" target="Follicular Thyroid Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Both follicular and oncocytic carcinomas share the characteristic of invading blood vessels in and around the thyroid.</data>
      <data key="d6">comparative pathology, cancer classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Oncocytic Carcinoma" target="Hürthle Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hürthle cell carcinoma is the historical medical name formerly used to classify oncocytic carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical nomenclature, disease classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Differentiated Thyroid Cancers" target="Lobectomy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lobectomy is utilized as a surgical treatment option for some small, low-risk differentiated thyroid cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical treatment, clinical application</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Differentiated Thyroid Cancers" target="Papillary Thyroid Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Papillary thyroid carcinoma is classified as a differentiated thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">classification, cancer subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Differentiated Thyroid Cancers" target="Follicular and Oncocytic Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Follicular and oncocytic carcinomas are forms of differentiated thyroid cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">classification, cancer subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Differentiated Thyroid Cancers" target="Targeted Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Targeted therapy is an option for thyroid cancers that fail to respond to standard therapies or have metastasized.</data>
      <data key="d6">advanced treatment, therapeutic alternative</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Medullary Thyroid Cancer" target="Radioactive Iodine Therapy">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radioactive iodine therapy is ineffective against medullary thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment limitation, non-responsiveness</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Medullary Thyroid Cancer" target="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biomarker testing can identify RET gene fusions, which are found in some medullary thyroid cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">molecular diagnostics, cancer subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Anaplastic Thyroid Cancer" target="Radioactive Iodine Therapy">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radioactive iodine therapy is not effective in treating anaplastic thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment limitation, non-responsiveness</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Anaplastic Thyroid Cancer" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy is frequently combined with radiation therapy to manage anaplastic thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">combination therapy, aggressive cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Anaplastic Thyroid Cancer" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy is used alongside chemotherapy as an intervention against anaplastic thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">combination therapy, aggressive cancer management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lobectomy" target="Total Thyroidectomy">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Both are surgical procedures used to treat thyroid cancer, differing in whether the entire gland or a single lobe is excised.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical alternatives, procedural comparison</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lobectomy" target="Thyroid Hormone Replacement Therapy">
      <data key="d4">21.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Approximately one in three patients requires thyroid hormone replacement therapy after undergoing a lobectomy.&lt;SEP&gt;Approximately one-third of patients require thyroid hormone replacement therapy following a lobectomy.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid hormone replacement therapy may be administered following a lobectomy on a case-by-case basis to manage TSH levels.</data>
      <data key="d6">hormone monitoring, partial thyroidectomy care&lt;SEP&gt;post-operative therapy, clinical necessity&lt;SEP&gt;postoperative care, endocrine management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lobectomy" target="Papillary Thyroid Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lobectomy is a surgical option for small, noninvasive papillary thyroid carcinoma limited to the thyroid.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical treatment, clinical indication</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lobectomy" target="Completion Thyroidectomy">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A completion thyroidectomy is carried out if high-risk or concerning features are identified after a lobectomy.&lt;SEP&gt;A completion thyroidectomy is performed after an initial lobectomy if pathologic analysis reveals high-risk, concerning, or invasive cancer features.</data>
      <data key="d6">follow-up surgery, surgical escalation&lt;SEP&gt;secondary surgery, treatment escalation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lobectomy" target="Surgery">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lobectomy is a conservative surgical intervention designed to resect part of the thyroid gland.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedural subtype, surgical treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lobectomy" target="NIFTP">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">When pathologic examination following surgery identifies a tumor as NIFTP, no additional surgery or treatment beyond removal is needed.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical outcome, low-risk diagnosis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lobectomy" target="Neck Ultrasound">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A neck ultrasound is recommended 6 to 12 months after lobectomy to monitor the remaining thyroid and neck tissue.</data>
      <data key="d6">imaging surveillance, post-surgical care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Total Thyroidectomy" target="Thyroid Hormone Replacement Therapy">
      <data key="d4">28.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lifelong thyroid hormone replacement therapy using levothyroxine is required after a total thyroidectomy.&lt;SEP&gt;Patients who undergo a total thyroidectomy require lifelong thyroid hormone replacement therapy because the gland is entirely removed.&lt;SEP&gt;Patients who undergo total thyroidectomy require lifelong thyroid hormone replacement therapy with levothyroxine.</data>
      <data key="d6">post-operative care, hormone management&lt;SEP&gt;post-operative therapy, hormone replacement&lt;SEP&gt;postoperative care, medical necessity</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Total Thyroidectomy" target="Care Team">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The care team coordinates surgical recovery, instructions, and symptom management after a total thyroidectomy.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient management, clinical oversight</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Total Thyroidectomy" target="Neck Dissection">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A neck dissection to remove nearby lymph nodes is performed in conjunction with a total thyroidectomy when lymph node involvement is suspected.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical staging, operative intervention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Total Thyroidectomy" target="Hypoparathyroidism">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hypoparathyroidism, resulting in low blood calcium levels, is a recognized long-term adverse effect of surgical thyroid removal.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical risk, clinical complication</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Total Thyroidectomy" target="Radioactive Iodine Therapy">
      <data key="d4">25.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radioactive iodine therapy is commonly administered after total thyroidectomy to destroy remaining cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Radioactive iodine therapy is frequently evaluated and administered after total thyroidectomy to destroy residual cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Radioactive iodine therapy is typically administered after total thyroidectomy to ablate residual thyroid tissue.</data>
      <data key="d6">adjuvant therapy, post-surgical care&lt;SEP&gt;post-surgical treatment, adjuvant therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Total Thyroidectomy" target="Papillary Thyroid Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Total thyroidectomy is recommended for papillary thyroid carcinoma that is large, high-risk, or spread beyond the thyroid.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical treatment, clinical indication</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Total Thyroidectomy" target="Surgery">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Total thyroidectomy is a major surgical procedure used as a primary surgical intervention for thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedural subtype, surgical treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Total Thyroidectomy" target="Systemic Therapy">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Systemic therapy is considered alongside other modalities if significant cancer remains after total thyroidectomy.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic therapy is considered as an adjuvant treatment option when significant residual cancer remains after thyroidectomy.</data>
      <data key="d6">adjuvant treatment, systemic cancer care&lt;SEP&gt;post-surgical intervention, multimodal care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Total Thyroidectomy" target="Follicular Thyroid Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Total thyroidectomy is recommended for suspected follicular thyroid carcinoma that has grown beyond the thyroid or is larger than 4 cm.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical treatment, cancer management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Total Thyroidectomy" target="Neck Ultrasound">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A neck ultrasound is a standard surveillance follow-up test recommended 6 to 12 months following a total thyroidectomy.&lt;SEP&gt;A neck ultrasound is recommended 6 to 12 months post-total thyroidectomy to monitor for structural recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d6">imaging surveillance, post-surgical care&lt;SEP&gt;post-operative surveillance, diagnostic follow-up</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Total Thyroidectomy" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy is a potential secondary treatment option if a concerning amount of cancer persists after total thyroidectomy.</data>
      <data key="d6">adjuvant treatment, salvage therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Total Thyroidectomy" target="RAI Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Patients who undergo total thyroidectomy may receive subsequent RAI therapy and whole-body RAI imaging for surveillance.</data>
      <data key="d6">post-operative treatment, cancer therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Total Thyroidectomy" target="Thyroglobulin">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">After total thyroidectomy, serum thyroglobulin serves as a biomarker to monitor for recurrence since healthy thyroid tissue has been removed.</data>
      <data key="d6">post-operative monitoring, tumor marker</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Hormone Replacement Therapy" target="Differentiated Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">TSH suppression via thyroid hormone replacement therapy is a critical component of treating and preventing recurrence in differentiated thyroid cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer suppression, hormonal therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Hormone Replacement Therapy" target="Thyrotoxicosis">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Taking too high a dose of levothyroxine in thyroid hormone replacement therapy can induce thyrotoxicosis.</data>
      <data key="d6">drug overdose, adverse effect</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Hormone Replacement Therapy" target="Levothyroxine">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Levothyroxine is the standard medication prescribed for thyroid hormone replacement therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">pharmacotherapy, hormone substitution</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Differentiated Thyroid Cancer" target="Radioactive Iodine Therapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radioactive iodine therapy selectively targets and destroys iodine-avid cells in differentiated thyroid cancers at higher risk of recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d6">targeted treatment, cancer therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radioactive Iodine Therapy" target="Whole-Body RAI Scan">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Whole-body radioiodine scans are performed before or after RAI therapy to detect residual thyroid tissue and assess treatment efficacy.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic imaging, therapeutic monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radioactive Iodine Therapy" target="Remnant Ablation">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radioactive iodine therapy is used at lower doses specifically to perform remnant ablation after surgery.</data>
      <data key="d6">therapeutic technique, post-surgical ablation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radioactive Iodine Therapy" target="Differentiated High-Grade Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Presentation of differentiated high-grade carcinoma is a primary clinical indication for recommending radioactive iodine therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment indication, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radioactive Iodine Therapy" target="Whole-Body RAI Imaging">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Patients who undergo radioactive iodine therapy may undergo whole-body RAI imaging to detect residual cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">surveillance imaging, post-therapy assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Remnant Ablation" target="Thyroidectomy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Remnant ablation is conducted shortly after thyroidectomy to clear any residual thyroid cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical follow-up, disease management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="U.S. FDA" target="Clinical Trials">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clinical trials investigate therapies that may eventually receive approval from the U.S. FDA.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. FDA reviews clinical trial data to approve safe and effective investigational drugs and treatments.</data>
      <data key="d6">regulatory approval, medical research&lt;SEP&gt;regulatory evaluation, treatment approval</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="U.S. FDA" target="Clinical Trial">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clinical trials test investigational therapies that may receive regulatory evaluation and approval from the U.S. FDA.</data>
      <data key="d6">regulatory evaluation, therapy approval</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Kinase Inhibitors" target="Targeted Therapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Kinase inhibitors are the specific class of targeted therapies commonly prescribed for thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">drug type, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="External Radiation" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
