    <edge source="Sarcoma Cancer Center" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">It is recommended that diagnostic biopsies be performed at specialized centers such as a sarcoma cancer center.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical procedure, treatment location</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Ossification" target="Bone Remodeling">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Ossification establishes initial bone formation during development, after which bone remodeling continuously replaces mature bone tissue.</data>
      <data key="d6">bone biology, physiological development</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bone Remodeling" target="Osteoblasts">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Osteoblasts participate directly in the bone remodeling cycle by synthesizing new bone from circulating calcium.</data>
      <data key="d6">cellular function, bone formation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bone Remodeling" target="Osteoclasts">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Osteoclasts facilitate bone remodeling by breaking down existing bone and releasing calcium into the bloodstream.</data>
      <data key="d6">bone resorption, cellular mechanism</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Metaphysis" target="Physis">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The metaphysis is positioned adjacent to the physis (growth plate) within the structural anatomy of bone.</data>
      <data key="d6">skeletal anatomy, structural relation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Metaphysis" target="Diaphysis">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The metaphysis connects the growth plate to the diaphysis along the length of the bone.</data>
      <data key="d6">skeletal anatomy, structural relation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Diaphysis" target="Physis">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The physis (growth plate) and diaphysis form distinct, adjacent anatomical zones within bone anatomy.</data>
      <data key="d6">bone structure, anatomy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Physis" target="Skeletal Maturity">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Skeletal maturity causes the physis cartilage growth plate to close, leaving a visible physeal scar.</data>
      <data key="d6">developmental maturation, skeletal anatomy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="T Category" target="Appendicular Skeleton">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Primary tumor staging rules are specifically outlined for tumors located within the appendicular skeleton.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical staging, tumor classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="T Category" target="Spine">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Tumors in the spine follow specialized T staging criteria based on vertebral involvement.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical staging, spinal oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="M Category" target="Lung">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The M category differentiates lung metastasis as M1a within distant metastatic bone cancer classification.</data>
      <data key="d6">distant metastasis, staging classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Appendicular Skeleton" target="Shoulder Girdle">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The shoulder girdle represents one of the major bone complexes constituting the appendicular skeleton.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical subdivision, skeletal system</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Appendicular Skeleton" target="Pelvic Girdle">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The pelvic girdle forms part of the appendicular skeleton that anchors the lower limbs.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical subdivision, skeletal system</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Spine" target="Spinal Canal">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The spinal canal runs through the vertebral column and marks severe stage T4 spinal tumor infiltration.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical region, tumor invasion</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Primary Liver Cancer" target="Hepatocellular Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hepatocellular carcinoma is identified as the most common form of primary liver cancer in adults.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, cancer subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Primary Liver Cancer" target="Intrahepatic Cholangiocarcinoma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma represents the second most common type of primary liver cancer in adults.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer subtype, bile duct cancer</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Primary Liver Cancer" target="Secondary Liver Cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Primary and secondary liver cancer are contrasted based on whether the malignancy originates in the liver or metastasizes there from another site.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease distinction, tumor origin</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Primary Liver Cancer" target="Metastatic Liver Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Primary liver cancer can progress to metastatic liver cancer by spreading to distant anatomical structures such as the lungs and bones.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease progression, metastasis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Primary Liver Cancer" target="Sarcoma Of The Liver">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Sarcoma of the liver is a rare form of primary liver cancer arising in blood vessel cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, rare malignancy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Primary Liver Cancer" target="Mixed-Type Liver Tumor">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mixed-type tumors, combining hepatocellular carcinoma and cholangiocarcinoma, are categorized as primary liver cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, mixed neoplasm</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Primary Liver Cancer" target="Hepatitis">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hepatitis is identified as a critical risk factor associated with the development of liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">risk factors, oncology etiology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hepatocellular Carcinoma" target="Fibrolamellar Hepatocellular Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Fibrolamellar hepatocellular carcinoma is a rare clinical subtype of hepatocellular carcinoma occurring in younger demographics.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype classification, oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hepatocellular Carcinoma" target="NCCN">
      <data key="d4">15.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN provides patient guidelines and treatment recommendations focusing on hepatocellular carcinoma.")&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN publishes patient guidelines and resources detailing information and treatment for hepatocellular carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical guidelines, cancer guidance&lt;SEP&gt;medical guidelines, patient education</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hepatocellular Carcinoma" target="Cirrhosis">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cirrhosis acts as a major predisposing risk factor for developing hepatocellular carcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Cirrhosis is a major predisposing chronic liver condition and risk factor for developing hepatocellular carcinoma.")</data>
      <data key="d6">disease risk factor, oncogenesis&lt;SEP&gt;risk factor, disease development</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hepatocellular Carcinoma" target="Liver Cancer Screening">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Liver cancer screening aims to identify hepatocellular carcinoma in its early stages before symptoms appear.&lt;SEP&gt;The primary objective of liver cancer screening is to detect hepatocellular carcinoma early when it is more treatable.</data>
      <data key="d6">detection, preventative medicine&lt;SEP&gt;early detection, clinical prevention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hepatocellular Carcinoma" target="Metastatic Liver Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hepatocellular carcinoma can metastasize through blood or lymphatic vessels to distant sites, developing into metastatic liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease metastasis, cancer progression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hepatocellular Carcinoma" target="Alpha-Fetoprotein Test">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Rising or elevated AFP levels in an alpha-fetoprotein test serve as a clinical biomarker indicating primary hepatocellular carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">tumor biomarker, cancer diagnosis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hepatocellular Carcinoma" target="Non-Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease, through its links with obesity and diabetes, serves as an established risk factor for liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease risk factor, oncogenesis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hepatocellular Carcinoma" target="Chronic Hepatitis B">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chronic hepatitis B is an established risk factor for the development of hepatocellular carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">risk factor, disease development</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hepatocellular Carcinoma" target="Liver Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hepatocellular carcinoma is the primary specific subtype of liver cancer discussed.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease classification, oncology</data>
