      <data key="d5">Testing for CBCL is the comprehensive diagnostic process required to identify, evaluate, and plan treatment for cutaneous B-cell lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnosis, medical evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cutaneous B-Cell Lymphoma" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biopsy is a critical diagnostic procedure needed to confirm cutaneous B-cell lymphoma and formulate treatment plans.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic procedure, disease management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cutaneous B-Cell Lymphoma" target="Blood And Tissue Tests">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Blood and tissue tests provide necessary results to determine the treatment plan for cutaneous B-cell lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">laboratory diagnostics, treatment planning</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Diagnostic CT" target="Radiology Department">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Diagnostic CT scans are performed inside imaging equipment housed in the radiology department.</data>
      <data key="d6">imaging venue, medical examination</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLL Specialist" target="Consultation With A CLL Specialist">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The consultation is conducted by the CLL specialist to address questions and deliver medical guidance.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical appointment, expert advice</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Primary Cutaneous Lymphomas" target="Cutaneous T-Cell Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cutaneous T-cell lymphoma is one of the two primary categories of primary cutaneous lymphomas.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype, medical categorization</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cutaneous T-Cell Lymphoma" target="NCCN">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Information and clinical guidance on types of CTCL are provided on NCCN platforms and patient guides.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical guidelines, patient resource</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cutaneous T-Cell Lymphoma" target="Mycosis Fungoides">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mycosis fungoides is classified as a specific type of cutaneous T-cell lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease subtype, medical classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cutaneous T-Cell Lymphoma" target="Sézary Syndrome">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Sézary syndrome is recognized as a specific type of cutaneous T-cell lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease subtype, medical classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cutaneous T-Cell Lymphoma" target="Primary Cutaneous T-Cell Lymphoproliferative Disorders">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PCLPDs are categorized among cutaneous T-cell lymphoma disorders.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease subtype, clinical classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Primary Cutaneous Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma, Leg Type" target="NCCN">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Treatment details for PC-DLBCL, leg type are published within NCCN Guidelines for Patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical guidelines, patient resource</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Testing For CBCL" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biopsy is a critical test included in the diagnostic testing protocol for CBCL.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic method, procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Doctor Visit" target="Doctor">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Doctors conduct visits to examine patients, answer questions, and discuss test findings.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical consultation, patient assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Doctor Visit" target="Medical History Assessment">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Taking a comprehensive medical history is a key element of doctor visits and clinical assessments.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient intake, clinical evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urothelial Cancer" target="Glandular Subtype">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Glandular subtype is a rare histological variant of urothelial cancer that may require aggressive management.</data>
      <data key="d6">histological subtype, disease variation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urothelial Cancer" target="Squamous Cell Subtype">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Squamous cell subtype is a recognized variant of urothelial cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">histological subtype, disease variation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urothelial Cancer" target="Micropapillary Subtype">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Micropapillary subtype is an uncommon subtype of urothelial cancer characterized by fast progression.</data>
      <data key="d6">histological subtype, disease variation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urothelial Cancer" target="Plasmacytoid Subtype">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Plasmacytoid subtype is an aggressive variant of urothelial bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">histological subtype, disease variation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urothelial Cancer" target="Sarcomatoid Subtype">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Sarcomatoid subtype is a recognized rare variant of urothelial cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">histological subtype, disease variation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urothelial Cancer" target="Nested Subtype">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Nested subtype is a morphological variant of urothelial carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">histological subtype, disease variation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urothelial Cancer" target="Lymphoepithelioma-Like Subtype">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lymphoepithelioma-like subtype is an uncommon variant form of urothelial cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">histological subtype, disease variation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urothelial Cancer" target="Microcystic And Tubular Subtype">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Microcystic and tubular subtype is a rare histological variant of urothelial cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">histological subtype, disease variation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hematuria" target="Urinalysis">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Urinalysis allows clinicians to detect microscopic levels of blood in the urine when it is not visibly apparent.</data>
      <data key="d6">detection, diagnostic test</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Non-Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer" target="Biomarker Test">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biomarker tests are used to conduct surveillance for disease recurrence in patients treated for non-muscle-invasive bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer surveillance, tumor biomarkers</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer" target="Stage 2">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 2 represents the initial category of muscle-invasive bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical classification, disease severity</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer" target="Stage 3 Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 3 is a form of muscle-invasive bladder cancer extending into surrounding perivesical fat and lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease severity, staging progression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer" target="Stage 4 Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 4 is the most advanced form of muscle-invasive bladder cancer, characterized by distant metastases.</data>
      <data key="d6">advanced disease, metastatic spread</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer" target="Radical Cystectomy">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radical cystectomy is the primary standard surgical intervention used to treat muscle-invasive bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical indication, oncology treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Metastatic Bladder Cancer" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy is used systemically to treat cancer throughout the body in patients with metastatic bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">systemic therapy, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Metastatic Bladder Cancer" target="Immunotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Immunotherapy is administered to treat cancer across the body in cases of metastatic bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">systemic treatment, cancer care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urine Cytology" target="Pathologist">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A pathologist examines urine cytology samples under a microscope to identify abnormal or cancerous cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">cytopathology, diagnostic analysis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Clinic Or Hospital" target="Tumor Removal">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Surgical tumor removal operations require hospital or clinic operating room facilities.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical setting, medical facility</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Blue Light Cystoscopy" target="White Light Cystoscopy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Blue light cystoscopy is evaluated against white light cystoscopy to assess superior tumor identification.</data>
      <data key="d6">comparative diagnostics, optical enhancement</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Narrow-Band Imaging" target="White Light Cystoscopy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Narrow-band imaging is compared with standard white light cystoscopy for enhanced tumor detection.</data>
      <data key="d6">comparative diagnostics, visual contrast</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lab" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biopsy tissue samples obtained during cystoscopy are delivered to the lab for pathological evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d6">specimen analysis, pathology testing</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Grading" target="Care Team">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The care team relies on cancer grading to gauge tumor aggressiveness and predict the course of the disease.&lt;SEP&gt;The care team uses tumor grade to understand how fast the cancer is growing and predict its clinical course.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease assessment, clinical decision&lt;SEP&gt;prognostic assessment, therapeutic strategy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Grading" target="Pathologist">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A pathologist examines tumor samples under a microscope in a laboratory to assign the cancer grade.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic pathology, cell analysis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 0" target="Stage 0a">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 0 includes Stage 0a as a non-invasive subgroup growing toward the interior of the bladder.</data>
      <data key="d6">subgroup classification, staging subdivision</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 0" target="Urothelial Layer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 0 bladder cancer is strictly confined to the inner urothelial layer of the bladder.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical localization, disease depth</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 2" target="Muscle Layer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 2 bladder cancer is defined by its direct invasion into the bladder muscle layer.</data>
      <data key="d6">tissue invasion, disease staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Low Grade" target="Pathologist">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A pathologist designates low grade cancer when cells closely resemble normal cells and display slow growth.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathological diagnosis, cellular grading</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="High Grade" target="Pathologist">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A pathologist identifies high grade cancer when tumor cells appear markedly different from normal cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathological diagnosis, cellular grading</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TURBT" target="Pathologist">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A pathologist evaluates biopsy samples collected during TURBT to determine cancer subtype and muscle invasion.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic evaluation, biopsy examination</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TURBT" target="Intravesical Therapy">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Intravesical therapy is commonly administered in combination with a TURBT procedure.&lt;SEP&gt;Intravesical therapy is frequently administered in combination with a TURBT procedure.</data>
      <data key="d6">combined therapy, oncology protocol&lt;SEP&gt;multimodal therapy, procedural combination</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TURBT" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A repeat TURBT procedure is often performed to collect a deeper biopsy sample for staging.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic procedure, tissue sampling</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TURBT" target="Intravesical BCG">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Intravesical BCG therapy is commonly initiated three to six weeks after a TURBT procedure to reduce recurrence risks.</data>
      <data key="d6">post-operative treatment, cancer management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TURBT" target="Intravesical Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Intravesical chemotherapy is frequently instilled within 24 hours after a TURBT surgery to eliminate residual cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">post-operative treatment, cancer management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TURBT" target="Trimodal Therapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">TURBT forms the initial surgical step of trimodal therapy, removing visible cancer before subsequent chemoradiation.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment component, bladder preservation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Intravesical Therapy" target="Intravesical BCG">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Intravesical BCG is one of the primary types of intravesical therapy used to treat non-muscle-invasive bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment classification, localized therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Intravesical Therapy" target="Intravesical Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Intravesical chemotherapy is one of the main forms of intravesical therapy administered directly into the bladder.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment classification, localized therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Indiana University" target="Continent Cutaneous Pouch">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The continent cutaneous pouch is named the Indiana pouch because it was developed by surgeons at Indiana University.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical innovation, institutional origin</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Indiana University" target="Indiana Pouch">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The Indiana pouch urinary diversion technique was developed by surgeons at Indiana University.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical innovation, surgical development</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cystectomy" target="Radical Cystectomy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radical cystectomy is the complete form of cystectomy that removes the whole bladder alongside nearby organs and nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical classification, procedural subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cystectomy" target="Partial Cystectomy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Partial cystectomy represents a limited form of cystectomy where only part of the bladder is excised.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical classification, procedural subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radical Cystectomy" target="Trimodal Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Trimodal therapy serves as an alternative bladder-preserving option to avoid radical cystectomy surgery.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical alternative, bladder preservation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Neobladder" target="Urinary Incontinence">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Urinary incontinence is a recognized side effect or functional challenge following the construction of a neobladder.</data>
      <data key="d6">postoperative complication, functional outcome</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Neobladder" target="Catheterization">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Catheterization through the urethra may be necessary to assist patients who cannot completely empty their neobladder.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical intervention, post-surgical management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Continent Cutaneous Pouch" target="Catheterization">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A continent cutaneous pouch requires routine catheterization through the stoma to drain accumulated urine.</data>
      <data key="d6">drainage method, postoperative care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urinary Incontinence" target="Bladder Removal Surgery">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Bladder removal surgery can directly cause side effects including urinary incontinence and difficulties with urinary diversion.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical complication, urinary function</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urinary Incontinence" target="Pelvic Floor Exercises">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pelvic floor exercises are specifically recommended to improve muscle control and reduce urinary incontinence after surgery.</data>
      <data key="d6">rehabilitation, symptom management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BCG Shortage" target="Intravesical BCG">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The BCG shortage limits patient access to intravesical BCG therapy, requiring dosage adjustments or alternative therapies.</data>
      <data key="d6">supply shortage, treatment availability</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BCG Shortage" target="Non–Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Patients diagnosed with non–muscle-invasive bladder cancer are heavily impacted by the shortage of BCG.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient impact, healthcare crisis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BCG Shortage" target="Bladder Removal Surgery">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Bladder removal surgery is considered an alternative intervention for high-risk patients when BCG is unavailable.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment alternative, surgical intervention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BCG Shortage" target="Clinical Trial">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clinical trials are alternative options for bladder cancer patients when BCG treatment is unavailable or ineffective.</data>
      <data key="d6">experimental care, therapeutic alternatives</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Intravesical BCG" target="Intravesical Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Intravesical chemotherapy serves as an alternative treatment when intravesical BCG is unavailable or ineffective.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment alternatives, oncology protocol</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Intravesical BCG" target="High-Grade Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Intravesical BCG is commonly prescribed as standard treatment for high-grade non-muscle-invasive bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease management, targeted treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Intravesical Chemotherapy" target="Systemic Therapy">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Systemic chemotherapy treats the entire body via the bloodstream, whereas intravesical chemotherapy targets only the bladder lining.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment scope, comparative oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Intravesical Chemotherapy" target="Systemic Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Systemic and intravesical chemotherapy represent two distinct categories of chemotherapy differing by whole-body versus localized bladder administration.</data>
      <data key="d6">therapeutic comparison, treatment classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder Removal Surgery" target="Sexual Dysfunction">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Surgical removal of the bladder can result in physical and psychological sexual dysfunction.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical complication, quality of life</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FDA" target="Clinical Trial">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clinical trials produce the safety and efficacy evidence required for FDA approval of new cancer drugs and treatments.</data>
      <data key="d6">regulatory evaluation, clinical research</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FDA" target="Phase 3 Clinical Trial">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Favorable results from Phase 3 clinical trials serve as the basis for treatment approval by the FDA.</data>
      <data key="d6">regulatory approval, trial benchmark</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FDA" target="Phase 4 Clinical Trial">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Phase 4 clinical trials monitor the long-term safety and efficacy of treatments after they have received FDA approval.</data>
      <data key="d6">post-market surveillance, long-term monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FDA" target="Clinical Trials">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The FDA evaluates data from clinical trials to approve new treatments and oversees post-approval Phase 4 safety monitoring.</data>
      <data key="d6">regulatory approval, safety monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Systemic Chemotherapy" target="Dose-Dense Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Dose-dense chemotherapy is an intensified scheduling method used in systemic chemotherapy delivery.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment scheduling, chemotherapy regimen</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Systemic Chemotherapy" target="Care Team">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The care team manages patient side effects and monitors health during systemic chemotherapy cycles.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient care, toxicity monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Phase 1 Clinical Trial" target="Clinical Trial">
