      <data key="d6">trial phase, research progression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Phase 2 Clinical Trial" target="Clinical Trial">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Phase 2 trials are an intermediate phase within clinical trial programs to assess cancer-specific efficacy.</data>
      <data key="d6">trial phase, research progression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Phase 3 Clinical Trial" target="Clinical Trial">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Phase 3 trials are large comparative studies within the clinical trial process.</data>
      <data key="d6">trial phase, comparative research</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Dose-Dense Chemotherapy" target="ddMVAC">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">ddMVAC is the most common regimen utilizing the dose-dense systemic chemotherapy approach for bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">drug regimen, dose intensification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Antibody-Drug Conjugate" target="Targeted Therapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Antibody-drug conjugates represent a specialized mechanism of targeted therapy for bladder cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">targeted treatment, drug class</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Human Papillomavirus Infection" target="HPV16">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">HPV16 is specifically identified as a high-risk strain responsible for human papillomavirus-related cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">viral strain, oncogenic risk</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Anal Dysplasia" target="High-Resolution Anoscopy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">High-Resolution Anoscopy provides magnification to detect and biopsy pre-cancerous anal dysplasia lesions.</data>
      <data key="d6">precancer detection, endoscopic examination</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Anal Dysplasia" target="Anal Intraepithelial Neoplasia">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Anal intraepithelial neoplasia (AIN) is another medical term used to describe pre-cancerous anal dysplasia.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical taxonomy, dysplasia classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Anal Dysplasia" target="Anal Precancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Anal dysplasia is one of the clinical designations for anal precancerous lesions.</data>
      <data key="d6">terminology, precancerous condition</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Superficially Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma" target="High-Resolution Anoscopy">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">High-Resolution Anoscopy is utilized during follow-up monitoring for patients treated for Superficially Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient follow-up, disease monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="High-Resolution Anoscopy" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">During a high-resolution anoscopy, tissue samples are taken via biopsy from suspicious areas for diagnostic testing.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic sampling, clinical procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="High-Resolution Anoscopy" target="Standard Anoscopy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Standard anoscopy examines the anal canal without magnification, whereas high-resolution anoscopy utilizes a colposcope for greater diagnostic effectiveness.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedural comparison, diagnostic sensitivity</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="High-Resolution Anoscopy" target="SISCCA">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Close follow-up using high-resolution anoscopy is recommended to monitor patients with SISCCA.</data>
      <data key="d6">follow-up, surveillance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Anal Squamous Cell Carcinoma" target="SISCCA">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Anal squamous cell carcinoma is an invasive cancer that is more advanced than SISCCA and requires different management.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease severity, differential diagnosis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Anal Squamous Cell Carcinoma" target="Precancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Precancer consists of abnormal lesions that exist before invasive anal squamous cell carcinoma develops.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease progression, malignancy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Anal Squamous Cell Carcinoma" target="Inguinal Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Anal cancer cells can spread through the lymphatic system to the inguinal lymph nodes in the groin.</data>
      <data key="d6">lymphatic spread, metastasis pathway</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lymph Node Palpation" target="Inguinal Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Physicians examine inguinal lymph nodes in the groin by surface palpation to check for enlargement.</data>
      <data key="d6">physical examination, diagnostic assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inguinal Lymph Nodes" target="Groin">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Inguinal lymph nodes are anatomically located in the groin.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical location, lymphatic system</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Anal Precancer" target="High-Grade Anal Intraepithelial Neoplasia">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">High-grade anal intraepithelial neoplasia (AIN) is a form and clinical name for anal precancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">terminology, precancerous lesions</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Anal Precancer" target="High-Grade Squamous Intraepithelial Lesions">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">High-grade squamous intraepithelial lesions (HSIL) are a categorization of anal precancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">terminology, precancerous lesions</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage II Anal Cancer" target="Stage IIA Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage II anal cancer is divided into substages, including stage IIA depending on tumor size.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease substages, clinical classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage II Anal Cancer" target="Stage IIB Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage II anal cancer is divided into substages, including stage IIB for tumors larger than 5 cm.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease substages, clinical classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage III Anal Cancer" target="Stage IIIA Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage IIIA is a regional nodal substage of stage III anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease substages, clinical staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage III Anal Cancer" target="Stage IIIB Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage IIIB is an organ-invasive substage of stage III anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease substages, clinical staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage III Anal Cancer" target="Stage IIIC Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage IIIC is an advanced substage of stage III anal cancer involving nodal spread and large or invasive tumors.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease substages, clinical staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Infertility" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy used in anal cancer treatment damages ovaries and pelvic reproductive organs, directly causing infertility.</data>
      <data key="d6">adverse treatment effect, reproductive toxicity</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Infertility" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy administered during anal cancer treatment can damage sperm and eggs, causing infertility.</data>
      <data key="d6">adverse treatment effect, reproductive toxicity</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Metastatic Anal Cancer" target="Immunotherapy">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Immunotherapy is prescribed as a systemic treatment option for patients with metastatic anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is recommended for patients whose metastatic anal cancer progresses during or after chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">second-line treatment, disease progression&lt;SEP&gt;systemic therapy, advanced cancer management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Metastatic Anal Cancer" target="Carboplatin and Paclitaxel">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Carboplatin and paclitaxel is a standard first-line chemotherapy regimen recommended for metastatic anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">chemotherapy regimen, metastatic treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Metastatic Anal Cancer" target="5-FU and Cisplatin">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">5-FU and cisplatin is an established platinum-based first-line chemotherapy regimen for metastatic anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">chemotherapy regimen, metastatic treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Metastatic Anal Cancer" target="FOLFCIS">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">FOLFCIS is one of the systemic chemotherapy options recommended for metastatic anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">chemotherapy regimen, metastatic treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Metastatic Anal Cancer" target="Modified DCF">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Modified DCF is an intensive chemotherapy regimen used for metastatic anal cancer after which chemoradiation is generally not considered.</data>
      <data key="d6">chemotherapy regimen, metastatic treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Metastatic Anal Cancer" target="Clinical Trial">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Patients diagnosed with metastatic anal cancer are encouraged to consider enrollment in clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical research, patient options</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Metastatic Anal Cancer" target="Thom">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Thom survived anal cancer and shares guidance and self-advocacy strategies for patients facing the disease.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient advocacy, survivorship</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Metastatic Anal Cancer" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy regimens, including platinum-based agents and modified DCF, are standard treatment options for metastatic anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease management, oncologic treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Metastatic Anal Cancer" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy, including SBRT and palliative radiation, is utilized to manage tumors and alleviate symptoms in metastatic anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">symptom management, radiation oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Metastatic Anal Cancer" target="Clinical Trials">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clinical trials offer eligible anal cancer patients access to investigational drugs and novel treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d6">investigational therapy, clinical research</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Anal Canal Cancer" target="Perianal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Perianal cancer and anal canal cancer share common chemoradiation regimens and follow-up surveillance pathways.</data>
      <data key="d6">protocol similarity, oncology care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Digital Rectal Examination" target="Complete Remission">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Digital rectal exams are scheduled every 3 to 6 months for 5 years to monitor patients who are in complete remission.</data>
      <data key="d6">surveillance monitoring, remission maintenance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Anoscopy" target="Complete Remission">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Anoscopy is recommended every 6 to 12 months for 3 years to maintain surveillance for patients in complete remission.</data>
      <data key="d6">follow-up surveillance, cancer monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Aquaphor" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Aquaphor ointment is used to soothe and heal skin side effects induced by radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">symptom management, topical treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Silvadene" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Silvadene ointment is applied to treat radiation-induced skin burns and sores.</data>
      <data key="d6">skin healing, supportive care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mitomycin" target="5-FU">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">5-FU is commonly combined with mitomycin as a standard chemotherapy component of chemoradiation for anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Mitomycin is combined with continuous-infusion 5-FU as a primary chemotherapy regimen for anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">chemotherapy combination, medical protocol&lt;SEP&gt;drug combination, chemotherapy regimen</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="5-FU" target="PICC Line">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A PICC line serves as an alternative deep intravenous catheter to administer continuous 5-FU infusions.</data>
      <data key="d6">vascular access, drug delivery</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="5-FU" target="5-FU Infusion">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">5-FU is administered during dedicated 4- to 5-day continuous infusion sessions.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment schedule, clinical procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Feedback Survey" target="NCCN">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN hosts the patient feedback survey via its website to gather insights on educational materials.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient feedback, resource management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Complete Remission" target="Follow-Up Examination">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The follow-up examination evaluates post-treatment response and determines if the patient has achieved complete remission.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic classification, post-treatment evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Complete Remission" target="Inguinal Lymph Node Exam">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Inguinal lymph node exams are performed every 3 to 6 months for 5 years as part of surveillance during complete remission.</data>
      <data key="d6">lymph node assessment, post-treatment follow-up</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Persistent Disease" target="Follow-Up Examination">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The follow-up examination identifies whether residual cancer remains without growth, categorizing it as persistent disease.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical monitoring, response evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Progressive Disease" target="Follow-Up Examination">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The follow-up examination checks if the tumor has enlarged or spread, designating it as progressive disease.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease progression, diagnostic outcome</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Progressive Disease" target="Abdominoperineal Resection (APR)">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Abdominoperineal resection is recommended as a surgical intervention when anal cancer demonstrates local progression.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical intervention, disease management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Progressive Disease" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A biopsy is conducted to histologically confirm suspected progressive disease detected 8 to 12 weeks after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic confirmation, disease progression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Progressive Disease" target="Immunotherapy">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Immunotherapy with immune checkpoint inhibitors may be attempted prior to surgery in patients with locally progressive disease.</data>
      <data key="d6">alternative therapy, systemic treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Recurrent Anal Cancer" target="Groin Dissection">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Groin dissection is performed to remove lymph nodes when recurrent cancer is identified in the groin area.</data>
      <data key="d6">lymph node clearance, recurrence management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Recurrent Anal Cancer" target="Carboplatin and Paclitaxel">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Carboplatin and paclitaxel is a preferred chemotherapy regimen for cancer returning to lymph nodes in patients with prior radiation.</data>
      <data key="d6">recurrence therapy, systemic treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Recurrent Anal Cancer" target="5-FU and Cisplatin">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">5-FU and cisplatin is an option for treating recurrent anal cancer in groin lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">recurrence therapy, systemic treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Recurrent Anal Cancer" target="Mitomycin and Capecitabine">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mitomycin and capecitabine is prescribed with radiation for inguinal lymph node recurrence in patients without prior radiation.</data>
      <data key="d6">combination therapy, recurrence management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Recurrent Anal Cancer" target="5-FU and Mitomycin">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">5-FU and mitomycin is an alternative chemotherapy option paired with radiation for nodal recurrence without prior radiation.</data>
      <data key="d6">combination therapy, recurrence management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Muscle Flap Reconstruction" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Tissue damage caused by prior radiation therapy necessitates muscle flap reconstruction to ensure proper healing after surgery.</data>
      <data key="d6">tissue damage, surgical reconstruction</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Muscle Flap Reconstruction" target="Doctor">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Patients planning an APR are advised to consult their doctor regarding muscle flap reconstruction.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical consultation, surgical planning</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Survivorship" target="Survivorship Care Plan">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A survivorship care plan provides the roadmap for long-term health, monitoring, and side-effect management during survivorship.</data>
      <data key="d6">care coordination, survivorship management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Anal Cancer Foundation" target="Peer-To-Peer Support Program">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The Anal Cancer Foundation manages and facilitates the Peer-To-Peer Support Program for patients and caregivers.</data>
      <data key="d6">program management, patient support</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pelvic Floor Physical Therapy" target="Pelvic Floor">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pelvic floor physical therapy targets and rehabilitates the pelvic floor muscles to restore continence and sexual function.</data>
      <data key="d6">muscle rehabilitation, pelvic care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pelvic Floor Physical Therapy" target="Kegel Exercises">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Kegel exercises serve as an at-home component of pelvic floor physical therapy programs.</data>
      <data key="d6">exercise therapy, rehabilitation technique</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Vaginal Dilator Therapy" target="Vaginal Stenosis">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Vaginal dilator therapy is specifically utilized to stretch tissue and treat or prevent vaginal stenosis.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical therapy, symptom mitigation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Ostomy Care" target="Ostomy Nurse">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Ostomy nurses provide specialized clinical guidance and consultations for patients managing ostomy care.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical care, patient management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Vaginal Stenosis" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pelvic radiation therapy causes tissue narrowing and shortening known as vaginal stenosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiation therapy directed to the pelvis can lead to complications such as vaginal stenosis.</data>
      <data key="d6">adverse effect, clinical complication&lt;SEP&gt;treatment complication, adverse effect</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Survivorship" target="NCCN">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN publishes specialized guidelines addressing survivorship care for healthy living and managing cancer-related late and long-term effects.</data>
      <data key="d6">survivorship guidance, healthcare resources</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Survivorship" target="Integrative Oncology">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Integrative oncology approaches help survivors manage chronic treatment side effects, comfort, and general well-being.</data>
      <data key="d6">symptom management, patient well-being</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Survivorship" target="Smoking Cessation">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Doctors emphasize smoking cessation counseling as a critical lifestyle measure to support cancer survivorship and general health.</data>
      <data key="d6">lifestyle modification, health maintenance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Integrative Oncology" target="Complementary And Alternative Therapies">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Integrative oncology formally incorporates complementary and alternative therapies alongside conventional cancer treatment protocols.</data>
      <data key="d6">holistic care, therapy integration</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Complementary And Alternative Therapies" target="Acupuncture">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Acupuncture is recognized as an integrative complementary therapy that helps improve comfort and alleviate treatment side effects.</data>
      <data key="d6">complementary practice, symptom relief</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Complementary And Alternative Therapies" target="Medical Marijuana">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Medical marijuana is a complementary therapeutic product utilized to support patient comfort during cancer therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">integrative product, symptom management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Complementary And Alternative Therapies" target="Yoga">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Yoga is an active complementary practice offered within integrative oncology regimens.</data>
      <data key="d6">mind-body practice, wellness</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Complementary And Alternative Therapies" target="Meditation">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Meditation is a complementary practice recommended to alleviate psychological distress and fatigue.</data>
      <data key="d6">mental well-being, mindfulness</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Shared Decision-Making" target="Second Opinion">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Seeking a second opinion is a recommended component of informed shared decision-making for patients considering cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment confirmation, patient autonomy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Shared Decision-Making" target="Clinical Trials">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Patients and physicians discuss eligibility, potential benefits, and risks of clinical trials during shared decision-making conversations.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment options, clinical research</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Shared Decision-Making" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Patient perspectives on chemotherapy risks and adverse side effects, like nausea and vomiting, are central to shared decision-making discussions.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient preference, treatment evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Shared Decision-Making" target="Surgery">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Patient comfort, personal beliefs, and risk assessment regarding surgery play an essential role in shared treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedural choice, treatment discussion</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Shared Decision-Making" target="Health Insurance Companies">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Financial considerations and insurance coverage parameters are critical factors weighed by patients during shared treatment decision-making.</data>
      <data key="d6">financial considerations, healthcare choices</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Shared Decision-Making" target="Fertility Specialists">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Consultations with fertility specialists are recommended during treatment decision-making to address how proposed therapies may affect fertility or pregnancy.</data>
      <data key="d6">fertility preservation, clinical consultation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Second Opinion" target="Health Insurance Companies">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Health insurance company rules and out-of-pocket costs directly influence a patient's ability to consult other doctors for second opinions.</data>
      <data key="d6">coverage policy, financial access</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bone Density Monitoring" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation to the pelvis can weaken pelvic bones, necessitating medical monitoring of bone density to prevent fractures.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment side effect, medical surveillance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Support Community" target="Survivor Support Group">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cancer Support Community connects patients to survivor support groups and emotional well-being resources.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient support, community network</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pelvic Radiation" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pelvic radiation is a specialized anatomical form of radiation therapy that impacts subsequent radiation planning.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment history, anatomical radiation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Follow-Up Care" target="Survivor Support Group">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Survivor support groups complement follow-up care by addressing emotional recovery and life-long side effects after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">survivorship care, post-treatment support</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Anal Canal" target="Anus">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The anal canal leads directly to the anal opening through which stool is evacuated.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical connection, digestive tract</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Anal Sphincter" target="Anus">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The anal sphincter encircles the anus to open and close the anal opening.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical structure, functional control</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Anus" target="Perianal Region">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The perianal region comprises the skin directly surrounding the anus.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical proximity, dermatology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN" target="Immunotherapy">
      <data key="d4">22.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN provides patient guidelines and resources on managing the side effects of immune checkpoint inhibitors used in immunotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN publishes patient guidelines addressing the management of immunotherapy and immune checkpoint inhibitor side effects.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN publishes patient guidelines specifically detailing the side effects and management of immune checkpoint inhibitors.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical guidance, patient care&lt;SEP&gt;clinical guidance, treatment monitoring&lt;SEP&gt;patient resources, side effect management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN" target="Care Team">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The patient's care team utilizes expert clinical standards such as the NCCN Guidelines to plan and administer oncology care.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical treatment, clinical guidelines</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN provides clinical guidelines and patient educational materials for managing side effects associated with chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN provides clinical patient guidelines to help manage adverse side effects caused by chemotherapy treatments, such as nausea and vomiting.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient resources, side effect management&lt;SEP&gt;patient resources, symptom management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN" target="Clinical Trial">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN experts recommend that all patients diagnosed with primary CNS lymphoma consider enrolling in a clinical trial.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN experts recommend that patients with primary CNS lymphoma consider enrolling in clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical guidance, treatment recommendations&lt;SEP&gt;treatment recommendation, clinical research</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surgery" target="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">24.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Surgery and radiation therapy are alternative or complementary local treatment modalities to systemic therapy in squamous cell skin cancer management.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgery and radiation therapy are frequently combined into a multimodal treatment regimen for gliomas.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgery and radiation therapy are often combined to deliver comprehensive treatment for gliomas.</data>
      <data key="d6">combined therapy, multimodal treatment&lt;SEP&gt;combined therapy, treatment multimodal&lt;SEP&gt;treatment modalities, disease management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surgery" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">24.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy is systematically scheduled in relation to surgery, either prior to it as neoadjuvant therapy or following it as adjuvant therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgery and chemotherapy are major complementary treatment modalities coordinated by the care team.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgery is used alongside chemotherapy and facilitates delivery via implanted ports or intraperitoneal catheters.</data>
      <data key="d6">multimodal treatment, oncology protocol&lt;SEP&gt;procedural support, multimodal treatment&lt;SEP&gt;treatment sequence, multimodal therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surgery" target="Imaging Tests">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Imaging tests offer preliminary estimates of cancer spread to help determine whether and how surgery should proceed.</data>
      <data key="d6">preoperative planning, diagnostic imaging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surgery" target="Pathologist">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pathologists evaluate organs and resected tissues removed during surgical procedures.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathology, surgical evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radiation Therapy" target="Systemic Therapy">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy may be administered alongside systemic therapy for patients with unresectable colon cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic therapy is frequently administered in combination with radiation therapy when tumors cannot be surgically excised.</data>
      <data key="d6">combination therapy, cancer treatment&lt;SEP&gt;multimodal therapy, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radiation Therapy" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">25.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy is sometimes combined with radiation therapy to enhance treatment efficacy against cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiation therapy and chemotherapy are frequently combined with each other in glioma management.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiation therapy is administered five days per week concurrently with systemic chemotherapy regimens.</data>
      <data key="d6">combination therapy, chemoradiation&lt;SEP&gt;combined therapy, multimodal treatment&lt;SEP&gt;multimodal therapy, concurrent treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radiation Therapy" target="Palliative Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Palliative radiation is a sub-application of radiation therapy focused on symptom reduction and tumor management.</data>
      <data key="d6">symptom relief, palliative care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Systemic Therapy" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">141.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy is a major subcategory and example of systemic therapy used to treat cancer throughout the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemotherapy is a major subcategory and form of systemic therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemotherapy is a major subcategory of systemic therapy that circulates via the bloodstream.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemotherapy is a primary category of systemic therapy administered through the bloodstream.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemotherapy is a primary form of systemic therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemotherapy is a primary modality within systemic drug therapy used to reduce cancer burden before or after surgery.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemotherapy is an established form of systemic therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemotherapy is an example of systemic therapy applied in cases of metastatic or recurrent squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemotherapy is an intravenous type of systemic therapy used to treat bladder cancer throughout the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemotherapy is classified as a form of systemic therapy delivering cytotoxic drugs through the bloodstream.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemotherapy is classified as a major form of systemic therapy that travels via the bloodstream to reach cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemotherapy is classified as a major subtype of systemic cancer therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemotherapy is defined as a form of systemic therapy that travels via the bloodstream to reach cells body-wide.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemotherapy is defined as a specific type of systemic therapy used to destroy cancer cells throughout the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemotherapy is one of the primary subcategories of systemic therapy used to treat cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemotherapy, particularly platinum-based regimens, represents a core systemic therapy modality.</data>
      <data key="d6">classification, medical oncology&lt;SEP&gt;medical classification, oncology treatment&lt;SEP&gt;medical oncology, drug therapy&lt;SEP&gt;medical taxonomy, systemic treatment&lt;SEP&gt;therapeutic categorization, drug treatment&lt;SEP&gt;treatment categorization, systemic therapy&lt;SEP&gt;treatment classification, drug administration&lt;SEP&gt;treatment classification, drug delivery&lt;SEP&gt;treatment classification, drug regimen&lt;SEP&gt;treatment classification, drug therapy&lt;SEP&gt;treatment classification, oncology&lt;SEP&gt;treatment classification, pharmacotherapy&lt;SEP&gt;treatment classification, systemic medicine&lt;SEP&gt;treatment subclass, oncologic pharmacology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Systemic Therapy" target="Targeted Therapy">
      <data key="d4">118.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Targeted biological therapies such as bevacizumab are incorporated into systemic combination regimens.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy is a specific form of systemic therapy used alongside or as an alternative to other treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy is a subtype of systemic therapy that targets specific cancer cell mechanisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy is a systemic drug therapy modality used to treat cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy is a systemic treatment option for patients who are not eligible for immunotherapy or clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy is a type of systemic treatment option considered for advanced squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy is an advanced category of systemic therapy effective against cancers displaying particular biomarkers.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy is an advanced form of systemic cancer therapy that reaches cells throughout the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy is an advanced systemic therapy class guided by specific biomarkers.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy is an established category of systemic therapy aimed at specific cellular receptors.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy is categorized as a type of systemic therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy is classified as a form of systemic therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy is classified as a specialized form of systemic therapy focusing on specific cancer cell mechanisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy is explicitly categorized as a form of systemic therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical taxonomy, systemic treatment&lt;SEP&gt;medical taxonomy, targeted treatment&lt;SEP&gt;targeted treatment, medical oncology&lt;SEP&gt;targeted treatment, systemic oncology&lt;SEP&gt;therapy classification, precision medicine&lt;SEP&gt;treatment categorization, oncology therapy&lt;SEP&gt;treatment categorization, targeted treatment&lt;SEP&gt;treatment classification, biomarker therapy&lt;SEP&gt;treatment classification, drug therapy&lt;SEP&gt;treatment classification, targeted mechanism&lt;SEP&gt;treatment classification, targeted therapy&lt;SEP&gt;treatment option, alternative therapy&lt;SEP&gt;treatment subclass, precision oncology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Systemic Therapy" target="Immunotherapy">
      <data key="d4">143.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Immunotherapy drugs like pembrolizumab serve as systemic therapy options for specific biomarker-positive cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is a category of systemic therapy employed in cancer management.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is a category of systemic therapy that provides treatment options for recurrent or metastatic disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is a category of systemic therapy that stimulates immune system action against cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is a category of systemic therapy utilized in biomarker-specific disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is a modality of systemic therapy used when squamous cell skin cancer has metastasized.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is categorized as a systemic therapy approach used prior to surgery or in advanced cancer stages.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is classified as a modality of systemic therapy used against cancer throughout the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is classified under systemic therapy options for treating breast cancer throughout the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is defined as a form of systemic therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is grouped under systemic therapy alongside chemotherapy and targeted therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is one of the primary forms of systemic therapy used to treat bladder cancer through intravenous administration.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is recognized as a form of systemic cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is recognized as a form of systemic therapy enhancing whole-body immune defenses.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is recognized as a type of systemic therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy using immune checkpoint inhibitors is a preferred form of systemic therapy for eligible CSCC patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy, including immune checkpoint inhibitors, is an established subcategory of systemic therapy for CSCC.</data>
