    </edge>
    <edge source="Biopsy" target="Pathologist">
      <data key="d4">88.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A pathologist analyzes biopsy tissue samples taken from suspicious bladder areas to determine the presence of disease.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist analyzes the tissue samples obtained during a biopsy to identify cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist evaluates the tissue and fluid samples collected during a biopsy under a microscope to detect cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist examines tumor samples collected during a biopsy to diagnose cancer type and characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;Biopsy specimens retrieved during procedures are examined by a pathologist to determine cancer staging and removal completeness.&lt;SEP&gt;Biopsy tissue samples are submitted to a pathologist for microscopic confirmation of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Pathologists analyze cells and tissue samples extracted during needle biopsies.&lt;SEP&gt;Pathologists analyze tissue and fluid specimens obtained via biopsy to detect cancer and establish tumor histology.&lt;SEP&gt;Tissue and fluid specimens obtained from a biopsy are examined by a pathologist to diagnose cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Tissue and fluid specimens removed during biopsies are evaluated by pathologists to verify cancer presence and cell classification.</data>
